STUDY_CONTENT.modules.push({"id":"ahad-cardio-ii-20261022","course":"AHAD","system":"cardio","title":"Cardiology II guest lecture · October 22 2026 · Slides and notes","file":"Cardiology II slides and condensed notes · Julie Lorenzin","source":"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf","sourceLabel":"Download slides and notes PDF","sourceKind":"Guest lecture with aligned notes","practiceRoute":"ahad-skills","practiceLabel":"ECG and examination practice","mappingNote":"AHAD I Module 6. All 178 original slides are retained; recorded teaching, slide-reference summaries and editorial clarifications are separately labelled. Notes are condensed from the supplied October 22 recording.","sections":[{"block":0,"title":"Lecture guide and downloads","text":"Cardiology II October 22 2026 Julie Lorenzin AHAD Module 6 guest lecture slides condensed notes. Holter ECG palpitations atrial fibrillation syncope coronary disease.","html":"<p>Julie Lorenzin MScN NP-PHC · AHAD I Module 6 · October 22, 2026.</p><p>All 178 original slides with condensed explanations, examples and questions directly underneath. Search by topic or slide number, or use the PDF bookmarks.</p><div class=\"actions\"><a class=\"button primary\" href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open slides and notes PDF</a><a class=\"button\" href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf\" download>Download PDF</a><a class=\"button\" href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Notes.md\" download>Download editable notes</a><a class=\"button\" href=\"#ahad-skills\">ECG and examination practice</a></div><p>The recording starts during slide 6. Earlier slides and material skipped in the live session are labelled separately. Timestamps are approximate; clarifications are editorial additions, distinct from the lecture. Sources for clarifications are at the end.</p>"},{"block":1,"title":"Slide 1 · Cardiology II","text":"Slide 1 Cardiology II Cardiology II\nJulie Lorenzin MScN, NP-PHC\nWestern University\nPrimary Health Care Nurse Practitioner Program\nModule 6\nAdvanced Health Assessment and Diagnosis I \nFall 2026 Cardiology II with Julie Lorenzin, MScN, NP-PHC. Module 6 of Advanced Health Assessment and Diagnosis I, Fall 2026. The supplied recording begins partway through the Holter indications discussion on slide 6. No opening remarks are available for this slide.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">No matching recorded segment · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-001.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 1 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-001.webp\" alt=\"Slide 1: Cardiology II\"></a><h3>Not captured in recording</h3><p>Cardiology II with Julie Lorenzin, MScN, NP-PHC. Module 6 of Advanced Health Assessment and Diagnosis I, Fall 2026.</p><p>The supplied recording begins partway through the Holter indications discussion on slide 6. No opening remarks are available for this slide.</p>"},{"block":2,"title":"Slide 2 · Seminar Agenda","text":"Slide 2 Seminar Agenda Seminar Agenda\n• Indications for a ECG monitoring\n• 12 Lead ECG\n• Holter and event monitors\n• Sinus, atrial and ventricular arrhythmias\n• Sinus rhythms – normal, brady, tachy\n• Palpitations\n• PVCs/PACs – benign causes\n• Atrial Rhythms\n• Atrial fibrillation\n• Atrial flutter\n• Ventricular tachycardia\n• Syncope\n• Coronary artery disease\n• Stable angina\n• Acute coronary syndromes \n• STEMI, NSTEMI, UA (unstable angina) Use the agenda to organize revision into ECG monitoring and interpretation; sinus rhythms and ectopy; atrial arrhythmias; conduction blocks and syncope; ventricular arrhythmias; and coronary disease with diagnostic testing. No corresponding spoken introduction is present in the supplied audio.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">No matching recorded segment · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-002.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 2 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-002.webp\" alt=\"Slide 2: Seminar Agenda\"></a><h3>Not captured in recording</h3><p>Use the agenda to organize revision into ECG monitoring and interpretation; sinus rhythms and ectopy; atrial arrhythmias; conduction blocks and syncope; ventricular arrhythmias; and coronary disease with diagnostic testing.</p><p>No corresponding spoken introduction is present in the supplied audio.</p>"},{"block":3,"title":"Slide 3 · Indications for the 12 Lead ECG and Cardiac ","text":"Slide 3 Indications for the 12 Lead ECG and Cardiac  Indications for the 12 Lead ECG and Cardiac \nRhythm Monitoring\n• CNO does not outline an exhaustive list of treatments or diagnostic \nand/or laboratory tests NPs can provide\n• Access to a broad range of diagnostic tests or procedures is a crucial \ncomponent of NP practice to establish a diagnosis and provide access \nto quality client care, such as ordering of:\n• laboratory tests\n• imaging (X-ray, CT, MRI, Ultrasound)\n• miscellaneous services (pulmonary function test, cardiac stress test, ECGs \nand EEGs)\n• 12 lead ECG\n• provides a brief sample of cardiac electrical activity over 10 seconds Slide reference: A standard 12-lead ECG samples about 10 seconds of electrical activity. It can identify an abnormality present during that brief window, but intermittent symptoms may require longer monitoring. The ordering and scope discussion on this slide was not captured in the recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">No matching recorded segment · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-003.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 3 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-003.webp\" alt=\"Slide 3: Indications for the 12 Lead ECG and Cardiac \"></a><h3>Not captured in recording</h3><p>Slide reference: A standard 12-lead ECG samples about 10 seconds of electrical activity. It can identify an abnormality present during that brief window, but intermittent symptoms may require longer monitoring.</p><p>The ordering and scope discussion on this slide was not captured in the recording.</p>"},{"block":4,"title":"Slide 4 · Indications for a 12-Lead ECG","text":"Slide 4 Indications for a 12-Lead ECG Indications for a 12-Lead ECG\n• Acute Cardiac Ischemia / Infarction\n• Typical chest pain/pressure or atypical equivalents (jaw/arm radiation, \nshortness of breath, diaphoresis)\n• Arrhythmias & Conduction Disturbances\n• Palpitations, unexplained syncope or dizziness, persistent tachycardia, or \nbradycardia\n• Systemic & Metabolic Monitoring\n• Electrolyte imbalances (e.g., potassium/calcium), drug toxicities, and \npacemaker/ICD evaluation\n• Pre-Operative & Risk Assessment\n• Pre-procedural screening for major surgeries and risk evaluation in patients \nwith cardiac risk factors Slide reference: ECG indications include suspected ischemia, palpitations, syncope or dizziness, persistent abnormal heart rate, metabolic or medication effects, and selected risk assessments. Choose the test to answer the clinical question. A brief normal ECG cannot establish the cause of symptoms occurring at another time.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">No matching recorded segment · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-004.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 4 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-004.webp\" alt=\"Slide 4: Indications for a 12-Lead ECG\"></a><h3>Not captured in recording</h3><p>Slide reference: ECG indications include suspected ischemia, palpitations, syncope or dizziness, persistent abnormal heart rate, metabolic or medication effects, and selected risk assessments.</p><p>Choose the test to answer the clinical question. A brief normal ECG cannot establish the cause of symptoms occurring at another time.</p>"},{"block":5,"title":"Slide 5 · Holter Monitoring","text":"Slide 5 Holter Monitoring Holter Monitoring\n• ambulatory ECG monitoring provides a view of \nECG data over an extended period of time\n• permits evaluation of dynamic and transient \ncardiac electrical phenomena\n• most common ambulatory ECG application is in \nthe diagnosis and assessment of cardiac \narrhythmias or conduction abnormalities \n(symptomatic or asymptomatic) or the \npresence of potential arrhythmias (such as in \npatients with syncope or presyncope)\n• short as 24-48 hours, 7-14 days or for as long as \nmonths to years (implantable loop recorders) Slide reference: Ambulatory monitoring samples cardiac activity over a longer period to detect transient arrhythmias or conduction abnormalities. Options range from short Holters to longer external or implanted monitoring. No separate explanation of this introductory slide is available in the recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">No matching recorded segment · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-005.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 5 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-005.webp\" alt=\"Slide 5: Holter Monitoring\"></a><h3>Not captured in recording</h3><p>Slide reference: Ambulatory monitoring samples cardiac activity over a longer period to detect transient arrhythmias or conduction abnormalities. Options range from short Holters to longer external or implanted monitoring.</p><p>No separate explanation of this introductory slide is available in the recording.</p>"},{"block":6,"title":"Slide 6 · Holter Monitoring Indications ","text":"Slide 6 Holter Monitoring Indications  Holter Monitoring Indications \n• When to choose Holter monitoring\n• refer ambulatory ECG monitoring test for patients with daily or near daily \nsymptoms, and for patients in whom a comprehensive/continuous \nassessment of all cardiac activity is required\n• Clinical scenarios in which a Holter monitor is a good choice:\n• syncope, near syncope, or dizziness occurring on a daily or near daily basis\n• palpitations occurring on a daily or near daily basis. \n• atrial fibrillation (AF) for assessment of ventricular rate control\n• frequent ectopy PACs or PVCs, requiring quantification of the ectopy burden\n• recent acute coronary syndrome, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, \narrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, long QT syndrome, dilated \nor restrictive cardiomyopathy, congenital heart disease, or Brugada \nsyndrome, screening for ventricular ectopy or non-sustained ventricular \narrhythmias might alter prognosis and therapy (likely a Cardiology ordered \nand managed issue) The recording opens during the more specialized indications: prior ischemic events, cardiomyopathy, prolonged-QT concerns, congenital disease, and known arrhythmias requiring further characterization. These situations are often investigated and managed by cardiology. Primary care still needs to understand why monitoring was ordered, what the findings mean, and which follow-up responsibilities remain with the NP. The slide's general selection rule is daily or near-daily symptoms when continuous assessment is needed. Less frequent symptoms may need a longer monitoring strategy.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">00:00-00:55 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-006.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 6 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-006.webp\" alt=\"Slide 6: Holter Monitoring Indications \"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The recording opens during the more specialized indications: prior ischemic events, cardiomyopathy, prolonged-QT concerns, congenital disease, and known arrhythmias requiring further characterization.</p><p>These situations are often investigated and managed by cardiology. Primary care still needs to understand why monitoring was ordered, what the findings mean, and which follow-up responsibilities remain with the NP.</p><p>The slide&#x27;s general selection rule is daily or near-daily symptoms when continuous assessment is needed. Less frequent symptoms may need a longer monitoring strategy.</p>"},{"block":7,"title":"Slide 7 · Holter Monitoring","text":"Slide 7 Holter Monitoring Holter Monitoring\n• Typical Holter monitor report includes the following information:\n• Total heart beats\n• Average heart rate\n• Maximum and minimum heart rates\n• Number of premature beats (supraventricular and ventricular)\n• Episodes of tachyarrhythmia and the etiology of the arrhythmias (i.e. \nsupraventricular or ventricular)\n• Longest R-R interval and any pauses greater than three seconds and etiology \nof the pauses (i.e. sinus pauses versus atrioventricular block)\n• ST segment changes\n• Patient-reported symptoms and any associated ECG findings (called symptom \nrhythm correlation)\n• Representative samples of ECG tracings (eg, hourly samples) A Holter report provides total beats, average/minimum/maximum rate, PAC and PVC counts, arrhythmia type, frequency and duration, pauses, ST changes, and sample tracings. Some systems can also flag important events remotely; this depends on the device and service. The symptom diary is especially useful. Ask the patient to record the exact date, time, activity and symptoms. The reader compares the rhythm before, during and after that event: symptom-rhythm correlation. Symptoms without a corresponding rhythm abnormality can support broadening the differential to non-arrhythmic causes; interpret this within the limitations of the recording. !The lecturer used a three-second pause threshold as an absolute symptom cutoff. Do not memorize that as a rule: clinical significance depends on symptom correlation and context, rather than one universal pause duration. [1]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">00:55-04:10 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-007.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 7 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-007.webp\" alt=\"Slide 7: Holter Monitoring\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>A Holter report provides total beats, average/minimum/maximum rate, PAC and PVC counts, arrhythmia type, frequency and duration, pauses, ST changes, and sample tracings. Some systems can also flag important events remotely; this depends on the device and service.</p><p>The symptom diary is especially useful. Ask the patient to record the exact date, time, activity and symptoms. The reader compares the rhythm before, during and after that event: symptom-rhythm correlation.</p><p>Symptoms without a corresponding rhythm abnormality can support broadening the differential to non-arrhythmic causes; interpret this within the limitations of the recording.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The lecturer used a three-second pause threshold as an absolute symptom cutoff. Do not memorize that as a rule: clinical significance depends on symptom correlation and context, rather than one universal pause duration. [1]</p>"},{"block":8,"title":"Slide 8 · Event Monitoring ","text":"Slide 8 Event Monitoring  Event Monitoring \n• For patients with less frequent (ie, weekly to \nmonthly) symptoms and for patients in whom a \ncomprehensive assessment of all cardiac activity over \na 24 to 48 hour interval is not required\n• Looping memory monitor is a small device about the \nsize of a pager that can be programmed to record \nyour ECG for a period of time, such as five minutes\n• push a button to activate it, and it stores your \nECG for the period before and during symptoms\n• Symptom event monitor a hand-held device \n• place the monitor on your chest and activate a \nrecording button\n• back of this device has small metal discs that \nfunction as the electrodes\n• stores your ECG in memory\n• won’t store your before you activate it Longer external patches or event monitors can help when symptoms are too infrequent for a short Holter. A handheld recorder is placed against the chest and activated during symptoms; the metal contacts act as electrodes. Distinguish a recorder that starts only when activated from a looping device that can preserve preceding rhythm. This affects whether the onset of the event is available for interpretation. An implantable loop recorder is a very small subcutaneous device used for much longer surveillance. The lecturer described monitoring for a year or two when needed; selection depends on the clinical problem.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">04:10-06:15 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-008.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 8 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-008.webp\" alt=\"Slide 8: Event Monitoring \"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Longer external patches or event monitors can help when symptoms are too infrequent for a short Holter. A handheld recorder is placed against the chest and activated during symptoms; the metal contacts act as electrodes.</p><p>Distinguish a recorder that starts only when activated from a looping device that can preserve preceding rhythm. This affects whether the onset of the event is available for interpretation.</p><p>An implantable loop recorder is a very small subcutaneous device used for much longer surveillance. The lecturer described monitoring for a year or two when needed; selection depends on the clinical problem.</p>"},{"block":9,"title":"Slide 9 · Figure or report","text":"Slide 9 Figure or report  This comparison is a reference for choosing continuous ECG, a Holter, an external loop recorder or an implantable loop recorder. Match the monitoring window to symptom frequency and the information needed. Implantable loops are inserted by cardiology or an arrhythmia specialist. Access and referral pathways vary by practice location.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">06:15-07:15 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-009.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 9 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-009.webp\" alt=\"Slide 9: Figure or report\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>This comparison is a reference for choosing continuous ECG, a Holter, an external loop recorder or an implantable loop recorder. Match the monitoring window to symptom frequency and the information needed.</p><p>Implantable loops are inserted by cardiology or an arrhythmia specialist. Access and referral pathways vary by practice location.</p>"},{"block":10,"title":"Slide 10 · Electrophysiology & Cardiac Conduction","text":"Slide 10 Electrophysiology & Cardiac Conduction Electrophysiology & Cardiac Conduction\n• Electrophysiology is the study of the electrical properties of \nbiological cells and tissues\n• involves measurements of voltage change or electric current \n• All cardiac cells have an electrical charge \n• Stimulation of one cell initiates stimulation of adjacent cells, \nultimately leading to cell contraction (you remember action \npotentials right!) \n• There are 2 types of cardiac cells: \n• Mechanical (Myocardial) \n• Responsible for muscular contraction and relaxation of the heart \n• Form the middle (muscle) layer of the atria and ventricles \n• Are able to stretch and contract \n• Electrical Cells \n• Specialized cells responsible for impulse formation and conduction The opening ECG section was mainly provided for independent review. Electrical cells generate and transmit impulses; myocardial cells produce the mechanical contraction. Keep the electrical event distinct from its mechanical consequence when relating the tracing to cardiac physiology.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">07:15-08:00 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-010.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 10 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-010.webp\" alt=\"Slide 10: Electrophysiology &amp; Cardiac Conduction\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The opening ECG section was mainly provided for independent review. Electrical cells generate and transmit impulses; myocardial cells produce the mechanical contraction.</p><p>Keep the electrical event distinct from its mechanical consequence when relating the tracing to cardiac physiology.</p>"},{"block":11,"title":"Slide 11 · Electrophysiology & Cardiac Conduction","text":"Slide 11 Electrophysiology & Cardiac Conduction Electrophysiology & Cardiac Conduction\n• The electrical impulse begins in \nthe SA node and travels through \nthe internodal tracts and \nBachmann’s bundle to the AV \nnode\n• From the AV node, the impulse \ntravels down the bundle of His, \nalong the bundle branches, and \nthrough the Purkinje fibers\n• Intrinsic firing rates\n• SA node — 60 to 100/minute\n• AV junction — 40 to 60/minute\n• Purkinje fibers — 20 to 40/minute Follow the impulse from the SA node across the atria to the AV node, then through the His-Purkinje system to the ventricles. The intended sequence is atrial contraction followed by ventricular contraction. Intrinsic pacing slows farther down the conduction system: SA node 60-100/min, AV junction 40-60/min, and Purkinje tissue 20-40/min. When a lower escape focus takes over, the resulting rate is usually slower. This relationship helps explain why more distal conduction disease can produce a markedly slow ventricular rate.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">07:42-08:58 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-011.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 11 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-011.webp\" alt=\"Slide 11: Electrophysiology &amp; Cardiac Conduction\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Follow the impulse from the SA node across the atria to the AV node, then through the His-Purkinje system to the ventricles. The intended sequence is atrial contraction followed by ventricular contraction.</p><p>Intrinsic pacing slows farther down the conduction system: SA node 60-100/min, AV junction 40-60/min, and Purkinje tissue 20-40/min. When a lower escape focus takes over, the resulting rate is usually slower.</p><p>This relationship helps explain why more distal conduction disease can produce a markedly slow ventricular rate.</p>"},{"block":12,"title":"Slide 12 · A Word About RE and DE ‘polarization’","text":"Slide 12 A Word About RE and DE ‘polarization’ A Word About RE and DE ‘polarization’\n•The electrical cells are able to generate and \nconduct electrical impulses resulting in \ndepolarization (contraction) and repolarization \n(relaxation and filling) of myocardial cells\n•These impulses are a result of a flow of positively \ncharged ions back and forth across the semi-\npermeable cardiac cell membrane\n•R in repolarization as standing for REST… \nremember that repolarization is the resting phase \nof the cardiac cycle This slide was passed over as part of the ECG review section. Depolarization is electrical activation; repolarization is electrical recovery. Use the slide to review the movement of ions and the relationship between electrical activation and the cardiac cycle.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-012.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 12 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-012.webp\" alt=\"Slide 12: A Word About RE and DE ‘polarization’\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>This slide was passed over as part of the ECG review section. Depolarization is electrical activation; repolarization is electrical recovery.</p><p>Use the slide to review the movement of ions and the relationship between electrical activation and the cardiac cycle.</p>"},{"block":13,"title":"Slide 13 · Atrial depolarization","text":"Slide 13 Atrial depolarization Atrial depolarization\ncontraction\nVentricular depolarization\nAV node\n“pause”\nVentricular\n repolarization Relate the P wave to atrial depolarization, the QRS to ventricular depolarization, and the T wave to ventricular repolarization. The PR interval includes conduction from atrial activation to ventricular activation. The figure was not discussed individually in the recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-013.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 13 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-013.webp\" alt=\"Slide 13: Atrial depolarization\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Relate the P wave to atrial depolarization, the QRS to ventricular depolarization, and the T wave to ventricular repolarization. The PR interval includes conduction from atrial activation to ventricular activation.</p><p>The figure was not discussed individually in the recording.</p>"},{"block":14,"title":"Slide 14 · Cardiac Conduction","text":"Slide 14 Cardiac Conduction Cardiac Conduction Use this conduction illustration to trace the sequence from the SA node through the AV node and His-Purkinje system. Relate the pathway to the waveforms on the previous slide. No separate spoken explanation was given for this figure.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-014.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 14 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-014.webp\" alt=\"Slide 14: Cardiac Conduction\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Use this conduction illustration to trace the sequence from the SA node through the AV node and His-Purkinje system. Relate the pathway to the waveforms on the previous slide.</p><p>No separate spoken explanation was given for this figure.</p>"},{"block":15,"title":"Slide 15 · The 12 Lead ECG","text":"Slide 15 The 12 Lead ECG The 12 Lead ECG\n• horizontal axis represents time\n• vertical axis represents voltage\n• grid is divided into large squares 5mm X 5mm\n• 1 small square = 40 ms (0.04 sec)\n• 1 large square (5 small squares) = 200 ms (0.2 sec)\n• 5 large squares (25 small squares) = 1000 ms (1 sec) At standard paper speed, one small horizontal square is 0.04 seconds, one large square is 0.20 seconds, and five large squares equal one second. The lecturer emphasized remembering the box durations before calculating rates or measuring intervals. The horizontal axis measures time; the vertical axis measures voltage.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-015.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 15 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-015.webp\" alt=\"Slide 15: The 12 Lead ECG\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>At standard paper speed, one small horizontal square is 0.04 seconds, one large square is 0.20 seconds, and five large squares equal one second.</p><p>The lecturer emphasized remembering the box durations before calculating rates or measuring intervals. The horizontal axis measures time; the vertical axis measures voltage.</p>"},{"block":16,"title":"Slide 16 · The 12 Lead ECG","text":"Slide 16 The 12 Lead ECG The 12 Lead ECG Use the lead-placement illustration to relate the limb and chest leads to their views of cardiac electrical activity. A 12-lead ECG records different perspectives of the same cardiac cycle. This was included for review rather than a separate live teaching segment.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=17\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-016.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 16 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-016.webp\" alt=\"Slide 16: The 12 Lead ECG\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Use the lead-placement illustration to relate the limb and chest leads to their views of cardiac electrical activity. A 12-lead ECG records different perspectives of the same cardiac cycle.</p><p>This was included for review rather than a separate live teaching segment.</p>"},{"block":17,"title":"Slide 17 · The Normal ECG","text":"Slide 17 The Normal ECG The Normal ECG Identify the normal waveform sequence before comparing abnormal examples. Locate the P wave, PR interval, QRS complex, ST segment and T wave. The lecturer's main instruction was to return to this introductory material independently if a refresher is needed.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-017.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 17 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-017.webp\" alt=\"Slide 17: The Normal ECG\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Identify the normal waveform sequence before comparing abnormal examples. Locate the P wave, PR interval, QRS complex, ST segment and T wave.</p><p>The lecturer&#x27;s main instruction was to return to this introductory material independently if a refresher is needed.</p>"},{"block":18,"title":"Slide 18 · The P Wave","text":"Slide 18 The P Wave The P Wave\n• first deflection representing \nthe depolarization of the atria\n• location - precedes the QRS \ncomplex\n• amplitude - 2 to 3 mm high\n• duration - 0.06 to 0.12 second\n• configuration - rounded and \nupright \n• peaked, notched, or enlarged P \nwaves - atrial hypertrophy or \nenlargement associated with \nCOPD, pulmonary emboli, \nvalvular disease, or heart \nfailure The P wave represents atrial depolarization. Assess its presence, shape and relationship to the QRS; compare waves across the strip rather than identifying just one. The slide lists size and duration features and examples of abnormal morphology. These details were not expanded on in the recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-018.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 18 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-018.webp\" alt=\"Slide 18: The P Wave\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The P wave represents atrial depolarization. Assess its presence, shape and relationship to the QRS; compare waves across the strip rather than identifying just one.</p><p>The slide lists size and duration features and examples of abnormal morphology. These details were not expanded on in the recording.</p>"},{"block":19,"title":"Slide 19 · The QRS Complex","text":"Slide 19 The QRS Complex The QRS Complex\n• The QRS complex consists of \none or multiple waves \nrepresenting the \ndepolarization of the \nventricles.\n• The Q Wave is the initial \nnegative (downward) \ndeflection.\n• The R Wave is the first \npositive (upright) deflection.\n• The S Wave is a negative \ndeflection following the R \nwave. The QRS represents ventricular depolarization. Q is the initial negative deflection, R the first positive deflection, and S a negative deflection after R. Not every complex contains all three visible components. Use this terminology when describing the tracing.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-019.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 19 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-019.webp\" alt=\"Slide 19: The QRS Complex\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The QRS represents ventricular depolarization. Q is the initial negative deflection, R the first positive deflection, and S a negative deflection after R.</p><p>Not every complex contains all three visible components. Use this terminology when describing the tracing.</p>"},{"block":20,"title":"Slide 20 · PR Interval","text":"Slide 20 PR Interval PR Interval\n• PR interval measured from \nthe beginning of the P wave \nto the beginning the QRS \ncomplex\n• duration - 0.12-0.20 seconds\n• includes the time when the \nimpulse travels the atria, A-\nV node, bundle of His, \nbundle branches, Purkinje \nfibres Measure PR from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS. The teaching range is 0.12-0.20 seconds. Check both its duration and whether it stays constant. Later AV-block examples depend on recognizing a prolonged, progressively changing or unrelated PR interval.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-020.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 20 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-020.webp\" alt=\"Slide 20: PR Interval\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Measure PR from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS. The teaching range is 0.12-0.20 seconds.</p><p>Check both its duration and whether it stays constant. Later AV-block examples depend on recognizing a prolonged, progressively changing or unrelated PR interval.</p>"},{"block":21,"title":"Slide 21 · QRS Duration","text":"Slide 21 QRS Duration QRS Duration\n• QRS duration measured from the \nbeginning to the end of the QRS \ncomplex (J point)\n• represents the time required for \nthe ventricles to depolarize\n• Location - follows the PR interval \n• Amplitude - 5 to 30 mm high but \ndiffers for each lead used\n• Duration - 0.06 to 0.10 second, or \nhalf of the PR interval \n• may not always see all three \nwaves; ventricles depolarize \nquickly, minimizing contact time \nbetween the stylus and the ECG \npaper Measure QRS from the beginning to the end of ventricular depolarization. Compare width and shape across the strip. The deck gives slightly different normal ranges in different places. For the later rhythm exercises, the practical narrow-versus-wide boundary is 0.12 seconds. [2]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-021.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 21 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-021.webp\" alt=\"Slide 21: QRS Duration\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Measure QRS from the beginning to the end of ventricular depolarization. Compare width and shape across the strip.</p><p>The deck gives slightly different normal ranges in different places. For the later rhythm exercises, the practical narrow-versus-wide boundary is 0.12 seconds. [2]</p>"},{"block":22,"title":"Slide 22 · The T Wave","text":"Slide 22 The T Wave The T Wave\n• The T wave represents the \nrepolarization of the \nventricles\n• Location - follows the S \nwave\n• Amplitude - 0.5 mm in leads \nI, II, and III and up to 10 mm \nin the precordial leads\n• Configuration - typically \nround and smooth The T wave represents ventricular repolarization. Assess its contour and direction in the relevant leads and in relation to the rest of the ECG. T-wave changes return later in the discussion of ischemia; this slide itself was not explained separately.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=23\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-022.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 22 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-022.webp\" alt=\"Slide 22: The T Wave\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The T wave represents ventricular repolarization. Assess its contour and direction in the relevant leads and in relation to the rest of the ECG.</p><p>T-wave changes return later in the discussion of ischemia; this slide itself was not explained separately.</p>"},{"block":23,"title":"Slide 23 · ST Segment","text":"Slide 23 ST Segment ST Segment\n• ST segment: the portion of \nthe ECG tracing from the \nend of the QRS complex (J \npoint) to the onset of the T \nwave\n• Location - extends from the \nS wave to the beginning of \nthe T wave\n• Deflection - usually \nisoelectric (neither positive \nnor negative); may vary \nfrom  - 0.5 to + 1 mm in \nsome leads The ST segment extends from the end of the QRS, at the J point, to the onset of the T wave. Assess it relative to the baseline. Elevation or depression must be interpreted across the relevant leads and in the clinical context, rather than from an isolated shape alone.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-023.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 23 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-023.webp\" alt=\"Slide 23: ST Segment\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The ST segment extends from the end of the QRS, at the J point, to the onset of the T wave. Assess it relative to the baseline.</p><p>Elevation or depression must be interpreted across the relevant leads and in the clinical context, rather than from an isolated shape alone.</p>"},{"block":24,"title":"Slide 24 · The QTc Duration","text":"Slide 24 The QTc Duration The QTc Duration\n• The QT interval is measured \nfrom the beginning of the \nQRS complex to the end of \nthe T wave\n• Location - extends from the \nbeginning of the QRS \ncomplex to the end of the T \nwave\n• Duration - varies according to \nage, sex, and heart rate\n• usually lasts from 0.36 to 0.44 \nsecond\n• shouldn’t be greater than half \nthe distance between \nconsecutive R waves when the \nrhythm is regular QT extends from the start of QRS to the end of T and includes ventricular depolarization and repolarization. Its expected duration changes with heart rate. !QT and QTc are different: QTc is QT corrected for heart rate. The half-R-R comparison is only a rough screen, not a substitute for assessing QTc. [2]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-024.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 24 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-024.webp\" alt=\"Slide 24: The QTc Duration\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>QT extends from the start of QRS to the end of T and includes ventricular depolarization and repolarization. Its expected duration changes with heart rate.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> QT and QTc are different: QTc is QT corrected for heart rate. The half-R-R comparison is only a rough screen, not a substitute for assessing QTc. [2]</p>"},{"block":25,"title":"Slide 25 · RR Interval","text":"Slide 25 RR Interval RR Interval\n• measured from the beginning of an R wave to the beginning of \nthe next R wave\n• represents the period of one cardiac cycle\n• used to calculate the heart rate\n• any wave in the QRS complex (or part of a wave, such as the \npeak of the R wave) can be used to determine the RR interval \nas long as you are consistent with the wave you choose R-R is the interval between consecutive ventricular complexes. Use the same reference point on each complex, usually the R-wave peak. Compare several intervals to assess regularity. The R-R calculation of rate works best when the rhythm is regular.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">08:58-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-025.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 25 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-025.webp\" alt=\"Slide 25: RR Interval\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>R-R is the interval between consecutive ventricular complexes. Use the same reference point on each complex, usually the R-wave peak.</p><p>Compare several intervals to assess regularity. The R-R calculation of rate works best when the rhythm is regular.</p>"},{"block":26,"title":"Slide 26 · Calculating Heart Rate","text":"Slide 26 Calculating Heart Rate Calculating Heart Rate The lecturer reviewed two approaches: count complexes over a known duration and scale to one minute, or use the R-R method for a regular rhythm. For a 10-second strip, multiply the number of QRS complexes by six. The following slide examples use six seconds, so multiply those counts by ten. Confirm the duration before choosing the multiplier.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">09:26-10:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-026.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 26 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-026.webp\" alt=\"Slide 26: Calculating Heart Rate\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The lecturer reviewed two approaches: count complexes over a known duration and scale to one minute, or use the R-R method for a regular rhythm.</p><p>For a 10-second strip, multiply the number of QRS complexes by six. The following slide examples use six seconds, so multiply those counts by ten. Confirm the duration before choosing the multiplier.</p>"},{"block":27,"title":"Slide 27 · Calculating Heart Rate","text":"Slide 27 Calculating Heart Rate Calculating Heart Rate\nR to R Method For a regular rhythm, heart rate is approximately 300 divided by the number of large boxes between two successive R waves. Example: two large boxes gives 150/min; two and a half gives 120/min. If the R-R intervals vary, use a timed-strip estimate rather than relying on one interval.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">09:53-10:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-027.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 27 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-027.webp\" alt=\"Slide 27: Calculating Heart Rate\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>For a regular rhythm, heart rate is approximately 300 divided by the number of large boxes between two successive R waves.</p><p>Example: two large boxes gives 150/min; two and a half gives 120/min. If the R-R intervals vary, use a timed-strip estimate rather than relying on one interval.</p>"},{"block":28,"title":"Slide 28 · Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate","text":"Slide 28 Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate\n1) Count out a 6 second section\n1 small box = 0.20 seconds \n5 large boxes = 1 sec\n30 large boxes = 6 seconds\n2) Count number of QRS in that duration\n3) Multiply number of QRS by 10\n12 x 10 = 120\nRate = 120 bpm\nRR Interval method (green arrows)\n300/2.5 = 120\nRate 120 bpm Six-second method: count 30 large squares, count QRS complexes within that window, then multiply by ten. Twelve complexes give 120/min. The R-R example reaches the same answer: 300 divided by 2.5 large squares equals 120/min. !The printed statement that one small box is 0.20 seconds is a typo. One small box is 0.04 seconds; one large box is 0.20 seconds.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">09:26-10:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=29\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-028.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 28 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-028.webp\" alt=\"Slide 28: Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Six-second method: count 30 large squares, count QRS complexes within that window, then multiply by ten. Twelve complexes give 120/min.</p><p>The R-R example reaches the same answer: 300 divided by 2.5 large squares equals 120/min.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The printed statement that one small box is 0.20 seconds is a typo. One small box is 0.04 seconds; one large box is 0.20 seconds.</p>"},{"block":29,"title":"Slide 29 · Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate","text":"Slide 29 Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate\n5 x 10 =  50\nRate = 50 bpm\nOR\n4 x 10 = 40\nRate = 50 bpm\nRate 45 bpm The example illustrates the imprecision of counting a short strip, particularly when a complex lies at the edge of the window. Four complexes over six seconds estimate 40/min; five estimate 50/min. !The slide's line '4 x 10 = 40' is followed by a printed rate of 50/min. The arithmetic result is 40/min. A longer sample or regular R-R measurement can refine the estimate.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">09:26-10:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=30\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-029.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 29 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-029.webp\" alt=\"Slide 29: Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The example illustrates the imprecision of counting a short strip, particularly when a complex lies at the edge of the window. Four complexes over six seconds estimate 40/min; five estimate 50/min.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The slide&#x27;s line &#x27;4 x 10 = 40&#x27; is followed by a printed rate of 50/min. The arithmetic result is 40/min. A longer sample or regular R-R measurement can refine the estimate.</p>"},{"block":30,"title":"Slide 30 · Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate","text":"Slide 30 Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate\n3 x 10 =  30\nRate = 30 bpm Practice the timed-strip method: three QRS complexes in six seconds give an estimated ventricular rate of 30/min. The number identifies marked bradycardia; the rest of the tracing and the patient's perfusion determine its clinical meaning.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">09:26-10:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-030.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 30 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-030.webp\" alt=\"Slide 30: Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Practice the timed-strip method: three QRS complexes in six seconds give an estimated ventricular rate of 30/min.</p><p>The number identifies marked bradycardia; the rest of the tracing and the patient&#x27;s perfusion determine its clinical meaning.</p>"},{"block":31,"title":"Slide 31 · Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate","text":"Slide 31 Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate\n14 x 10 =  140\nRate = 140 bpm Fourteen QRS complexes in six seconds give approximately 140/min. After calculating the rate, assess regularity, P waves and the P-QRS relationship before naming the rhythm. Rate alone does not identify its origin.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">09:26-10:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=32\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-031.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 31 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-031.webp\" alt=\"Slide 31: Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Fourteen QRS complexes in six seconds give approximately 140/min.</p><p>After calculating the rate, assess regularity, P waves and the P-QRS relationship before naming the rhythm. Rate alone does not identify its origin.</p>"},{"block":32,"title":"Slide 32 · Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate","text":"Slide 32 Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate\n4 x 10 =  40\nRate = 40 bpm Four QRS complexes in six seconds give approximately 40/min. Use the same sequence for every tracing: rate, rhythm, atrial activity, conduction and clinical context.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">09:26-10:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=33\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-032.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 32 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-032.webp\" alt=\"Slide 32: Let’s Calculate the Heart Rate\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Four QRS complexes in six seconds give approximately 40/min.</p><p>Use the same sequence for every tracing: rate, rhythm, atrial activity, conduction and clinical context.</p>"},{"block":33,"title":"Slide 33 · A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation","text":"Slide 33 A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\n1) Determine the rhythm - atrial and ventricular, \nregular or irregular?\n2) Determine the rate\n3) Evaluate the P wave\n4) Determine the duration of the PR interval\n5) Duration of the QRS duration\n6) Evaluate the T waves\n7) Determine the duration of the QT interval\n8) Evaluate any other components The lecturer supplied a stepwise approach for later reference rather than working through every step live. Use one consistent sequence so that an obvious abnormality does not distract you from the remaining components. Assess rhythm and rate, P waves, PR, QRS, T waves and QT, then other features such as ST changes.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">10:23-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=34\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-033.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 33 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-033.webp\" alt=\"Slide 33: A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The lecturer supplied a stepwise approach for later reference rather than working through every step live. Use one consistent sequence so that an obvious abnormality does not distract you from the remaining components.</p><p>Assess rhythm and rate, P waves, PR, QRS, T waves and QT, then other features such as ST changes.</p>"},{"block":34,"title":"Slide 34 · A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation","text":"Slide 34 A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\n• Determine the rhythm - atrial and ventricular, regular or irregular?\n• Starting at the left side of the rhythm strip, measure the distance between 2 \nR waves\n• Measure from R wave to R wave across the rhythm strip \n• Can be regular or irregular\n• Sometimes this is very obvious and you do not need to measure the \nR-R interval to determine regularity Compare R-R intervals across the whole strip, not just a single pair. Assess atrial regularity separately if P waves can be identified. Some patterns are clearly irregular by inspection; measurement is helpful when the variation is subtle.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">10:23-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=35\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-034.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 34 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-034.webp\" alt=\"Slide 34: A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Compare R-R intervals across the whole strip, not just a single pair. Assess atrial regularity separately if P waves can be identified.</p><p>Some patterns are clearly irregular by inspection; measurement is helpful when the variation is subtle.</p>"},{"block":35,"title":"Slide 35 · A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation","text":"Slide 35 A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\n• Determine the rate\n• Six-second method\n• Look at six second strip (30 squares)\n• Count the number of QRS complexes in the period of six seconds and multiply by 10 \n(ventricular rate) & count the number of P waves for that same period to determine \nthe atrial rate\n• Can be used for regular or irregular rhythms\n• Simplest method In six seconds, QRS count multiplied by ten estimates ventricular rate. P-wave count multiplied by ten estimates atrial rate. Keeping the two rates separate becomes important when some atrial impulses are not conducted to the ventricles.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">10:23-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=36\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-035.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 35 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-035.webp\" alt=\"Slide 35: A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>In six seconds, QRS count multiplied by ten estimates ventricular rate. P-wave count multiplied by ten estimates atrial rate.</p><p>Keeping the two rates separate becomes important when some atrial impulses are not conducted to the ventricles.</p>"},{"block":36,"title":"Slide 36 · A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation","text":"Slide 36 A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\n• Evaluate the P wave\n• What does it look like?\n• Normal in appearance?\n• Are they identical?\n• Is there a P wave for every QRS complex?\n• What is the ratio of P waves to QRS complexes?\n• Is there a QRS for every P?\n• ECG hint… P for every QRS, QRS for every P?\n• SINUS RHYTHM Ask whether P waves are present, look alike, precede each QRS, and are followed by a QRS. Count the P-to-QRS relationship. !A one-to-one relationship supports organized conduction but does not alone prove sinus origin. Assess compatible P-wave morphology and the rest of the tracing as well. [2]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">10:23-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=37\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-036.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 36 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-036.webp\" alt=\"Slide 36: A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Ask whether P waves are present, look alike, precede each QRS, and are followed by a QRS. Count the P-to-QRS relationship.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> A one-to-one relationship supports organized conduction but does not alone prove sinus origin. Assess compatible P-wave morphology and the rest of the tracing as well. [2]</p>"},{"block":37,"title":"Slide 37 · A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation","text":"Slide 37 A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\n• Determine the duration of the PR interval\n• Measured from the beginning of the P wave to the beginning \nof the QRS complex\n• Should be between 0.12-0.20 seconds\n• Is the PR Interval measurement the same for each complex? Measure PR from the start of P to the start of QRS and compare it from beat to beat. The teaching range is 0.12-0.20 seconds. A long but fixed PR, a progressively lengthening PR, and P waves unrelated to QRS represent different patterns in the later block examples.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">10:23-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=38\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-037.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 37 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-037.webp\" alt=\"Slide 37: A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Measure PR from the start of P to the start of QRS and compare it from beat to beat. The teaching range is 0.12-0.20 seconds.</p><p>A long but fixed PR, a progressively lengthening PR, and P waves unrelated to QRS represent different patterns in the later block examples.</p>"},{"block":38,"title":"Slide 38 · A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation","text":"Slide 38 A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\n• Duration of the QRS duration\n• All QRS complexes should look alike\n• Measure from where the QRS leaves the baseline to where the ST segment \nbegins\n• Normal duration is 0.04-0.12 seconds\n• Considered ‘narrow’ if less than 0.12 seconds\n• Considered ‘wide’ if less than 0.12 seconds Compare QRS width and morphology throughout the tracing. A wide complex directs attention to altered ventricular conduction or ventricular origin, depending on the full pattern. !The final bullet is a typo: narrow QRS is less than 0.12 seconds; wide QRS is 0.12 seconds or greater, not less than 0.12 seconds. [2]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">10:23-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=39\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-038.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 38 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-038.webp\" alt=\"Slide 38: A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Compare QRS width and morphology throughout the tracing. A wide complex directs attention to altered ventricular conduction or ventricular origin, depending on the full pattern.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The final bullet is a typo: narrow QRS is less than 0.12 seconds; wide QRS is 0.12 seconds or greater, not less than 0.12 seconds. [2]</p>"},{"block":39,"title":"Slide 39 · A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation","text":"Slide 39 A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\n• Evaluate the T waves (measure QT)\n• QT Interval measured from the beginning of the QRS complex to the end of \nthe T wave\n• Normal duration is based on heart rate\n• T wave is electrical only, there is no simultaneous mechanical action\n• Inversion or flattening of the T wave may indicate myocardial ischemia or \ninjury Assess T waves and measure QT from QRS onset to the end of T. Compare findings with heart rate, other leads and the clinical presentation. The lecturer left this measurement section for independent review; the later coronary discussion revisits T-wave abnormalities.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">10:23-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-039.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 39 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-039.webp\" alt=\"Slide 39: A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Assess T waves and measure QT from QRS onset to the end of T. Compare findings with heart rate, other leads and the clinical presentation.</p><p>The lecturer left this measurement section for independent review; the later coronary discussion revisits T-wave abnormalities.</p>"},{"block":40,"title":"Slide 40 · A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation","text":"Slide 40 A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\n• Evaluate any other components\n• ST segment\n• Measure the ST segment from the end of the QRS complex to the beginning \nof the T wave\n• Should be flat\n• Elevation or depression may indicate myocardial ischemia or injury Assess ST segments after reviewing the basic rhythm. Look for deviations from the baseline and whether they occur in a meaningful group of leads. ST changes may support ischemia or injury but require clinical interpretation; the deck returns to this in the ACS section.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">10:23-10:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=41\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-040.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 40 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-040.webp\" alt=\"Slide 40: A Stepwise Approach to ECG Interpretation\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Assess ST segments after reviewing the basic rhythm. Look for deviations from the baseline and whether they occur in a meaningful group of leads.</p><p>ST changes may support ischemia or injury but require clinical interpretation; the deck returns to this in the ACS section.</p>"},{"block":41,"title":"Slide 41 · The ‘Sinus’ Rhythms","text":"Slide 41 The ‘Sinus’ Rhythms The ‘Sinus’ Rhythms Recognizing the normal pattern is the foundation for recognizing an abnormal rhythm. The lecturer introduced the sinus examples by asking students to establish the expected P-QRS sequence, regularity and rate first. Then describe what has changed: speed, regularity, premature beats or conduction.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">10:57-11:27 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=42\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-041.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 41 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-041.webp\" alt=\"Slide 41: The ‘Sinus’ Rhythms\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Recognizing the normal pattern is the foundation for recognizing an abnormal rhythm. The lecturer introduced the sinus examples by asking students to establish the expected P-QRS sequence, regularity and rate first.</p><p>Then describe what has changed: speed, regularity, premature beats or conduction.</p>"},{"block":42,"title":"Slide 42 · Recognizing Normal Sinus Rhythm","text":"Slide 42 Recognizing Normal Sinus Rhythm Recognizing Normal Sinus Rhythm\n• Before you can recognize an arrhythmia, you must first be able to \nrecognize normal sinus rhythm! \n• Characteristics of normal sinus rhythm:\n• Regular rhythm\n• Normal rate (60-100 bpm)\n• A P wave for every QRS complex; all P waves similar in size and shape\n• All QRS complexes similar in size and shape\n• Normal PR and QT intervals\n• Normal (upright and round) T waves Normal sinus rhythm has a regular atrial and ventricular pattern, a rate of 60-100/min, consistent P waves, and one P associated with each QRS. Assess intervals and QRS morphology as well. The lecturer's quick visual approach was to look for the repeating P-QRS pattern and regular R-R spacing before deciding which sinus category applies. Keep the full set of criteria in mind rather than using the one-to-one relationship by itself.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">11:27-12:22 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=43\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-042.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 42 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-042.webp\" alt=\"Slide 42: Recognizing Normal Sinus Rhythm\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Normal sinus rhythm has a regular atrial and ventricular pattern, a rate of 60-100/min, consistent P waves, and one P associated with each QRS. Assess intervals and QRS morphology as well.</p><p>The lecturer&#x27;s quick visual approach was to look for the repeating P-QRS pattern and regular R-R spacing before deciding which sinus category applies.</p><p>Keep the full set of criteria in mind rather than using the one-to-one relationship by itself.</p>"},{"block":43,"title":"Slide 43 · Sinus Rhythm","text":"Slide 43 Sinus Rhythm Sinus Rhythm\n•Rate: 60 to 100 bpm\n•Rhythm: Regular, P-P constant - atrial regular, R-R \nconstant - ventricular regular\n•P waves: Uniform\n•P/QRS ratio: 1:1\n•PR interval: 0.12-0.20 seconds\n•QRS Complex: 0.04-0.12 seconds Check that P-P spacing is consistent and R-R spacing is consistent. This means the atrial and ventricular rhythms are both regular. In the uncomplicated sinus example, P waves have a consistent appearance, P:QRS is 1:1 and PR is within the stated range. Once the rhythm is established, use the rate to distinguish normal sinus rhythm from sinus tachycardia or bradycardia.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">11:52-12:22 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=44\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-043.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 43 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-043.webp\" alt=\"Slide 43: Sinus Rhythm\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Check that P-P spacing is consistent and R-R spacing is consistent. This means the atrial and ventricular rhythms are both regular.</p><p>In the uncomplicated sinus example, P waves have a consistent appearance, P:QRS is 1:1 and PR is within the stated range. Once the rhythm is established, use the rate to distinguish normal sinus rhythm from sinus tachycardia or bradycardia.</p>"},{"block":44,"title":"Slide 44 · Sinus Rhythm","text":"Slide 44 Sinus Rhythm Sinus Rhythm On the 12-lead example, the lecturer used the continuous strip at the bottom to follow the repeating P-QRS relationship. Different leads view the same electrical activity from different directions. Scan the rhythm strip for continuity, then review the individual leads. The bottom strip is commonly lead II; some printouts use a different lead.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">12:22-12:51 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=45\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-044.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 44 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-044.webp\" alt=\"Slide 44: Sinus Rhythm\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>On the 12-lead example, the lecturer used the continuous strip at the bottom to follow the repeating P-QRS relationship. Different leads view the same electrical activity from different directions.</p><p>Scan the rhythm strip for continuity, then review the individual leads. The bottom strip is commonly lead II; some printouts use a different lead.</p>"},{"block":45,"title":"Slide 45 · Sinus Tachycardia","text":"Slide 45 Sinus Tachycardia Sinus Tachycardia\n•Rate: 100 to 160 bpm\n•Rhythm: Regular, P-P constant-atrial regular, R-R \nconstant-ventricular regular\n•P waves: Uniform\n•P/QRS ratio: 1:1\n•PR interval: 0.12-0.20 seconds\n•QRS Complex: < 0.12 seconds\n•Often is a response to another clinical event - \ntreatment is usually directed toward the underlying \ncause (i.e. infection, hypovolemia, pain) Sinus tachycardia retains the sinus pattern but is faster. Faster rates reduce ventricular filling time; consider the effect on cardiac output, especially when heart function is already impaired. Usually look for a driver such as infection or sepsis, hypovolemia, or pain, and direct management toward that cause. The lecturer also mentioned inappropriate sinus tachycardia and POTS as distinct diagnoses to consider in the appropriate presentation.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">12:51-14:21 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=46\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-045.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 45 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-045.webp\" alt=\"Slide 45: Sinus Tachycardia\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Sinus tachycardia retains the sinus pattern but is faster. Faster rates reduce ventricular filling time; consider the effect on cardiac output, especially when heart function is already impaired.</p><p>Usually look for a driver such as infection or sepsis, hypovolemia, or pain, and direct management toward that cause. The lecturer also mentioned inappropriate sinus tachycardia and POTS as distinct diagnoses to consider in the appropriate presentation.</p>"},{"block":46,"title":"Slide 46 · Sinus Tachycardia","text":"Slide 46 Sinus Tachycardia Sinus Tachycardia Student question: can AI interpret ECGs? The lecturer emphasized that machine-generated ECG interpretations use algorithms and can be wrong. A clinician must review the actual tracing and confirm or correct the interpretation. Her workplace uses cardiologist review and sign-off. An automated label does not remove the NP's responsibility to recognize the basic pattern and findings that need attention. !Do not use a narrow or wide QRS as a simple measure of how fast the heart is going. Measure ventricular conduction separately; QRS width is not determined by rate alone. [2]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">14:21-17:41 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=47\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-046.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 46 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-046.webp\" alt=\"Slide 46: Sinus Tachycardia\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and discussion</h3><p>Student question: can AI interpret ECGs? The lecturer emphasized that machine-generated ECG interpretations use algorithms and can be wrong. A clinician must review the actual tracing and confirm or correct the interpretation.</p><p>Her workplace uses cardiologist review and sign-off. An automated label does not remove the NP&#x27;s responsibility to recognize the basic pattern and findings that need attention.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> Do not use a narrow or wide QRS as a simple measure of how fast the heart is going. Measure ventricular conduction separately; QRS width is not determined by rate alone. [2]</p>"},{"block":47,"title":"Slide 47 · Sinus Bradycardia","text":"Slide 47 Sinus Bradycardia Sinus Bradycardia\n•Rate: 40 to 60 bpm\n•Rhythm: Regular, P-P constant-atrial regular, R-R \nconstant-ventricular regular\n•P waves: Uniform\n•P/QRS ratio: 1:1\n•PR interval: 0.12-0.20 seconds\n•QRS Complex: <0.12 seconds\n•Decreased rate is usually from a reduction in \nsympathetic output or excessive vagal tone\n•May be normal & patient may be asymptomatic Sinus bradycardia retains the sinus pattern at a slower rate. It can be a normal finding when the person is asymptomatic and the rhythm truly is sinus. The practical question is whether the rate is tolerated. A slow number alone and a slow rhythm with evidence of poor perfusion are different presentations.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">17:41-18:42 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=48\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-047.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 47 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-047.webp\" alt=\"Slide 47: Sinus Bradycardia\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Sinus bradycardia retains the sinus pattern at a slower rate. It can be a normal finding when the person is asymptomatic and the rhythm truly is sinus.</p><p>The practical question is whether the rate is tolerated. A slow number alone and a slow rhythm with evidence of poor perfusion are different presentations.</p>"},{"block":48,"title":"Slide 48 · Sinus Bradycardia","text":"Slide 48 Sinus Bradycardia Sinus Bradycardia Use the example to confirm the sinus P-QRS relationship and slow rate rather than assuming every slow rhythm is sinus bradycardia. !The spoken suggestion that QRS simply widens because the heart is slower is an oversimplification. QRS duration reflects ventricular depolarization and conduction; a widened complex needs its own assessment. [2]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">17:41-18:42 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=49\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-048.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 48 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-048.webp\" alt=\"Slide 48: Sinus Bradycardia\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Use the example to confirm the sinus P-QRS relationship and slow rate rather than assuming every slow rhythm is sinus bradycardia.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The spoken suggestion that QRS simply widens because the heart is slower is an oversimplification. QRS duration reflects ventricular depolarization and conduction; a widened complex needs its own assessment. [2]</p>"},{"block":49,"title":"Slide 49 · Sinus Bradycardia…When to Worry","text":"Slide 49 Sinus Bradycardia…When to Worry Sinus Bradycardia…When to Worry\n(and we will get into this more with syncope!)\n• Some patients are asymptomatic… don’t worry!\n• At slower rates some patients may show signs and \nsymptoms consistent with decreased perfusion\n• If a patient can’t tolerate bradycardia, they may \ndevelop these signs and symptoms:\n• Hypotension\n• Cool, clammy skin\n• Altered mental status\n• Dizziness\n• Blurred vision\n• Heart failure symptoms (crackles, dyspnea, and an S3 heart \nsound)\n• Chest pain\n• Presyncope (faint, lightheaded, say they feel like they are \ngoing to pass out\n• Syncope (overt LOC) Look for evidence that bradycardia is impairing perfusion: hypotension, cool clammy skin, confusion, dizziness, visual symptoms, chest pain, presyncope or syncope. Some patients tolerate slow rates; others, particularly with underlying disease, develop symptoms or heart failure. The lecturer described prolonged unrecognized complete heart block presenting with heart failure. Distinguish feeling faint from actual loss of consciousness. Both matter when deciding whether a slow rhythm is clinically significant.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">18:42-20:25 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=50\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-049.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 49 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-049.webp\" alt=\"Slide 49: Sinus Bradycardia…When to Worry\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Look for evidence that bradycardia is impairing perfusion: hypotension, cool clammy skin, confusion, dizziness, visual symptoms, chest pain, presyncope or syncope.</p><p>Some patients tolerate slow rates; others, particularly with underlying disease, develop symptoms or heart failure. The lecturer described prolonged unrecognized complete heart block presenting with heart failure.</p><p>Distinguish feeling faint from actual loss of consciousness. Both matter when deciding whether a slow rhythm is clinically significant.</p>"},{"block":50,"title":"Slide 50 · Sinus Arrhythmia","text":"Slide 50 Sinus Arrhythmia Sinus Arrhythmia\n• Impulse originates in SA node in an irregular pattern\n• Arrhythmia is characterized by an increase in rate \nduring inspiration and a decrease during expiration\n• Each beat is conducted normally\n• Rate: Normal (60-100) or sometimes slow (less than 60)\n• Rhythm: Irregular\n• P waves: Uniform\n• P/QRS ratio: 1:1\n• PR interval: 0.12-0.20 seconds\n• QRS Complex: < 0.12 seconds\n• Very common in children and young adults Sinus arrhythmia meets the other sinus criteria but has variable R-R spacing. Respiratory variation is common in children and young adults. The rhythm can therefore be irregular without being AF. Look for consistent sinus P waves and a P-QRS relationship before interpreting the irregularity.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">20:25-20:58 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=51\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-050.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 50 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-050.webp\" alt=\"Slide 50: Sinus Arrhythmia\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Sinus arrhythmia meets the other sinus criteria but has variable R-R spacing. Respiratory variation is common in children and young adults.</p><p>The rhythm can therefore be irregular without being AF. Look for consistent sinus P waves and a P-QRS relationship before interpreting the irregularity.</p>"},{"block":51,"title":"Slide 51 · Sinus Arrhythmia","text":"Slide 51 Sinus Arrhythmia Sinus Arrhythmia Follow each P wave into its QRS. The complexes maintain the sinus pattern even though the distance between successive R waves changes. The lecturer contrasted intervals that look similar with intervals that are visibly shorter or longer. This is the key visual difference from the completely regular sinus examples; the teaching example is benign respiratory sinus variation.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">20:58-22:02 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=52\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-051.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 51 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-051.webp\" alt=\"Slide 51: Sinus Arrhythmia\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Follow each P wave into its QRS. The complexes maintain the sinus pattern even though the distance between successive R waves changes.</p><p>The lecturer contrasted intervals that look similar with intervals that are visibly shorter or longer. This is the key visual difference from the completely regular sinus examples; the teaching example is benign respiratory sinus variation.</p>"},{"block":52,"title":"Slide 52 · AHAD I Case Study #3","text":"Slide 52 AHAD I Case Study #3 AHAD I Case Study #3\n• Julie, 38 years old, presents to the clinic reporting that she has felt \nher heart \"skipping beats\" over the past two months. Her family \nhistory is positive for paternal death from an MI at age 40. She \nsmokes two packs of cigarettes and drinks two pots of coffee per day. \nHer cardiac examination reveals: HR 84, regular; BP 124/74; normal \nS1 and S2; no ectopic beats; no S3 or S4.\n• 1.  List the differential diagnoses relevant to Julie's symptoms.\n• 2.  For your top three differential diagnoses, review the typical clinical \n     presentation (history and physical examination) associated with each.\n• 3.  What diagnostic test(s) would the NP order for Julie at this time? Julie is 38, with two months of skipped-beat sensations, a father who died young from MI, heavy smoking and two pots of coffee daily. Her examination and vital signs are reassuring between episodes. Caffeine and nicotine are plausible contributors to ectopy or anxiety symptoms, but do not dismiss a cardiac cause solely because stimulants are present. The family history requires attention. The lecturer returned to this case during the PAC/PVC and investigation discussion. A normal examination does not capture what the rhythm was doing during an earlier symptom episode.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">22:02-28:19 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=53\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-052.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 52 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-052.webp\" alt=\"Slide 52: AHAD I Case Study #3\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and case discussion</h3><p>Julie is 38, with two months of skipped-beat sensations, a father who died young from MI, heavy smoking and two pots of coffee daily. Her examination and vital signs are reassuring between episodes.</p><p>Caffeine and nicotine are plausible contributors to ectopy or anxiety symptoms, but do not dismiss a cardiac cause solely because stimulants are present. The family history requires attention.</p><p>The lecturer returned to this case during the PAC/PVC and investigation discussion. A normal examination does not capture what the rhythm was doing during an earlier symptom episode.</p>"},{"block":53,"title":"Slide 53 · Palpitations","text":"Slide 53 Palpitations Palpitations\n• one of the most common symptoms of patients presenting to \nprimary care providers and cardiologists\n• although the cause is usually benign, palpitations are occasionally a \nmanifestation of a concerning or potentially life-threatening \narrhythmia\n• subjective symptom\n• defined as an unpleasant awareness of the forceful, rapid, or irregular \nbeating of the heart\n• may describe sensation as a rapid fluttering or a flip-flopping in the chest, or \na pounding sensation in the chest or neck, ‘butterflies’\n• most common causes of palpitations include cardiac disorders, \nmedical conditions, psychiatric disorders, medication effects, and \ndrug or other substance use effects Palpitations are a subjective awareness of heartbeat, not a specific rhythm diagnosis. Patients may say fluttering, flip-flopping, pounding, butterflies or skipped beats; some feel the sensation in the neck or across the chest. Most causes are benign, but the same complaint can accompany a concerning arrhythmia. Clarify the patient's description, associated symptoms, triggers and background risk rather than treating the word 'palpitations' as the diagnosis.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">28:19-29:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=54\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-053.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 53 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-053.webp\" alt=\"Slide 53: Palpitations\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Palpitations are a subjective awareness of heartbeat, not a specific rhythm diagnosis. Patients may say fluttering, flip-flopping, pounding, butterflies or skipped beats; some feel the sensation in the neck or across the chest.</p><p>Most causes are benign, but the same complaint can accompany a concerning arrhythmia. Clarify the patient&#x27;s description, associated symptoms, triggers and background risk rather than treating the word &#x27;palpitations&#x27; as the diagnosis.</p>"},{"block":54,"title":"Slide 54 · Preatrial Contractions (PACs)","text":"Slide 54 Preatrial Contractions (PACs) Preatrial Contractions (PACs)\n• An early beat that originates in an ectopic pacemaker in \nthe atria\n• Occurs before the next expected normal complex\n• Single beat (not a rhythm)\n• Every other beat called bigeminy), every 3rd called \ntrigeminy\n• Shape of P wave is different (i.e. notched or flattened)\n• Underlying rhythm must always be identified\n• May originate from a single ectopic pacemaker site or \nfrom multiple sites in the atria A premature atrial contraction is an early beat from an atrial site outside the SA node. It arrives before the next expected sinus complex. A PAC is an individual ectopic beat, not the name of the underlying rhythm. The early P wave may be notched, flattened or otherwise unlike the usual sinus P waves. Describe the baseline rhythm first, then the ectopy. Bigeminy means every second beat is premature; trigeminy means every third. Different atrial origins can produce different P-wave shapes. !Use the standard term 'premature atrial contraction'; the slide's 'preatrial' wording is nonstandard.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">29:56-31:49 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=55\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-054.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 54 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-054.webp\" alt=\"Slide 54: Preatrial Contractions (PACs)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>A premature atrial contraction is an early beat from an atrial site outside the SA node. It arrives before the next expected sinus complex. A PAC is an individual ectopic beat, not the name of the underlying rhythm.</p><p>The early P wave may be notched, flattened or otherwise unlike the usual sinus P waves. Describe the baseline rhythm first, then the ectopy.</p><p>Bigeminy means every second beat is premature; trigeminy means every third. Different atrial origins can produce different P-wave shapes.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> Use the standard term &#x27;premature atrial contraction&#x27;; the slide&#x27;s &#x27;preatrial&#x27; wording is nonstandard.</p>"},{"block":55,"title":"Slide 55 · Preatrial Contractions (PACs)","text":"Slide 55 Preatrial Contractions (PACs) Preatrial Contractions (PACs)\n•Rate: Normal for age, dependent on normal \nrhythm\n•Rhythm: Irregular, due to premature ectopic beat\n•P waves: P waves associated with PACs are \npremature and abnormal in size, shape (sometimes \npeaked), direction (can be inverted), often found \nhidden in preceding T wave distorting T wave\n•P/QRS Ratio: 1:1\n•PRI: 0.11-0.20, may be prolonged \n•QRS: < 0.12 seconds The overall rate is determined by the underlying rhythm. The premature beat makes the strip appear irregular, but the remaining beats still need ordinary rhythm interpretation. The important change is the timing and morphology of the ectopic P wave. It may partly overlap the previous T wave, making that T wave look different.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">31:49-32:20 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=56\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-055.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 55 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-055.webp\" alt=\"Slide 55: Preatrial Contractions (PACs)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The overall rate is determined by the underlying rhythm. The premature beat makes the strip appear irregular, but the remaining beats still need ordinary rhythm interpretation.</p><p>The important change is the timing and morphology of the ectopic P wave. It may partly overlap the previous T wave, making that T wave look different.</p>"},{"block":56,"title":"Slide 56 · Preatrial Contractions (PACs)","text":"Slide 56 Preatrial Contractions (PACs) Preatrial Contractions (PACs) Compare the patient's usual P-QRS complex with the early complex: the ectopic P looks different, arrives early and is followed by a QRS. The tracing then returns to its underlying pattern. The lecturer pointed out a repeating every-third-beat pattern in one example. Patients may perceive the premature beat as a palpitation even when the overall underlying rhythm is sinus.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">32:20-33:50 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=57\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-056.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 56 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-056.webp\" alt=\"Slide 56: Preatrial Contractions (PACs)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Compare the patient&#x27;s usual P-QRS complex with the early complex: the ectopic P looks different, arrives early and is followed by a QRS. The tracing then returns to its underlying pattern.</p><p>The lecturer pointed out a repeating every-third-beat pattern in one example. Patients may perceive the premature beat as a palpitation even when the overall underlying rhythm is sinus.</p>"},{"block":57,"title":"Slide 57 · Preventricular Contractions (PVCs)","text":"Slide 57 Preventricular Contractions (PVCs) Preventricular Contractions (PVCs)\n• Common in hearts that are diseased or injured\n• Impulse arises from an irritable focus in the ventricle\n• Ectopic ventricular beat that occurs before the sinus \nconducted QRS\n• QRS is wide and bizarre\n• Usually compensatory pause\n• May be unifocal or multifocal\n• Impulse of the PVC doesn’t follow the usual bundle \nbranch conduction system\n• Results in slow conduction and a wide QRS A PVC comes from an irritable ventricular focus before the next expected sinus-conducted beat. It can occur in a normal heart but deserves more attention in a diseased or injured heart. The teaching visual is a premature, wide, unusually shaped QRS that does not match the surrounding sinus complexes. Ventricular activation does not follow the usual rapid conduction pathway, helping explain the broad appearance. !The lecturer initially described four or more beats as a VT run, then later corrected this: three or more consecutive ventricular beats at a tachycardic rate meet the teaching definition of VT. Use slide 126 for the consistent definition.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">33:50-35:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=58\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-057.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 57 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-057.webp\" alt=\"Slide 57: Preventricular Contractions (PVCs)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>A PVC comes from an irritable ventricular focus before the next expected sinus-conducted beat. It can occur in a normal heart but deserves more attention in a diseased or injured heart.</p><p>The teaching visual is a premature, wide, unusually shaped QRS that does not match the surrounding sinus complexes. Ventricular activation does not follow the usual rapid conduction pathway, helping explain the broad appearance.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The lecturer initially described four or more beats as a VT run, then later corrected this: three or more consecutive ventricular beats at a tachycardic rate meet the teaching definition of VT. Use slide 126 for the consistent definition.</p>"},{"block":58,"title":"Slide 58 · Premature Ventricular Complexes","text":"Slide 58 Premature Ventricular Complexes Premature Ventricular Complexes\nPossible Causes\n• Aging\n• Electrolyte imbalances\n• Medications: aminophylline, \nventolin, caffeine\n• Toxicities \n• Stress\n• Pregnancy\n• And other bad stuff… like cardiac \nischemia, hypoxia, acidosis, \nhypothermia, trauma, \nhyper/hypokalemia\nSigns and Symptoms\n• Generally asymptomatic\n• Describe feeling a ‘skipped beat’, \npalpitations\n• Observe for increasing \nfrequency/duration\n• Hemodynamic instability For Julie, heavy nicotine and caffeine exposure are plausible contributors in the absence of other reported symptoms. The lecturer favored starting with this explanation while continuing an appropriate assessment. Review aging, medications such as salbutamol, other stimulants, drug toxicity, stress, pregnancy, oxygenation, acid-base issues and potassium abnormalities. Ischemia is one possible serious cause in the right context. !Absence of chest pain or a normal examination alone does not exclude ischemia. The case discussion was a probability judgment about this presentation, not a rule-out test.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">35:23-37:31 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=59\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-058.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 58 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-058.webp\" alt=\"Slide 58: Premature Ventricular Complexes\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and case discussion</h3><p>For Julie, heavy nicotine and caffeine exposure are plausible contributors in the absence of other reported symptoms. The lecturer favored starting with this explanation while continuing an appropriate assessment.</p><p>Review aging, medications such as salbutamol, other stimulants, drug toxicity, stress, pregnancy, oxygenation, acid-base issues and potassium abnormalities. Ischemia is one possible serious cause in the right context.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> Absence of chest pain or a normal examination alone does not exclude ischemia. The case discussion was a probability judgment about this presentation, not a rule-out test.</p>"},{"block":59,"title":"Slide 59 · Preventricular Contractions (PVCs)","text":"Slide 59 Preventricular Contractions (PVCs) Preventricular Contractions (PVCs)\n•Rate: Depends on the underlying rhythm\n•Rhythm:  Atrial is irregular due to PVC; ventricular \ncan be regular or irregular\n•P Waves:  P waves associated with PVCs\n•P/QRS:  PVC is not proceeded by a P wave\n•PRI:  No P waves associated with PVCs\n•QRS: <0.12 seconds or more, wide and bizzare (I like \nto say… ugly!) A PVC has no preceding atrial P wave responsible for that ventricular beat. Start by naming the underlying rhythm, then describe the ventricular ectopy. The lecturer's visual shorthand was an 'ugly' QRS: it looks different from the patient's usual complexes. Use measurable width, timing and morphology rather than the adjective alone. !The printed QRS inequality is wrong. The typical PVC described here is wide, usually 0.12 seconds or greater; the slide's less-than sign should not be memorized. [2]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">37:31-38:08 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=60\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-059.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 59 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-059.webp\" alt=\"Slide 59: Preventricular Contractions (PVCs)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>A PVC has no preceding atrial P wave responsible for that ventricular beat. Start by naming the underlying rhythm, then describe the ventricular ectopy.</p><p>The lecturer&#x27;s visual shorthand was an &#x27;ugly&#x27; QRS: it looks different from the patient&#x27;s usual complexes. Use measurable width, timing and morphology rather than the adjective alone.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The printed QRS inequality is wrong. The typical PVC described here is wide, usually 0.12 seconds or greater; the slide&#x27;s less-than sign should not be memorized. [2]</p>"},{"block":60,"title":"Slide 60 · Preventricular Contractions (PVCs)","text":"Slide 60 Preventricular Contractions (PVCs) Preventricular Contractions (PVCs) Follow the normal P-QRS complexes, identify the early broad complex with no preceding P responsible for it, then observe the return to the baseline rhythm. The surrounding R-R intervals may also vary; do not let that distract from identifying the individual PVC. The underlying rhythm and the ectopic beat are separate parts of the description.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">38:08-38:36 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=61\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-060.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 60 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-060.webp\" alt=\"Slide 60: Preventricular Contractions (PVCs)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Follow the normal P-QRS complexes, identify the early broad complex with no preceding P responsible for it, then observe the return to the baseline rhythm.</p><p>The surrounding R-R intervals may also vary; do not let that distract from identifying the individual PVC. The underlying rhythm and the ectopic beat are separate parts of the description.</p>"},{"block":61,"title":"Slide 61 · Preventricular Contractions (PVCs)","text":"Slide 61 Preventricular Contractions (PVCs) Preventricular Contractions (PVCs) The lecturer contrasted a tracing with occasional PVCs with one containing several PVCs in a short sample, which she described as frequent. Use the description 'sinus rhythm with PVCs' when appropriate, then qualify frequency and pattern. A short ECG gives an impression; a Holter provides the actual burden over the monitoring period.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">38:36-39:57 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=62\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-061.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 61 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-061.webp\" alt=\"Slide 61: Preventricular Contractions (PVCs)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The lecturer contrasted a tracing with occasional PVCs with one containing several PVCs in a short sample, which she described as frequent.</p><p>Use the description &#x27;sinus rhythm with PVCs&#x27; when appropriate, then qualify frequency and pattern. A short ECG gives an impression; a Holter provides the actual burden over the monitoring period.</p>"},{"block":62,"title":"Slide 62 · Copyrights apply","text":"Slide 62 Copyrights apply Copyrights apply This reference table groups causes of palpitations. The lecturer used it to reinforce that the symptom has a broad differential, including cardiac, systemic, psychiatric, medication and substance-related causes. Use the history to narrow those categories rather than ordering the same investigations for every patient.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">39:57-40:15 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=63\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-062.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 62 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-062.webp\" alt=\"Slide 62: Copyrights apply\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>This reference table groups causes of palpitations. The lecturer used it to reinforce that the symptom has a broad differential, including cardiac, systemic, psychiatric, medication and substance-related causes.</p><p>Use the history to narrow those categories rather than ordering the same investigations for every patient.</p>"},{"block":63,"title":"Slide 63 · Common Causes of PVCs","text":"Slide 63 Common Causes of PVCs Common Causes of PVCs\n• Cardiac Causes\n• Ischemic heart disease – e.g., prior myocardial \ninfarction or active ischemia\n• Cardiomyopathy – dilated or hypertrophic \ncardiomyopathy\n• Heart failure – structural remodeling increases \nirritability\n• Valvular heart disease – especially aortic or \nmitral valve\n• Myocarditis – inflammation increases \nventricular excitability\n• Electrolyte and Metabolic Disturbances\n• Hypokalemia or hypomagnesemia – lower \nthreshold for ectopic activity\n• Hypercalcemia – can increase myocardial \nexcitability\n• Thyrotoxicosis (hyperthyroidism) – increases \nsympathetic tone\n• Hypoxia or acidosis –  respiratory failure or \nshock\n• Stimulants and Substances\n• Caffeine – excessive intake can trigger PVCs\n• Nicotine – sympathetic stimulation\n• Alcohol – especially binge drinking \n• Illicit drugs – cocaine, amphetamines\n• Certain medications – digoxin toxicity, some \nsympathomimetics, theophylline\n• Autonomic or Lifestyle Factors\n• Stress or anxiety – elevated catecholamines \nincrease ectopy\n• Physical exertion – particularly if excessive or \nsudden\n• Sleep deprivation – may increase ectopic \nactivity\n• Postoperative state – especially after cardiac \nsurgery\n• Idiopathic (Benign) PVCs\n• Occur in otherwise healthy individuals with no \nidentifiable cause\n• Often originate from the right ventricular \noutflow tract (RVOT)\n• Typically benign but may require monitoring if \nfrequent\n• Clinical note:\n• Occasional PVCs are usually benign and \nasymptomatic.\n• Frequent PVCs (>10% of total beats) or \nsymptomatic PVCs (palpitations, presyncope) \nmay warrant evaluation for underlying disease \nor treatment.\n• PVCs in the setting of structural heart disease \nor reduced ejection fraction carry a higher risk \nof malignant arrhythmias Consider structural or ischemic disease, cardiomyopathy, heart failure, valve disease and myocardial inflammation. Also review potassium and magnesium, calcium, thyroid function when indicated, hypoxia and acid-base disturbance. Ask about caffeine, nicotine, alcohol, stimulants, relevant medications, stress, sleep and exertion. Some PVCs are idiopathic, with no identified underlying disease. A PVC burden above about 10% on Holter was the lecturer's trigger for further assessment, commonly beginning with echocardiography. PVCs in a heart with prior infarction, reduced function or structural disease carry greater concern for dangerous ventricular arrhythmias.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">39:57-43:16 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=64\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-063.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 63 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-063.webp\" alt=\"Slide 63: Common Causes of PVCs\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Consider structural or ischemic disease, cardiomyopathy, heart failure, valve disease and myocardial inflammation. Also review potassium and magnesium, calcium, thyroid function when indicated, hypoxia and acid-base disturbance.</p><p>Ask about caffeine, nicotine, alcohol, stimulants, relevant medications, stress, sleep and exertion. Some PVCs are idiopathic, with no identified underlying disease.</p><p>A PVC burden above about 10% on Holter was the lecturer&#x27;s trigger for further assessment, commonly beginning with echocardiography. PVCs in a heart with prior infarction, reduced function or structural disease carry greater concern for dangerous ventricular arrhythmias.</p>"},{"block":64,"title":"Slide 64 · Palpitations","text":"Slide 64 Palpitations Palpitations\nHistory Taking\n•\nAge of onset\n•\nDuration of palpitations\n•\nHeart rate and rhythm regularity\n•\nAdditional descriptive sensations - \nrapid fluttering,  regularity or \nirregularity,  sensation of \"flip-\nflopping\" in the chest, pounding, or \nvery strong heart beat followed by \n\"stopping\" sensation, irregular, \npounding feeling in the neck \n•\nAssociated presyncope or syncope\n•\nAbruptness of palpitation onset and \nresolution\n•\nPatient self-termination of \npalpitations\n•\nEffect of positional changes\n•\nTemporal association with exercise or \nemotional stress\n• Personal and family history of cardiac \ndisease\n• MVP\n• Structural heart disease\n• Hx of cardiac conduction system \ndisease\n• Coexisting medical conditions\n• High-output cardiac state\n• Pregnancy related\n• Metabolic and endocrine causes\n• Hypoglycemia\n• Hyperthyroiodism\n• Pheochromocytoma\n• Chronic obstructive pulmonary \ndisease\n• Coexisting psychiatric disorders\n• Medications \n• Caffeine, nicotine, and other substance \nuse Characterize the event: onset, duration, frequency, abrupt start or stop, perceived rate and regularity, position, exertion and emotional stress. Ask directly about presyncope or actual syncope. Review personal and family cardiac history and all comorbid conditions. Poorly controlled disease, medication changes or difficulty following a treatment plan may explain a change in symptoms. Include prescribed drugs, OTC products, caffeine, nicotine, alcohol and other substances. The lecturer emphasized a robust history because a brief, normal examination may miss intermittent rhythm events.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">43:16-44:41 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=65\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-064.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 64 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-064.webp\" alt=\"Slide 64: Palpitations\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Characterize the event: onset, duration, frequency, abrupt start or stop, perceived rate and regularity, position, exertion and emotional stress. Ask directly about presyncope or actual syncope.</p><p>Review personal and family cardiac history and all comorbid conditions. Poorly controlled disease, medication changes or difficulty following a treatment plan may explain a change in symptoms.</p><p>Include prescribed drugs, OTC products, caffeine, nicotine, alcohol and other substances. The lecturer emphasized a robust history because a brief, normal examination may miss intermittent rhythm events.</p>"},{"block":65,"title":"Slide 65 · What Kind Of PVCs Should I Worry About?","text":"Slide 65 What Kind Of PVCs Should I Worry About? What Kind Of PVCs Should I Worry About?\nFeature\nBenign PVCs\nPathologic PVCs\nPatient profile\nOften healthy, no known cardiac \ndisease\nKnown heart disease (e.g., MI, \ncardiomyopathy)\nSymptoms\nUsually asymptomatic or mild \npalpitations\nPalpitations with syncope, \ndizziness, or presyncope\nOccurrence\nOccasional or infrequent (<10% of \ntotal beats)\nFrequent (>10–20% of beats) or in \nruns\nTriggers\nOften associated with caffeine, \nstress, stimulants, or exercise\nOccur at rest or during sleep, \nindependent of triggers\nPhysical exam\nNormal heart sounds, no signs of \nheart failure\nMay show signs of heart failure or \nstructural disease\nResponse to activity\nOften decrease or disappear with \nexercise\nMay persist or worsen with \nexertion\nIf PVC burden >10% on a 24-hour Holter, consider further evaluation \n(echocardiogram, stress test, etc.). Known cardiac disease, syncope or presyncope, more frequent ectopy, and impaired heart function should increase concern. An otherwise healthy person with occasional, well-tolerated symptoms offers more room for a staged outpatient assessment. Quantify burden rather than relying only on how dramatic the sensation sounds. The lecturer recommended an echo as an early next investigation when Holter PVC burden exceeds 10%, to assess cardiac structure and function. Read the table as a set of risk clues, not as a guarantee that a particular trigger or timing makes PVCs benign.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">44:41-45:44 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=66\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-065.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 65 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-065.webp\" alt=\"Slide 65: What Kind Of PVCs Should I Worry About?\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Known cardiac disease, syncope or presyncope, more frequent ectopy, and impaired heart function should increase concern. An otherwise healthy person with occasional, well-tolerated symptoms offers more room for a staged outpatient assessment.</p><p>Quantify burden rather than relying only on how dramatic the sensation sounds. The lecturer recommended an echo as an early next investigation when Holter PVC burden exceeds 10%, to assess cardiac structure and function.</p><p>Read the table as a set of risk clues, not as a guarantee that a particular trigger or timing makes PVCs benign.</p>"},{"block":66,"title":"Slide 66 · Palpitations","text":"Slide 66 Palpitations Palpitations\nPhysical Exam\n• Focused physical exam\n• Vital signs\n• Cardiovascular exam \n• Close attention to presence of cardiac murmurs\n• Findings of heart failure \n• Displaced PMI\n• Peipheral edema\n• Elevated JVP, +ve HJR\n• Presence of an S3\n• Respiratory exam\n• Think COPD… prolonged expiratory phase, wheezing, decreased a/e\n• General\n• Thyroid disease – hyper\n• Sweating \n• Tremor\n• Exopthalmos\n• Risk tendon relfexes Perform a comprehensive cardiac examination, including careful assessment for a murmur and signs of heart failure. The lecturer highlighted the aortic and mitral areas, JVP, hepatojugular reflux and S3 in addition to the usual examination. Assess the lungs and consider respiratory disease or hypoxia as contributors. Look for hyperthyroid features such as sweating, tremor, agitation, eye signs and brisk reflexes. The lecturer explicitly corrected the slide wording: it should say 'brisk tendon reflexes.' Combine signs with the history rather than diagnosing a cause from one finding.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">45:44-48:16 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=67\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-066.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 66 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-066.webp\" alt=\"Slide 66: Palpitations\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Perform a comprehensive cardiac examination, including careful assessment for a murmur and signs of heart failure. The lecturer highlighted the aortic and mitral areas, JVP, hepatojugular reflux and S3 in addition to the usual examination.</p><p>Assess the lungs and consider respiratory disease or hypoxia as contributors. Look for hyperthyroid features such as sweating, tremor, agitation, eye signs and brisk reflexes.</p><p>The lecturer explicitly corrected the slide wording: it should say &#x27;brisk tendon reflexes.&#x27; Combine signs with the history rather than diagnosing a cause from one finding.</p>"},{"block":67,"title":"Slide 67 · Palpitations","text":"Slide 67 Palpitations Palpitations\nLaboratory and Diagnostics\n• ECG\n• Echocardiogram (if appropriate, see next slide)\n• Limited laboratory testing – based on history or physical exam \nfindings\n• rule out anemia and hyperthyroidism\n• history or examination suggests substance use or withdrawal, perform \ntoxicology testing Begin with ECG, recognizing that a 10-second sample often misses an intermittent symptom. Daily or near-daily events are a reason to consider Holter monitoring. Laboratory testing should answer questions raised by the history and examination, such as anemia or hyperthyroidism; it is not automatically an extensive panel. Consider toxicology only when clinically indicated. Student question: how do you discuss substance testing? Explain the possible causes, why the test might help and what the patient agrees to. If they decline, document that the cause remains unassessed and continue evaluating alternatives. Refusal is not evidence that substances caused the symptoms.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">48:16-51:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=68\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-067.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 67 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-067.webp\" alt=\"Slide 67: Palpitations\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and discussion</h3><p>Begin with ECG, recognizing that a 10-second sample often misses an intermittent symptom. Daily or near-daily events are a reason to consider Holter monitoring.</p><p>Laboratory testing should answer questions raised by the history and examination, such as anemia or hyperthyroidism; it is not automatically an extensive panel. Consider toxicology only when clinically indicated.</p><p>Student question: how do you discuss substance testing? Explain the possible causes, why the test might help and what the patient agrees to. If they decline, document that the cause remains unassessed and continue evaluating alternatives. Refusal is not evidence that substances caused the symptoms.</p>"},{"block":68,"title":"Slide 68 · Palpitations","text":"Slide 68 Palpitations Palpitations\n• Echocardiogram in persons whose history, physical examination, or ECG raises concern for \nstructural heart disease\n• family history, symptoms, or ECG concerning for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy\n• ECG with Q waves suggesting prior myocardial infarction, a left bundle branch block, or changes consistent \nwith ventricular hypertrophy\n• murmur on physical examination that suggests valvular heart disease (i.e. aortic stenosis or mitral \nregurgitation)\n• Patients at low risk for concerning arrhythmias\n• palpitations are unsustained and well tolerated and who do not have evidence of heart disease by history, \nphysical examination, or 12-lead ECG\n• generally do not obtain ambulatory rhythm monitoring\n• counsel the patient regarding the low risk of a serious cardiac arrhythmia and the limited utility of further \ntesting\n• if the patient remains very concerned about their symptoms despite counseling, we perform two weeks of \nevent monitoring\n• Patients at high risk for concerning arrhythmias\n• ambulatory cardiac rhythm monitoring for all persons whose initial diagnostic evaluation suggests a high \nrisk for concerning arrhythmias, specifically, a disorder that is associated with the development of \nventricular tachycardia \n• palpitations that are sustained, poorly tolerated, or associated with syncope or presyncope\n• organic heart disease (eg, scar formation from myocardial infarction, dilated cardiomyopathy of any cause, \nclinically significant valvular heart disease, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy)\n• personal or family history of arrhythmia, syncope, sudden death, cardiomyopathy, or long QT syndrome\n• patients with frequent symptoms and associated syncope often benefit from expedited evaluation in an \ninpatient setting. For Julie, choose the next steps according to symptom frequency and risk. The lecturer favored Holter monitoring for daily symptoms, quantifying PVC burden, and echo when structural disease is suspected or burden is significant. Reduce nicotine and excessive caffeine while investigating appropriately. Complete reasonable primary-care investigations before a routine cardiology referral when the presentation allows; urgent risk features change that approach. Reassuring, infrequent, well-tolerated symptoms may need less testing. Ask about family sudden death, including death during sleep, abrupt unexplained collapse, or unexpected drowning in a competent swimmer. These stories may reveal an inherited arrhythmia risk that 'family history of heart disease' alone misses.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">52:31-57:41 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=69\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-068.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 68 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-068.webp\" alt=\"Slide 68: Palpitations\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and case discussion</h3><p>For Julie, choose the next steps according to symptom frequency and risk. The lecturer favored Holter monitoring for daily symptoms, quantifying PVC burden, and echo when structural disease is suspected or burden is significant. Reduce nicotine and excessive caffeine while investigating appropriately.</p><p>Complete reasonable primary-care investigations before a routine cardiology referral when the presentation allows; urgent risk features change that approach. Reassuring, infrequent, well-tolerated symptoms may need less testing.</p><p>Ask about family sudden death, including death during sleep, abrupt unexplained collapse, or unexpected drowning in a competent swimmer. These stories may reveal an inherited arrhythmia risk that &#x27;family history of heart disease&#x27; alone misses.</p>"},{"block":69,"title":"Slide 69 · Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB)","text":"Slide 69 Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB) Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB)\n• Normal cardiac conduction down both anterior and posterior left \nfascicles of the His-Purkinje system is compromised\n• LBBB is often associated with significant heart disease it can also be \nseen in patients without any clinical disease\n• i.e. CAD, HTN, valvular heart disease, cardiomyopathy (dilated), ACS, \nmyocarditis, medication\n• In isolation the presence of LBBB does not lend itself to any specific \nclinical concern, nor does it affect prognosis\n• In the proper clinical context, LBBB can of great consequence and \nimportance, especially in patients presenting with chest pain, \npre/syncope and heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF)\n• New onset LBBB in the proper setting of concerning clinical \nsymptoms should always be considered a sign of pathology \n• Patients with a new LBBB require cardiac evaluation A new LBBB warrants further cardiac evaluation. It can accompany coronary disease, hypertension, valvular disease, cardiomyopathy, myocarditis or other pathology, even before overt heart-failure symptoms appear. The lecturer emphasized context: new LBBB with chest pain, presyncope or syncope, or heart-failure features is especially concerning. Do not dismiss an abnormal conduction finding because the patient currently looks comfortable. Use it as a reason to investigate possible underlying disease, not as a stand-alone diagnosis of the cause.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">57:41-01:00:00 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=70\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-069.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 69 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-069.webp\" alt=\"Slide 69: Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>A new LBBB warrants further cardiac evaluation. It can accompany coronary disease, hypertension, valvular disease, cardiomyopathy, myocarditis or other pathology, even before overt heart-failure symptoms appear.</p><p>The lecturer emphasized context: new LBBB with chest pain, presyncope or syncope, or heart-failure features is especially concerning. Do not dismiss an abnormal conduction finding because the patient currently looks comfortable.</p><p>Use it as a reason to investigate possible underlying disease, not as a stand-alone diagnosis of the cause.</p>"},{"block":70,"title":"Slide 70 · • QRS duration greater than 120 milliseconds","text":"Slide 70 • QRS duration greater than 120 milliseconds • QRS duration greater than 120 milliseconds\n• Absence of Q wave in leads I, V5 and V6\n• Monomorphic R wave in I, V5 and V6\n• ST and T wave displacement opposite to the major deflection of the \nQRS complex The slide lists a broad QRS, absent lateral Q waves, characteristic lateral R-wave morphology and secondary ST-T changes as LBBB features. The recording concentrated on the importance of a new LBBB rather than walking through this tracing criterion by criterion. !A new LBBB alone is not a STEMI equivalent. Suspected ischemia in this setting requires assessment of symptoms and appropriate ECG criteria and urgent pathways. [5]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">57:41-01:00:00 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=71\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-070.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 70 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-070.webp\" alt=\"Slide 70: • QRS duration greater than 120 milliseconds\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The slide lists a broad QRS, absent lateral Q waves, characteristic lateral R-wave morphology and secondary ST-T changes as LBBB features.</p><p>The recording concentrated on the importance of a new LBBB rather than walking through this tracing criterion by criterion.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> A new LBBB alone is not a STEMI equivalent. Suspected ischemia in this setting requires assessment of symptoms and appropriate ECG criteria and urgent pathways. [5]</p>"},{"block":71,"title":"Slide 71 · The Atrial Rhythms","text":"Slide 71 The Atrial Rhythms The Atrial Rhythms The lecture now moves from sinus rhythms and premature beats to atrial fibrillation and atrial flutter. AF was the main focus because it is common and frequently encountered in primary care. Keep rhythm recognition, symptom burden, structural assessment and stroke prevention as separate parts of the evaluation.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:00:00-01:01:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=72\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-071.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 71 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-071.webp\" alt=\"Slide 71: The Atrial Rhythms\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The lecture now moves from sinus rhythms and premature beats to atrial fibrillation and atrial flutter. AF was the main focus because it is common and frequently encountered in primary care.</p><p>Keep rhythm recognition, symptom burden, structural assessment and stroke prevention as separate parts of the evaluation.</p>"},{"block":72,"title":"Slide 72 · Atrial Fibrillation (AF)","text":"Slide 72 Atrial Fibrillation (AF) Atrial Fibrillation (AF)\n• Atrial fibrillation (AF) is the most commonly treated cardiac arrhythmia\n• State of disorganized electrical activity from multiple locations within the \natria\n• Rapid atria depolarizations are not uniform\n• No discernible P waves\n• Small irregularly shaped fibrillatory (F waves) are seen\n• Ventricular response is irregular\n• Hemodynamic status may become compromised due to loss of “atrial kick”\n• Contributes about 30% of cardiac output\n• Decreased ventricular filling time\n• Less filling time means less blood will be ejected during \nventricular systole and less will be sent through the circulation In AF, disorganized atrial activation replaces the coordinated sinus impulse. Multiple electrical signals reach the AV node; variable conduction through that node produces an irregular ventricular response. The lecturer described the AV node as a gatekeeper that cannot pass every impulse through. There are no discrete sinus P waves. Instead, the baseline may show fibrillatory activity, with irregular R-R intervals. Loss of coordinated atrial contraction removes the atrial contribution to ventricular filling, or atrial kick. Combined with a fast rate and less filling time, this can reduce output, particularly in an already impaired heart. The lecture cited an atrial contribution of up to roughly 30%.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:01:17-01:06:04 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=73\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-072.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 72 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-072.webp\" alt=\"Slide 72: Atrial Fibrillation (AF)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>In AF, disorganized atrial activation replaces the coordinated sinus impulse. Multiple electrical signals reach the AV node; variable conduction through that node produces an irregular ventricular response. The lecturer described the AV node as a gatekeeper that cannot pass every impulse through.</p><p>There are no discrete sinus P waves. Instead, the baseline may show fibrillatory activity, with irregular R-R intervals.</p><p>Loss of coordinated atrial contraction removes the atrial contribution to ventricular filling, or atrial kick. Combined with a fast rate and less filling time, this can reduce output, particularly in an already impaired heart. The lecture cited an atrial contribution of up to roughly 30%.</p>"},{"block":73,"title":"Slide 73 · Atrial Fibrillation","text":"Slide 73 Atrial Fibrillation Atrial Fibrillation\n• Paroxysmal (self-terminating or \nintermittent) AF\n• terminates spontaneously or with \nintervention within seven days of onset\n• episodes may recur with variable \nfrequency\n• Persistent AF\n• fails to self-terminate within seven days\n• episodes often require pharmacologic or \nelectrical cardioversion to restore sinus \nrhythm\n• a patient who has had persistent AF can \nhave later episodes of paroxysmal AF, AF \nis generally considered a progressive \ndisease.\n• Long-standing persistent AF\n• refers to AF that has lasted for more than \n12 months\n• Permanent AF\n• term used to identify persistent AF for \nwhich a joint decision by the patient and \nclinician has been made to no longer \npursue a rhythm control strategy Paroxysmal AF ends within seven days; persistent AF lasts beyond seven days and may require an intervention to restore sinus rhythm. Symptoms vary greatly: some people can identify the exact onset and end, while others do not know they are in AF. Classification concerns duration and the ongoing treatment strategy, not a different ECG appearance. Some patients benefit from restoring sinus rhythm; in others, the patient and clinician decide to stop pursuing it. !AF lasting more than 12 months is long-standing persistent AF. Duration alone does not make it permanent. Permanent AF reflects the shared decision to accept the rhythm, as the slide itself explains.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:06:04-01:08:48 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=74\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-073.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 73 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-073.webp\" alt=\"Slide 73: Atrial Fibrillation\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Paroxysmal AF ends within seven days; persistent AF lasts beyond seven days and may require an intervention to restore sinus rhythm. Symptoms vary greatly: some people can identify the exact onset and end, while others do not know they are in AF.</p><p>Classification concerns duration and the ongoing treatment strategy, not a different ECG appearance. Some patients benefit from restoring sinus rhythm; in others, the patient and clinician decide to stop pursuing it.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> AF lasting more than 12 months is long-standing persistent AF. Duration alone does not make it permanent. Permanent AF reflects the shared decision to accept the rhythm, as the slide itself explains.</p>"},{"block":74,"title":"Slide 74 · Atrial Fibrillation","text":"Slide 74 Atrial Fibrillation Atrial Fibrillation\n•Rate: \n• Atrial rate: cannot be measured (400-700)\n• Ventricular rate: varies with number of impulses \nconducted through AV node (60-200)\n•Rhythm: Unable to measure atrial, ventricular is \nirregular\n•P Waves: Atria are fibrillating, no P waves are \nproduced\n•PRI: No P waves\n•QRS: <0.12 seconds Recognize AF by absent discrete P waves and an irregular ventricular rhythm. A PR interval cannot be measured when there are no identifiable P waves. The atrial activity is too rapid and disorganized to count conventionally; the slide gives 400-700/min. Ventricular rate depends on AV conduction and can be controlled or rapid. The lecturer used over about 110/min as a practical rapid-response discussion point. Describe the ventricular rate separately from the rhythm diagnosis.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:08:48-01:09:52 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=75\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-074.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 74 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-074.webp\" alt=\"Slide 74: Atrial Fibrillation\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Recognize AF by absent discrete P waves and an irregular ventricular rhythm. A PR interval cannot be measured when there are no identifiable P waves.</p><p>The atrial activity is too rapid and disorganized to count conventionally; the slide gives 400-700/min. Ventricular rate depends on AV conduction and can be controlled or rapid. The lecturer used over about 110/min as a practical rapid-response discussion point.</p><p>Describe the ventricular rate separately from the rhythm diagnosis.</p>"},{"block":75,"title":"Slide 75 · Atrial Fibrillation","text":"Slide 75 Atrial Fibrillation Atrial Fibrillation Compare organized sinus activity with the AF tracing. Look for the loss of discrete P waves and the uneven R-R intervals. Do not confuse a noisy-looking baseline alone with AF. The lecturer used the combination of atrial activity and ventricular irregularity to explain the pattern.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:09:52-01:10:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=76\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-075.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 75 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-075.webp\" alt=\"Slide 75: Atrial Fibrillation\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Compare organized sinus activity with the AF tracing. Look for the loss of discrete P waves and the uneven R-R intervals.</p><p>Do not confuse a noisy-looking baseline alone with AF. The lecturer used the combination of atrial activity and ventricular irregularity to explain the pattern.</p>"},{"block":76,"title":"Slide 76 · Atrial Fibrillation","text":"Slide 76 Atrial Fibrillation Atrial Fibrillation This second example demonstrates the same AF pattern at a faster ventricular rate. Changing the speed does not change the underlying rhythm diagnosis. Use the tracing to practice separating the questions 'what rhythm is this?' and 'how fast are the ventricles responding?' Then consider how well the patient is tolerating it.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:10:23-01:11:02 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=77\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-076.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 76 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-076.webp\" alt=\"Slide 76: Atrial Fibrillation\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>This second example demonstrates the same AF pattern at a faster ventricular rate. Changing the speed does not change the underlying rhythm diagnosis.</p><p>Use the tracing to practice separating the questions &#x27;what rhythm is this?&#x27; and &#x27;how fast are the ventricles responding?&#x27; Then consider how well the patient is tolerating it.</p>"},{"block":77,"title":"Slide 77 · Atrial Flutter","text":"Slide 77 Atrial Flutter Atrial Flutter\n• Characterized by an atrial rate of 250 to 350 beats/minute (generally \naround 300 beats/minute)\n• Originates from a single atrial focus\n• On an ECG, the P waves lose their distinction due to the rapid atrial \nrate\n• P waves blend together in a saw-toothed appearance and are called \nflutter waves, or f waves… these waves are the hallmark of atrial \nflutter \n• Not all flutter waves are conducted\n• Ventricular rate depends on the number of flutter waves that are \nblocked\n• Ventricular response is regular (block can be variable, resulting in \nirregular ventricular response)\n• 2:1/ 3:1 ratio\n• PRI not measured Flutter is more organized than AF. Its rapid atrial activity creates a repeating sawtooth pattern rather than discrete normal sinus P waves. Only some atrial impulses conduct through the AV node, so the ventricular rate depends on the conduction ratio. A fixed ratio can produce a regular ventricular rhythm; a changing ratio can make it irregular. !The 'single focus' description is a teaching simplification. Typical atrial flutter is an organized macroreentrant circuit; the central recognition feature here is the repeating flutter-wave pattern. [4]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:11:02-01:12:30 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=78\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-077.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 77 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-077.webp\" alt=\"Slide 77: Atrial Flutter\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Flutter is more organized than AF. Its rapid atrial activity creates a repeating sawtooth pattern rather than discrete normal sinus P waves.</p><p>Only some atrial impulses conduct through the AV node, so the ventricular rate depends on the conduction ratio. A fixed ratio can produce a regular ventricular rhythm; a changing ratio can make it irregular.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The &#x27;single focus&#x27; description is a teaching simplification. Typical atrial flutter is an organized macroreentrant circuit; the central recognition feature here is the repeating flutter-wave pattern. [4]</p>"},{"block":78,"title":"Slide 78 · Atrial Flutter","text":"Slide 78 Atrial Flutter Atrial Flutter The lecturer identified a 2:1 flutter example: two atrial flutter waves for each conducted ventricular complex. Count the atrial waves and QRS complexes rather than treating every visible deflection as a separate ventricular beat. With a stable conduction ratio, the ventricular response can be quite regular despite the rapid atrial rate.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:12:30-01:13:01 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=79\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-078.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 78 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-078.webp\" alt=\"Slide 78: Atrial Flutter\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The lecturer identified a 2:1 flutter example: two atrial flutter waves for each conducted ventricular complex. Count the atrial waves and QRS complexes rather than treating every visible deflection as a separate ventricular beat.</p><p>With a stable conduction ratio, the ventricular response can be quite regular despite the rapid atrial rate.</p>"},{"block":79,"title":"Slide 79 · Atrial Flutter","text":"Slide 79 Atrial Flutter Atrial Flutter Variable conduction means the AV node does not pass the same proportion of flutter waves each time. The ventricular intervals can therefore vary. The repeating organized flutter waves distinguish this example from the disorganized atrial baseline in AF. An irregular ventricular rhythm is not, by itself, enough to call the tracing AF.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:13:01-01:13:31 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=80\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-079.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 79 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-079.webp\" alt=\"Slide 79: Atrial Flutter\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Variable conduction means the AV node does not pass the same proportion of flutter waves each time. The ventricular intervals can therefore vary.</p><p>The repeating organized flutter waves distinguish this example from the disorganized atrial baseline in AF. An irregular ventricular rhythm is not, by itself, enough to call the tracing AF.</p>"},{"block":80,"title":"Slide 80 · Case Study","text":"Slide 80 Case Study Case Study\n• A 56-year-old man presents to your clinic with atrial \nfibrillation\n• He has been to ER 3 times in the last year with palpitations, \nrequiring cardioversions\n• He also has hypertension, dyslipidemia, and his BMI is 35\n• Current medications include amlodipine, rosuvastatin, and \nbisoprolol 2.5 mg\n• He says over the last 3 days he has been having palpitations \nagain, but they aren't as severe as before. \n• Will assume based on case this patient is ‘new to me/this \nclinic’ The case is a 56-year-old man with recurrent AF, three ED cardioversions in the past year, hypertension, dyslipidemia and BMI 35. He takes amlodipine, rosuvastatin and low-dose bisoprolol, and reports three days of recurrent palpitations. Assume he is new to the clinic. Confirm what the current rhythm actually is, assess symptoms and stability, review previous investigations and treatment, and consider stroke prevention as well as rate or rhythm management.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:13:31-01:14:10 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-080.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 80 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-080.webp\" alt=\"Slide 80: Case Study\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and case discussion</h3><p>The case is a 56-year-old man with recurrent AF, three ED cardioversions in the past year, hypertension, dyslipidemia and BMI 35. He takes amlodipine, rosuvastatin and low-dose bisoprolol, and reports three days of recurrent palpitations.</p><p>Assume he is new to the clinic. Confirm what the current rhythm actually is, assess symptoms and stability, review previous investigations and treatment, and consider stroke prevention as well as rate or rhythm management.</p>"},{"block":81,"title":"Slide 81 · Should We Routinely Screen for AF?","text":"Slide 81 Should We Routinely Screen for AF? Should We Routinely Screen for AF? Distinguish population screening from opportunistic screening at an existing medical encounter. The lecturer advocated checking for AF in people aged 65 and older when they are already being seen. A pulse check, cardiac auscultation or an appropriate rhythm device may identify irregularity. If AF is suspected, obtain an ECG to confirm it. Patients may bring watch or single-lead-device information. Treat it as information to assess, rather than assuming every device alert is a definitive diagnosis. Keep screening during future encounters when no AF is confirmed and the context calls for it.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:14:10-01:17:47 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=82\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-081.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 81 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-081.webp\" alt=\"Slide 81: Should We Routinely Screen for AF?\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and discussion</h3><p>Distinguish population screening from opportunistic screening at an existing medical encounter. The lecturer advocated checking for AF in people aged 65 and older when they are already being seen.</p><p>A pulse check, cardiac auscultation or an appropriate rhythm device may identify irregularity. If AF is suspected, obtain an ECG to confirm it.</p><p>Patients may bring watch or single-lead-device information. Treat it as information to assess, rather than assuming every device alert is a definitive diagnosis. Keep screening during future encounters when no AF is confirmed and the context calls for it.</p>"},{"block":82,"title":"Slide 82 · Gathering More Information ","text":"Slide 82 Gathering More Information  Gathering More Information \n•History\n• What does this patient understand about his atrial fibrillation? \n• OPQRST, symptom specific questions (cardiac and respiratory) and \nROS of current episode\n• Atrial fibrillation history\n• date of first symptomatic attack and the date of first objective \nconfirmation\n• duration and frequency of episodes (e.g. the dominant pattern of AF)\n• presence and nature of symptoms related to AF\n• symptom severity (including impact on quality of life)\n• treatment or procedures; details on previous cardioversions\n• Past med/surg Hx, medications + OTC, herbals/vitamins, allergies\n• Family Hx\n• Social Hx – work, home life, social supports\n• Lifestyle Hx – alcohol, drugs (stimulants specifically), smoking, diet, \nstress, exercise, mood Ask what the patient understands about AF, when it first occurred and how it has been managed. Establish episode frequency, duration, symptom severity and effect on work, exercise and daily life. Use OPQRST plus directed cardiac and respiratory questions. Ask about prior cardioversions, medications, procedures, specialist assessment and what has or has not helped. Review usual medical, surgical, medication, OTC, family and social history. Explore recent changes in alcohol, stimulants, smoking, stress, sleep, diet or exercise, and whether the patient recognizes triggers.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:17:47-01:19:59 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=83\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-082.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 82 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-082.webp\" alt=\"Slide 82: Gathering More Information \"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Ask what the patient understands about AF, when it first occurred and how it has been managed. Establish episode frequency, duration, symptom severity and effect on work, exercise and daily life.</p><p>Use OPQRST plus directed cardiac and respiratory questions. Ask about prior cardioversions, medications, procedures, specialist assessment and what has or has not helped.</p><p>Review usual medical, surgical, medication, OTC, family and social history. Explore recent changes in alcohol, stimulants, smoking, stress, sleep, diet or exercise, and whether the patient recognizes triggers.</p>"},{"block":83,"title":"Slide 83 · Gathering More Information","text":"Slide 83 Gathering More Information Gathering More Information\n• Physical Exam\n• Vital signs and anthropometric measurements\n•\nMeasure blood pressure and heart rate, height, weight, BMI and waist circumference\n• Cardiovascular, respiratory exam\n• Comprehensive cardiac and respiratory examination with a focus on determination of the causes of AF\n• Differential Diagnoses\n• Arrythmia – AF, PVCs, PACs\n• Anxiety\n• Hyperthyroidism\n• Substance induced\n• Diagnostic Investigations\n• ECG\n• Document presence of AF; PR, QRS, and QT intervals (e.g. baseline prior to therapy initiation or to monitor \ntherapy)\n• Identify potential causes of AF (i.e. structural heart disease such as myocardial infarction, ventricular \nhypertrophy, atrial enlargement, congenital heart disease)\n• Identify factors that increase risk of adverse outcomes (i.e. conduction disturbances, sinus node dysfunction, \nor repolarization abnormalities)\n• Echocardiogram\n• Evaluate ventricular size, wall thickness, and function\n• Evaluate left atrial size and left atrial volume\n• Exclude significant valvular or congenital heart disease (e.g. atrial septal defect)\n• Labs\n• CBC, coagulation profile, electrolytes including calcium and magnesium, renal function, liver function, TSH, \nlipid profile, HbA1C Do not assume every new palpitation is the patient's known AF. Consider PACs/PVCs, another arrhythmia, thyroid disease, substance effects and anxiety. Confirm the rhythm with ECG and assess with a cardiac and respiratory examination. Echo assesses ventricular function and size, atrial size, and valve disease, particularly mitral stenosis. Obtain it when AF is newly diagnosed or structural information is needed. The lecturer described a patient unaware of sustained rates around 140-160/min until pulmonary edema developed; EF had fallen from normal to about 20%. The example illustrates possible tachycardia-related dysfunction despite few preceding symptoms. Tailor bloodwork to the assessment: CBC, electrolytes, renal function and TSH, with other baseline or risk-factor tests as indicated.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:19:59-01:26:39 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=84\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-083.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 83 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-083.webp\" alt=\"Slide 83: Gathering More Information\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and clinical example</h3><p>Do not assume every new palpitation is the patient&#x27;s known AF. Consider PACs/PVCs, another arrhythmia, thyroid disease, substance effects and anxiety. Confirm the rhythm with ECG and assess with a cardiac and respiratory examination.</p><p>Echo assesses ventricular function and size, atrial size, and valve disease, particularly mitral stenosis. Obtain it when AF is newly diagnosed or structural information is needed.</p><p>The lecturer described a patient unaware of sustained rates around 140-160/min until pulmonary edema developed; EF had fallen from normal to about 20%. The example illustrates possible tachycardia-related dysfunction despite few preceding symptoms.</p><p>Tailor bloodwork to the assessment: CBC, electrolytes, renal function and TSH, with other baseline or risk-factor tests as indicated.</p>"},{"block":84,"title":"Slide 84 · Common Signs and Symptoms of AF","text":"Slide 84 Common Signs and Symptoms of AF Common Signs and Symptoms of AF\n• Cardiovascular-related symptoms\n• Palpitations – sensation of a rapid, fluttering, or pounding heartbeat\n• Irregular pulse – often detected on physical exam (irregularly irregular rhythm)\n• Tachycardia – resting heart rate >100 bpm is common\n• Chest discomfort or pain – may occur, especially in those with underlying heart \ndisease\n• Fatigue or weakness – due to decreased cardiac output\n• Dizziness or lightheadedness – related to reduced cerebral perfusion\n• Syncope – rare, but possible if cardiac output drops significantly or during \nconversion pauses\n• Respiratory symptoms\n• Shortness of breath (dyspnea) – especially with exertion\n• Exercise intolerance – reduced ability to perform usual activities\n• Other possible findings\n• Anxiety – often associated with palpitations\n• Signs of heart failure – peripheral edema, orthopnea, or paroxysmal nocturnal \ndyspnea if AF is chronic or uncontrolled\n• Asymptomatic – up to 30–40% of patients may have no obvious symptoms and \nare diagnosed incidentally (e.g., during a routine ECG) Possible AF symptoms include palpitations, chest discomfort, fatigue, weakness, lightheadedness, dyspnea and reduced exercise tolerance. Syncope is uncommon and needs attention rather than being casually attributed to AF. Look for heart-failure features when the rate is uncontrolled or cardiac function is impaired. Some patients have anxiety associated with the sensation; others have no symptoms at all. The lecturer cited roughly 30-40% as potentially asymptomatic and stressed incidental detection during an examination or ECG for another reason.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:26:39-01:28:06 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=85\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-084.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 84 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-084.webp\" alt=\"Slide 84: Common Signs and Symptoms of AF\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Possible AF symptoms include palpitations, chest discomfort, fatigue, weakness, lightheadedness, dyspnea and reduced exercise tolerance. Syncope is uncommon and needs attention rather than being casually attributed to AF.</p><p>Look for heart-failure features when the rate is uncontrolled or cardiac function is impaired. Some patients have anxiety associated with the sensation; others have no symptoms at all.</p><p>The lecturer cited roughly 30-40% as potentially asymptomatic and stressed incidental detection during an examination or ECG for another reason.</p>"},{"block":85,"title":"Slide 85 · Assessing Atrial Fibrillation Burden","text":"Slide 85 Assessing Atrial Fibrillation Burden Assessing Atrial Fibrillation Burden\n• Severity of Atrial Fibrillation \nScore (SAF)\n• Type of atrial fibrillation\n•\nValvular AF refers to patients with moderate to severe mitral stenosis; these patients \nhave a higher risk of stroke than patients without this condition\n•\nEverything else is called Nonvalvular AF\n•\nAfter a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation, all patients should have an echocardiogram to \ndetermine if there are any underlying structural, valvular or functional reasons for the \natrial fibrillation The lecturer corrected the headings above the two tables: the left table describes AF type, while the right is the symptom-severity scale. The SAF score ranges from 0, asymptomatic, to 4, severe symptoms or major impact. Echo is important because significant mitral stenosis changes anticoagulant selection. The lecturer emphasized warfarin for AF with moderate-to-severe mitral stenosis; a DOAC may be used in eligible patients without that condition. Her stated preference for apixaban was a clinical preference, with twice-daily adherence noted as a consideration. !Mechanical heart valves also require warfarin; do not interpret 'everything else is nonvalvular' as permission to use a DOAC with a mechanical valve. [7]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:28:06-01:31:41 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=86\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-085.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 85 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-085.webp\" alt=\"Slide 85: Assessing Atrial Fibrillation Burden\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and discussion</h3><p>The lecturer corrected the headings above the two tables: the left table describes AF type, while the right is the symptom-severity scale. The SAF score ranges from 0, asymptomatic, to 4, severe symptoms or major impact.</p><p>Echo is important because significant mitral stenosis changes anticoagulant selection. The lecturer emphasized warfarin for AF with moderate-to-severe mitral stenosis; a DOAC may be used in eligible patients without that condition.</p><p>Her stated preference for apixaban was a clinical preference, with twice-daily adherence noted as a consideration.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> Mechanical heart valves also require warfarin; do not interpret &#x27;everything else is nonvalvular&#x27; as permission to use a DOAC with a mechanical valve. [7]</p>"},{"block":86,"title":"Slide 86 · Atrial Fibrillation","text":"Slide 86 Atrial Fibrillation Atrial Fibrillation\nEpidemiological Considerations\n• Risk of atrial fibrillation doubles with each decade of life\n• Male sex is associated with 1.5-fold increase in risk of AF\n• AF prevalence is 9.2% in females and 15% in males\n• AF incidence in:\n• Females: 1.6 per 1000 person-years\n• Males: 1.8 per 1000 person-years\n• Absolute number of patients with AF is higher in females than males The main epidemiological teaching was that AF becomes more likely with age and is more common in men. The lecturer described risk as increasing markedly with each decade. Student question: does a single event count? A documented episode matters, even if brief. Record the event and its circumstances; distinguish an isolated episode during an acute reversible illness from an ongoing pattern, and reassess rather than assuming recurrence is impossible. The cardioversion and anticoagulation questions raised during this interval are collected under slide 98, alongside the AF guideline reference.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:31:41-01:37:02 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=87\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-086.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 86 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-086.webp\" alt=\"Slide 86: Atrial Fibrillation\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and related questions</h3><p>The main epidemiological teaching was that AF becomes more likely with age and is more common in men. The lecturer described risk as increasing markedly with each decade.</p><p>Student question: does a single event count? A documented episode matters, even if brief. Record the event and its circumstances; distinguish an isolated episode during an acute reversible illness from an ongoing pattern, and reassess rather than assuming recurrence is impossible.</p><p>The cardioversion and anticoagulation questions raised during this interval are collected under slide 98, alongside the AF guideline reference.</p>"},{"block":87,"title":"Slide 87 · Risk Factor Analysis ","text":"Slide 87 Risk Factor Analysis  Risk Factor Analysis \n• Established Risk Factors:\n• Advancing age, male sex, hypertension, HFrEF, valvular heart disease (mitral \nstenosis), overt thyroid disease, OSA, obesity, excessive alcohol intake, \ncongenital heart disease\n• CCS guidelines also suggest new emerging and potential risk factors \nfor atrial fibrillation\n• i.e. COPD, HFpEF, CAD, CKD, DM, tobacco use, genetics, inflammation\n• Triggers for AF episodes\n• stimulants, alcohol, sleep deprivation, emotional stress, physical exertion, \nsleep/nocturnal, digestive\n• Reversible causes or AF secondary to\n• cardiac or non-cardiac surgery, acute cardiac pathology or pulmonary \npathology, acute infection, thyrotoxicosis, alcohol, pharmacologic agents (e.g. \nIbrutinib), supraventricular tachycardia, ventricular pacing Established factors include age, hypertension, obesity, sleep apnea, heart failure, valvular disease, thyroid disease, congenital disease and excess alcohol. The lecturer highlighted obesity and OSA as particularly important in practice. Ask about episode triggers such as stimulants, alcohol, sleep loss, stress and exertion. Consider AF secondary to surgery, acute infection, thyroid disease, acute cardiopulmonary illness or relevant drugs. Treating a reversible contributor can remove the immediate trigger. Follow-up is still needed to establish whether AF has recurred and to revisit longer-term management.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:37:02-01:39:16 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=88\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-087.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 87 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-087.webp\" alt=\"Slide 87: Risk Factor Analysis \"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Established factors include age, hypertension, obesity, sleep apnea, heart failure, valvular disease, thyroid disease, congenital disease and excess alcohol. The lecturer highlighted obesity and OSA as particularly important in practice.</p><p>Ask about episode triggers such as stimulants, alcohol, sleep loss, stress and exertion. Consider AF secondary to surgery, acute infection, thyroid disease, acute cardiopulmonary illness or relevant drugs.</p><p>Treating a reversible contributor can remove the immediate trigger. Follow-up is still needed to establish whether AF has recurred and to revisit longer-term management.</p>"},{"block":88,"title":"Slide 88 · Figure or report","text":"Slide 88 Figure or report  This CCS table separates established, emerging and potential AF risk markers. The lecturer recommended the society's AF pocket guide as a useful clinical reference. Use the table to broaden risk-factor review without treating every association as equally established or as a direct cause in an individual patient.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:39:16-01:39:46 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=89\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-088.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 88 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-088.webp\" alt=\"Slide 88: Figure or report\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>This CCS table separates established, emerging and potential AF risk markers. The lecturer recommended the society&#x27;s AF pocket guide as a useful clinical reference.</p><p>Use the table to broaden risk-factor review without treating every association as equally established or as a direct cause in an individual patient.</p>"},{"block":89,"title":"Slide 89 · Atrial Fibrillation and Stroke","text":"Slide 89 Atrial Fibrillation and Stroke Atrial Fibrillation and Stroke\n• left atrial appendage (LAA) is a \nunique substrate for stroke; \nelongated cul-de-sac lined with \nendothelium\n• contractility of the LAA is reduced \n• atrial thrombi almost always \noriginate in the LAA\n• thrombi have been strongly and \nconsistently linked to reduced \nblood flow velocities (via echo \ndoppler) in the LAA confirming the \nrole of stasis in their genesis Stroke was the major preventable AF consequence emphasized in the lecture. The left atrial appendage is a pouch where reduced contraction and slower blood movement can permit thrombus formation. If a thrombus embolizes, it can pass into the left ventricle, through the aorta and into the systemic circulation, including the cerebral circulation. This is why stroke-prevention assessment remains necessary even when palpitations are mild or the patient feels well.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:39:46-01:41:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=90\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-089.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 89 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-089.webp\" alt=\"Slide 89: Atrial Fibrillation and Stroke\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Stroke was the major preventable AF consequence emphasized in the lecture. The left atrial appendage is a pouch where reduced contraction and slower blood movement can permit thrombus formation.</p><p>If a thrombus embolizes, it can pass into the left ventricle, through the aorta and into the systemic circulation, including the cerebral circulation.</p><p>This is why stroke-prevention assessment remains necessary even when palpitations are mild or the patient feels well.</p>"},{"block":90,"title":"Slide 90 · Sex Differences In Risk Of Stroke In AF ","text":"Slide 90 Sex Differences In Risk Of Stroke In AF  Sex Differences In Risk Of Stroke In AF \nPatients\n• Females are at higher risk of AF-\ninduced stroke\n• Likely to be under treated with respect to \nanticoagulation\n• Less likely to be cardioverted back to normal \nsinus rhythm \n• Less likely to undergo catheter ablation \n• Severity of stroke is greater in \nfemales compared to males\n• Experiencing more functional disability\n• More likely to be institutionalized\n• 12% increase in relative risk of all-\ncause mortality amongst females \ncompared to males (relative risk, \n1.12; 95% CI, 1.07-1.17) \n• Female patients have greater residual \nrisk of stroke even if anticoagulated \nproperly The lecturer emphasized disparities affecting women with AF: undertreatment with anticoagulation, less access to cardioversion or ablation, and greater disability after AF-related stroke. The practical point was to review whether an individual patient has received an appropriate stroke-prevention discussion and access to treatment options, rather than allowing sex or age-related assumptions to limit care. The numerical comparisons on the slide were not explored in detail during the session.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:41:17-01:42:53 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=91\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-090.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 90 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-090.webp\" alt=\"Slide 90: Sex Differences In Risk Of Stroke In AF \"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The lecturer emphasized disparities affecting women with AF: undertreatment with anticoagulation, less access to cardioversion or ablation, and greater disability after AF-related stroke.</p><p>The practical point was to review whether an individual patient has received an appropriate stroke-prevention discussion and access to treatment options, rather than allowing sex or age-related assumptions to limit care.</p><p>The numerical comparisons on the slide were not explored in detail during the session.</p>"},{"block":91,"title":"Slide 91 · Risk Scoring in Atrial Fibrillation","text":"Slide 91 Risk Scoring in Atrial Fibrillation Risk Scoring in Atrial Fibrillation The Canadian CHADS-65 pathway starts with age: age 65 or older supports anticoagulation. Below 65, assess CHADS2 factors: prior stroke/TIA, hypertension, heart failure or diabetes. Any such factor supports anticoagulation in the pathway discussed. For younger patients without those factors, distinguish coexisting coronary or peripheral arterial disease from AF-related embolic risk. The diagram includes antiplatelet therapy in its vascular-disease branch. !Antiplatelet therapy in that branch addresses the vascular indication; aspirin is not a substitute for anticoagulation for AF-related stroke prevention. Patients under 65 without CHADS2 factors generally do not need AF-directed antithrombotic treatment. [3]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:42:53-01:44:59 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=92\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-091.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 91 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-091.webp\" alt=\"Slide 91: Risk Scoring in Atrial Fibrillation\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The Canadian CHADS-65 pathway starts with age: age 65 or older supports anticoagulation. Below 65, assess CHADS2 factors: prior stroke/TIA, hypertension, heart failure or diabetes. Any such factor supports anticoagulation in the pathway discussed.</p><p>For younger patients without those factors, distinguish coexisting coronary or peripheral arterial disease from AF-related embolic risk. The diagram includes antiplatelet therapy in its vascular-disease branch.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> Antiplatelet therapy in that branch addresses the vascular indication; aspirin is not a substitute for anticoagulation for AF-related stroke prevention. Patients under 65 without CHADS2 factors generally do not need AF-directed antithrombotic treatment. [3]</p>"},{"block":92,"title":"Slide 92 · Figure or report","text":"Slide 92 Figure or report  The lecturer uses CHADS-65 to decide whether anticoagulation is indicated and a validated numerical score when a patient wants a more detailed discussion of estimated annual stroke risk. CHADS2 and CHA2DS2-VASc have different components and age weightings, as shown in the tables. Use the actual tool rather than mixing the two. Risk estimates are aids to shared decision-making, alongside bleeding risk, comorbidities, preferences and treatment feasibility.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:44:59-01:45:37 and 01:50:15-01:51:47 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-092.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 92 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-092.webp\" alt=\"Slide 92: Figure or report\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and discussion</h3><p>The lecturer uses CHADS-65 to decide whether anticoagulation is indicated and a validated numerical score when a patient wants a more detailed discussion of estimated annual stroke risk.</p><p>CHADS2 and CHA2DS2-VASc have different components and age weightings, as shown in the tables. Use the actual tool rather than mixing the two.</p><p>Risk estimates are aids to shared decision-making, alongside bleeding risk, comorbidities, preferences and treatment feasibility.</p>"},{"block":93,"title":"Slide 93 · Stroke Risk ","text":"Slide 93 Stroke Risk  Stroke Risk \n• Discussion with patient regarding what \ninformation has been provided to him \nwith regards stroke risk and \nanticoagulation\n• Calculating his risk score using validated \ntools\n• Bleeding risk\n• Other questions to ask: drug plan? drug \ncost?\n• Calculating his risk score using validated \ntools\n• CHADS2 = 1 à OAC\n• CCS 65 à age less than 65 à HTN \nà OAC\n• Annual stroke risk 2.8%\n• Bleeding risk\n• HAS-BLED\n• ATRIA Bleeding Risk Score\n• Low risk patient The 56-year-old case patient has hypertension, so the lecturer calculated CHADS2 = 1 and found anticoagulation indicated through CHADS-65 despite age below 65. The slide gives an annual stroke estimate of 2.8% for the referenced scoring table. Discuss benefit, bleeding risk, drug cost, coverage and adherence. Present the information and recommendation, then establish what matters to the patient and what they will accept. The discussion contrasted the possibility of bleeding with potentially disabling stroke; avoid assuming a patient's priorities. The quoted percentage is a score-based cohort estimate, not a precise prediction for this individual.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:45:37-01:49:20 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=94\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-093.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 93 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-093.webp\" alt=\"Slide 93: Stroke Risk \"></a><h3>Lecture notes and case discussion</h3><p>The 56-year-old case patient has hypertension, so the lecturer calculated CHADS2 = 1 and found anticoagulation indicated through CHADS-65 despite age below 65. The slide gives an annual stroke estimate of 2.8% for the referenced scoring table.</p><p>Discuss benefit, bleeding risk, drug cost, coverage and adherence. Present the information and recommendation, then establish what matters to the patient and what they will accept.</p><p>The discussion contrasted the possibility of bleeding with potentially disabling stroke; avoid assuming a patient&#x27;s priorities. The quoted percentage is a score-based cohort estimate, not a precise prediction for this individual.</p>"},{"block":94,"title":"Slide 94 · Bleeding Risk Scoring Tools","text":"Slide 94 Bleeding Risk Scoring Tools Bleeding Risk Scoring Tools\n• HAS-BLED (Pisters et al, 2010)\n• Developed from the Euro Heart Survey on AF in 3,456 patients with atrial \nfibrillation followed for 12 months\n• 1 point for each of the following: hypertension, abnormal renal/liver \nfunction, stroke, bleeding history or predisposition, labile INR, elderly, or \ndrug/alcohol use concomitantly\n• Score - Low (0), Intermediate (1–2), High (3+) \n• ATRIA Risk Score (Fang et al, 2011) - Warfarin\n• Developed in 9186 atrial fibrillation patients enrolled in a large integrated \nhealthcare system and followed for 6 years\n• Anemia (3 points), severe renal disease (3 points), age ≥ 75 yrs (2 points), \nprior bleed (1 point), hypertension (1 point)\n• Scoring - Low (0–3), Intermediate (4), High (5–10) HAS-BLED and ATRIA were presented as tools to structure bleeding-risk assessment before and during anticoagulation decisions. Consider the actual risk factors and address those that can be modified. Discuss the difference between minor and major bleeding, relevant comorbidities, and the patient's concerns. A decision should integrate stroke risk, bleeding risk and preferences rather than rely on a score label alone. The lecturer did not work through each component of these two tools in the live recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:45:37-01:49:20 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=95\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-094.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 94 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-094.webp\" alt=\"Slide 94: Bleeding Risk Scoring Tools\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>HAS-BLED and ATRIA were presented as tools to structure bleeding-risk assessment before and during anticoagulation decisions. Consider the actual risk factors and address those that can be modified.</p><p>Discuss the difference between minor and major bleeding, relevant comorbidities, and the patient&#x27;s concerns. A decision should integrate stroke risk, bleeding risk and preferences rather than rely on a score label alone.</p><p>The lecturer did not work through each component of these two tools in the live recording.</p>"},{"block":95,"title":"Slide 95 · Rate Control Versus Rhythm Control Strategy","text":"Slide 95 Rate Control Versus Rhythm Control Strategy Rate Control Versus Rhythm Control Strategy\n1. Rate Control:\n• Primary goal: Control ventricular rate to relieve symptoms and prevent \ntachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy.\n• Preferred when:\n•\nPatients are minimally symptomatic or asymptomatic.\n•\nRhythm control is unlikely to maintain sinus rhythm (e.g., significant atrial remodeling or \nlongstanding persistent AF).\n•\nRisks of rhythm control (antiarrhythmic drugs, procedures) outweigh benefits.\n•\nAs a baseline strategy while assessing rhythm control candidacy.\n2. Rhythm Control:\n• Primary goal: Restore and maintain sinus rhythm to improve symptoms and \nquality of life.\n• Preferred when:\n•\nPatients are highly symptomatic despite adequate rate control.\n•\nThere is a need to improve left ventricular function (e.g., AF-induced cardiomyopathy).\n•\nIn early AF (short duration) where maintenance of sinus rhythm is more likely to succeed.\n•\nYounger patients or those with paroxysmal AF who may benefit from long-term sinus \nrhythm.\n•\nAs part of a broader strategy to reduce hospitalization and potentially improve outcomes, \nespecially post-ablation. Rate control aims to control ventricular response, reduce symptoms and avoid tachycardia-related dysfunction. Rhythm control aims to restore and maintain sinus rhythm; the preferred approach depends on symptoms, comorbidities and the likelihood of success. The lecturer described rate control and anticoagulation as work that can often be started and followed in primary care, within competence and the local clinical pathway. Refer for a rhythm-control strategy because antiarrhythmic selection and procedures can be more complex. Stroke prevention still requires assessment whichever rhythm strategy is chosen.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:49:20-01:50:15 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=96\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-095.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 95 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-095.webp\" alt=\"Slide 95: Rate Control Versus Rhythm Control Strategy\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Rate control aims to control ventricular response, reduce symptoms and avoid tachycardia-related dysfunction. Rhythm control aims to restore and maintain sinus rhythm; the preferred approach depends on symptoms, comorbidities and the likelihood of success.</p><p>The lecturer described rate control and anticoagulation as work that can often be started and followed in primary care, within competence and the local clinical pathway.</p><p>Refer for a rhythm-control strategy because antiarrhythmic selection and procedures can be more complex. Stroke prevention still requires assessment whichever rhythm strategy is chosen.</p>"},{"block":96,"title":"Slide 96 · Consultation or Referral","text":"Slide 96 Consultation or Referral Consultation or Referral\n• Cardiology\n• If available Electrophysiology/Arrhythmia specialist\n• Rhythm control strategy\n• If fails medical management for rate control, is patient’s preference or other \nfactors determine this is preferred\n• Pill in pocket antiarrhythmic drug therapy\n• Catheter ablation Refer to cardiology, or directly to electrophysiology/arrhythmia services where available, for rhythm-control planning, unsuccessful rate management or procedural options such as ablation. The patient's wish to pursue sinus rhythm and whether their other cardiac conditions would benefit from it also inform referral. Service availability and referral routes differ by location.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:51:47-01:52:49 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=97\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-096.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 96 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-096.webp\" alt=\"Slide 96: Consultation or Referral\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Refer to cardiology, or directly to electrophysiology/arrhythmia services where available, for rhythm-control planning, unsuccessful rate management or procedural options such as ablation.</p><p>The patient&#x27;s wish to pursue sinus rhythm and whether their other cardiac conditions would benefit from it also inform referral. Service availability and referral routes differ by location.</p>"},{"block":97,"title":"Slide 97 · Figure or report","text":"Slide 97 Figure or report  This CCS visual summarizes AF management themes. Use it as a map connecting risk-factor management, recognition, stroke prevention and specialist treatment options. The lecturer did not separately explain every item in the infographic. The substantive discussion is captured under the corresponding slides above.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:39:16 and 01:52:19 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=98\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-097.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 97 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-097.webp\" alt=\"Slide 97: Figure or report\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>This CCS visual summarizes AF management themes. Use it as a map connecting risk-factor management, recognition, stroke prevention and specialist treatment options.</p><p>The lecturer did not separately explain every item in the infographic. The substantive discussion is captured under the corresponding slides above.</p>"},{"block":98,"title":"Slide 98 · Figure or report","text":"Slide 98 Figure or report  Before cardioversion, establish symptom onset, the reliability of that timing, and anticoagulation history. The lecturer warned against restoring rhythm when an atrial thrombus may have formed; some patients cannot reliably recognize when AF began. For warfarin, she discussed obtaining a baseline INR and noted that mechanical-valve targets depend on valve type and position. Do not apply an AF INR target to every mechanical valve. !The spoken '24 hours' and 'anytime if anticoagulated' rules need qualification. Cardioversion planning depends on stability, AF duration, stroke risk, adequate therapeutic anticoagulation and/or imaging to exclude thrombus. Canadian guidance uses risk-stratified timing; ESC 2024 uses a 24-hour threshold. Appropriate anticoagulation before and after the procedure remains important. [3,4]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:33:58-01:36:25 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=99\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-098.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 98 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-098.webp\" alt=\"Slide 98: Figure or report\"></a><h3>Related lecture questions</h3><p>Before cardioversion, establish symptom onset, the reliability of that timing, and anticoagulation history. The lecturer warned against restoring rhythm when an atrial thrombus may have formed; some patients cannot reliably recognize when AF began.</p><p>For warfarin, she discussed obtaining a baseline INR and noted that mechanical-valve targets depend on valve type and position. Do not apply an AF INR target to every mechanical valve.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The spoken &#x27;24 hours&#x27; and &#x27;anytime if anticoagulated&#x27; rules need qualification. Cardioversion planning depends on stability, AF duration, stroke risk, adequate therapeutic anticoagulation and/or imaging to exclude thrombus. Canadian guidance uses risk-stratified timing; ESC 2024 uses a 24-hour threshold. Appropriate anticoagulation before and after the procedure remains important. [3,4]</p>"},{"block":99,"title":"Slide 99 · Atrioventicular (AV) Blocks","text":"Slide 99 Atrioventicular (AV) Blocks Atrioventicular (AV) Blocks The lecturer retained the AV-block section because conduction disease is relevant to syncope, but did not teach these slides in detail during this recording. The following notes are concise summaries of the supplied slides, not reconstructed lecture dialogue.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=100\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-099.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 99 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-099.webp\" alt=\"Slide 99: Atrioventicular (AV) Blocks\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The lecturer retained the AV-block section because conduction disease is relevant to syncope, but did not teach these slides in detail during this recording.</p><p>The following notes are concise summaries of the supplied slides, not reconstructed lecture dialogue.</p>"},{"block":100,"title":"Slide 100 · Atrioventicular (AV) Blocks","text":"Slide 100 Atrioventicular (AV) Blocks Atrioventicular (AV) Blocks\n•Result from interference in the conduction \npathway between atria and ventricles\n•AV Blocks are the most common cause of \nbradycardia\n•Block can occur at the AV node, Bundle of His or \nbundle branches\n•Block at the bundle branches may result in a wider \nQRS complex\n•Can be transient or permanent\n•Classifications:\n• First Degree\n• Second Degree (type I and type II)\n• Third Degree AV block is impaired conduction between atria and ventricles. The site and severity of block affect which impulses conduct, the ventricular rate and sometimes QRS width. Organize recognition into first degree, second degree Mobitz I or II, and complete block. Assess atrial activity separately from ventricular activity.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=101\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-100.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 100 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-100.webp\" alt=\"Slide 100: Atrioventicular (AV) Blocks\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>AV block is impaired conduction between atria and ventricles. The site and severity of block affect which impulses conduct, the ventricular rate and sometimes QRS width.</p><p>Organize recognition into first degree, second degree Mobitz I or II, and complete block. Assess atrial activity separately from ventricular activity.</p>"},{"block":101,"title":"Slide 101 · First Degree AV Block","text":"Slide 101 First Degree AV Block First Degree AV Block\n• Delay of the electrical impulse between the atria and ventricle\n• PRI is greater than 0.20 seconds\n• First degree AV block is not considered a dysrhythmia\n• Medications such as digitalis, beta-blockers and calcium channel \nblockers are usually the cause\n• The sinus impulse is conducted normally to the AV node but is delayed \nlonger than usual before being conducted to the ventricles\n• Rate: That of underlying rhythm, atrial = ventricular\n• Rhythm: Regular\n• P Waves: Upright and uniform\n• P/QRS:  1:1\n• PRI: Prolonged (> 0.20 seconds)\n• QRS: Normal, <0.12 seconds First-degree AV block has a prolonged PR interval above 0.20 seconds with all atrial impulses conducted: P:QRS remains 1:1. The slide lists AV-nodal-slowing medications among possible contributors. Identify the underlying rhythm and review the clinical and medication context rather than interpreting PR prolongation in isolation.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=102\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-101.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 101 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-101.webp\" alt=\"Slide 101: First Degree AV Block\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>First-degree AV block has a prolonged PR interval above 0.20 seconds with all atrial impulses conducted: P:QRS remains 1:1.</p><p>The slide lists AV-nodal-slowing medications among possible contributors. Identify the underlying rhythm and review the clinical and medication context rather than interpreting PR prolongation in isolation.</p>"},{"block":102,"title":"Slide 102 · First Degree AV Block","text":"Slide 102 First Degree AV Block First Degree AV Block\nPR interval: 11 small boxes = 0.44 sec\nNormal PR = 0.12-0.20 sec The example labels PR as 11 small squares: 11 multiplied by 0.04 seconds equals 0.44 seconds. This is longer than the teaching range of 0.12-0.20 seconds. The defining visual relationship is prolonged atrioventricular conduction with a conducted QRS after every P, rather than dropped ventricular complexes.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=103\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-102.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 102 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-102.webp\" alt=\"Slide 102: First Degree AV Block\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The example labels PR as 11 small squares: 11 multiplied by 0.04 seconds equals 0.44 seconds. This is longer than the teaching range of 0.12-0.20 seconds.</p><p>The defining visual relationship is prolonged atrioventricular conduction with a conducted QRS after every P, rather than dropped ventricular complexes.</p>"},{"block":103,"title":"Slide 103 · Second Degree AV Block – Type 1 and Type 2","text":"Slide 103 Second Degree AV Block – Type 1 and Type 2 Second Degree AV Block – Type 1 and Type 2\n•Occurs when the electrical impulse from the SA \nnode is delayed or intermittently blocked\n•ECG will show more P waves than QRS complexes\n•Key to determining type of second degree block is \nthe PR interval\n• Type 1 has increasing PR intervals, increasing until the QRS is \"dropped\" or \nmissing\n• Type 2 has constant PR intervals, with randomly dropped QRS complexes\n• Type 2 heart block is a permanent condition and \ncan sometimes progress to third degree heart block\n• This is an important contrast to Type 1 which is usually temporary \nand rarely progresses\n• Type 2 to 3’ - patient has a high probability of needing a pacemaker\n• Also referred to as Mobitz Type 1 or Wenkebach and Mobitz Type 2 In second-degree block, some P waves are not followed by a QRS. Use the PR pattern to help distinguish types. Mobitz I: PR progressively lengthens before a nonconducted P. Mobitz II: conducted beats have a stable PR while some P waves do not conduct. The slide highlights greater concern for progression in Mobitz II. Do not reduce assessment to 'temporary versus permanent'; identify the pattern and clinical risk.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=104\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-103.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 103 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-103.webp\" alt=\"Slide 103: Second Degree AV Block – Type 1 and Type 2\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>In second-degree block, some P waves are not followed by a QRS. Use the PR pattern to help distinguish types.</p><p>Mobitz I: PR progressively lengthens before a nonconducted P. Mobitz II: conducted beats have a stable PR while some P waves do not conduct.</p><p>The slide highlights greater concern for progression in Mobitz II. Do not reduce assessment to &#x27;temporary versus permanent&#x27;; identify the pattern and clinical risk.</p>"},{"block":104,"title":"Slide 104 · Second Degree AV Block - Type 1","text":"Slide 104 Second Degree AV Block - Type 1 Second Degree AV Block - Type 1\n•Rate:  atrial - that of underlying rhythm, ventricular \n- will depend on number of impulses conducted \nthrough AV node but will be less than atrial rate\n•Rhythm: atrial - regular, ventricular - irregular\n•P Waves: Sinus, normal size and shape\n•PRI: Varies & progressively lengthens until a P wave \noccurs without a QRS \n•QRS: Normal, <0.12 seconds Mobitz I usually has a regular atrial rhythm with an irregular ventricular rhythm because an atrial impulse intermittently fails to conduct. The key pattern is progressive PR lengthening followed by a P wave without a QRS. Ventricular rate is lower than atrial rate because fewer impulses reach the ventricles.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=105\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-104.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 104 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-104.webp\" alt=\"Slide 104: Second Degree AV Block - Type 1\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Mobitz I usually has a regular atrial rhythm with an irregular ventricular rhythm because an atrial impulse intermittently fails to conduct.</p><p>The key pattern is progressive PR lengthening followed by a P wave without a QRS. Ventricular rate is lower than atrial rate because fewer impulses reach the ventricles.</p>"},{"block":105,"title":"Slide 105 · Second Degree AV Block - Type 1","text":"Slide 105 Second Degree AV Block - Type 1 Second Degree AV Block - Type 1 On this tracing, mark each P wave, then compare the PR intervals of successive conducted beats. Find the nonconducted P and the repeating sequence. This is a practice illustration; the lecturer did not give an individual walkthrough in the supplied recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=106\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-105.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 105 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-105.webp\" alt=\"Slide 105: Second Degree AV Block - Type 1\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>On this tracing, mark each P wave, then compare the PR intervals of successive conducted beats. Find the nonconducted P and the repeating sequence.</p><p>This is a practice illustration; the lecturer did not give an individual walkthrough in the supplied recording.</p>"},{"block":106,"title":"Slide 106 · Second Degree AV Block - Type I","text":"Slide 106 Second Degree AV Block - Type I Second Degree AV Block - Type I\nPossible causes:\n• Medications: digoxin, beta \nblockers, Ca channel \nblockers\n• Increased vagal tone \n• Myocarditis\n• Ischemic heart disease\n• Cardiac surgery\nSigns & Symptoms:\n• Presyncopal symptoms\n• Weakness\n• Dizziness\n• Lightneadedness\n• Hemodynamic compromise\n•\nType 1 may occur in otherwise normal healthy children during sleep \nor in conditioned athletes The slide lists medications, increased vagal tone, ischemia, inflammation and postoperative factors as possible contributors to Mobitz I. It can also occur during sleep or in trained athletes. Assess symptoms and perfusion: weakness, dizziness or presyncope make the context different from an incidental asymptomatic finding.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=107\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-106.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 106 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-106.webp\" alt=\"Slide 106: Second Degree AV Block - Type I\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The slide lists medications, increased vagal tone, ischemia, inflammation and postoperative factors as possible contributors to Mobitz I. It can also occur during sleep or in trained athletes.</p><p>Assess symptoms and perfusion: weakness, dizziness or presyncope make the context different from an incidental asymptomatic finding.</p>"},{"block":107,"title":"Slide 107 · Second Degree AV Block - Type 2","text":"Slide 107 Second Degree AV Block - Type 2 Second Degree AV Block - Type 2\n• A failure of some of the sinus impulses being conducted to \nthe ventricles\n• Rate: atrial - that of underlying rhythm (usually sinus), \nventricular - depends on number of impulses conducted \nthrough AV node; less than atrial rate\n• Rhythm: atrial - regular, ventricular - regular unless AV \nconduction ratio varies\n• P Waves: Normal size and shape\n• P/QRS Ratio:  More P waves than QRS complexes\n• PRI: May be normal or prolonged but remains constant\n• QRS: Normal if block located in Bundle of His, wide if block \nlocated in bundle branches Mobitz II shows atrial impulses that fail to conduct despite a constant PR interval on the conducted beats. More P waves than QRS complexes are present. The ventricular rhythm may be regular with a fixed conduction ratio or irregular with variable conduction. QRS width can vary with the level of conduction disease.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=108\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-107.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 107 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-107.webp\" alt=\"Slide 107: Second Degree AV Block - Type 2\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Mobitz II shows atrial impulses that fail to conduct despite a constant PR interval on the conducted beats. More P waves than QRS complexes are present.</p><p>The ventricular rhythm may be regular with a fixed conduction ratio or irregular with variable conduction. QRS width can vary with the level of conduction disease.</p>"},{"block":108,"title":"Slide 108 · Second Degree AV Block - Type 2","text":"Slide 108 Second Degree AV Block - Type 2 Second Degree AV Block - Type 2 Practice recognizing nonconducted P waves while comparing the PR of the conducted beats. Unlike Mobitz I, do not look for a progressive PR staircase before the missed QRS. No separate explanation of this particular tracing was recorded.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=109\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-108.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 108 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-108.webp\" alt=\"Slide 108: Second Degree AV Block - Type 2\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Practice recognizing nonconducted P waves while comparing the PR of the conducted beats. Unlike Mobitz I, do not look for a progressive PR staircase before the missed QRS.</p><p>No separate explanation of this particular tracing was recorded.</p>"},{"block":109,"title":"Slide 109 · Second Degree AV Block - Type 2","text":"Slide 109 Second Degree AV Block - Type 2 Second Degree AV Block - Type 2 Use this additional example to count atrial and ventricular activity separately and examine the conduction ratio. Interpret the entire pattern before assigning the block type; an isolated missed ventricular beat is not a complete description.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=110\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-109.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 109 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-109.webp\" alt=\"Slide 109: Second Degree AV Block - Type 2\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Use this additional example to count atrial and ventricular activity separately and examine the conduction ratio.</p><p>Interpret the entire pattern before assigning the block type; an isolated missed ventricular beat is not a complete description.</p>"},{"block":110,"title":"Slide 110 · Second Degree AV Block - Type 2","text":"Slide 110 Second Degree AV Block - Type 2 Second Degree AV Block - Type 2 This is another conduction-block practice tracing. Identify P waves, conducted QRS complexes and whether the conducted PR intervals are stable. The supplied audio does not include the lecturer's answer-by-answer analysis of this example.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=111\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-110.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 110 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-110.webp\" alt=\"Slide 110: Second Degree AV Block - Type 2\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>This is another conduction-block practice tracing. Identify P waves, conducted QRS complexes and whether the conducted PR intervals are stable.</p><p>The supplied audio does not include the lecturer&#x27;s answer-by-answer analysis of this example.</p>"},{"block":111,"title":"Slide 111 · Second Degree AV Block, Type II","text":"Slide 111 Second Degree AV Block, Type II Second Degree AV Block, Type II\nPossible causes:\n• Post open-heart surgery \nwhere there is trauma at or \nbelow the Bundle of His\n• Medications: digoxin, beta \nblockers, Ca channel \nblockers\n• Increased vagal tone \n• Myocarditis\n• Ischemic heart disease\n• Cardiac surgery\n• Conduction system disease\nSigns & Symptoms:\n• Weakness\n• Dizziness\n• Syncope\n• Hemodynamic compromise Review the slide's possible contributors, including ischemia, conduction-system disease, medications and cardiac procedures, in the patient's actual context. Weakness, dizziness, syncope and hemodynamic compromise are important symptoms. The later syncope discussion specifically identifies Mobitz II as a high-risk ECG finding requiring urgent assessment.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=112\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-111.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 111 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-111.webp\" alt=\"Slide 111: Second Degree AV Block, Type II\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Review the slide&#x27;s possible contributors, including ischemia, conduction-system disease, medications and cardiac procedures, in the patient&#x27;s actual context.</p><p>Weakness, dizziness, syncope and hemodynamic compromise are important symptoms. The later syncope discussion specifically identifies Mobitz II as a high-risk ECG finding requiring urgent assessment.</p>"},{"block":112,"title":"Slide 112 · Third Degree or Complete Heart Block","text":"Slide 112 Third Degree or Complete Heart Block Third Degree or Complete Heart Block\n• All electrical impulses from the atria are blocked before \nthey reach the ventricle\n• Atrial block could occur at the AV junction, Bundle of \nHis, or the bundle branches\n• P waves will be present and are consistent in shape and \nare regular in rhythm but will have no relationship to \nthe QRS complexes\n• Complete blocks that occur at the AV junction will be \nfaster in rate and have a normal QRS duration\n• Complete blocks that occur at the Bundle of His or the \nbundle branches will have a slower rate and a QRS \nduration >0.12 seconds Complete heart block means atrial impulses do not conduct to the ventricles. P waves and QRS complexes continue independently, without a consistent PR relationship. The escape rhythm's origin influences its rate and QRS width. A more distal ventricular escape is generally slower and broader than a junctional escape.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=113\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-112.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 112 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-112.webp\" alt=\"Slide 112: Third Degree or Complete Heart Block\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Complete heart block means atrial impulses do not conduct to the ventricles. P waves and QRS complexes continue independently, without a consistent PR relationship.</p><p>The escape rhythm&#x27;s origin influences its rate and QRS width. A more distal ventricular escape is generally slower and broader than a junctional escape.</p>"},{"block":113,"title":"Slide 113 · Third Degree or Complete Heart Block","text":"Slide 113 Third Degree or Complete Heart Block Third Degree or Complete Heart Block\n• A complete absence of conduction between the atria and \nventricles\n• The atria and ventricles beat independently of each other\n• Rate: atrial - that of underlying rhythm (usually sinus), \nventricular - usually slower than normal depending on \nrhythm\n• Rhythm:  atrial – regular, ventricular - regular\n• P Waves: Sinus P waves\n• PRI: P waves have no relationship to QRS\n• QRS: Depends on origin\n• <0.12 seconds for junctional\n• >0.12 seconds for ventricular Assess P-P regularity and R-R regularity separately: each may be regular while the two are unrelated. This atrioventricular dissociation is the central teaching feature here. Do not force a PR measurement when there is no consistent relationship. Determine the ventricular rate and assess the patient's perfusion.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=114\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-113.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 113 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-113.webp\" alt=\"Slide 113: Third Degree or Complete Heart Block\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Assess P-P regularity and R-R regularity separately: each may be regular while the two are unrelated. This atrioventricular dissociation is the central teaching feature here.</p><p>Do not force a PR measurement when there is no consistent relationship. Determine the ventricular rate and assess the patient&#x27;s perfusion.</p>"},{"block":114,"title":"Slide 114 · Third Degree or Complete Heart Block","text":"Slide 114 Third Degree or Complete Heart Block Third Degree or Complete Heart Block On the tracing, follow the P waves across the strip without using QRS position as their reference, then follow the ventricular complexes separately. Look for the lack of a repeated P-to-QRS relationship. No separate live interpretation of this image was recorded.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=115\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-114.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 114 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-114.webp\" alt=\"Slide 114: Third Degree or Complete Heart Block\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>On the tracing, follow the P waves across the strip without using QRS position as their reference, then follow the ventricular complexes separately.</p><p>Look for the lack of a repeated P-to-QRS relationship. No separate live interpretation of this image was recorded.</p>"},{"block":115,"title":"Slide 115 · Third Degree or Complete Heart Block","text":"Slide 115 Third Degree or Complete Heart Block Third Degree or Complete Heart Block This additional complete-block example allows practice identifying atrial and ventricular independence and the escape rhythm's appearance. Compare it with the first- and second-degree examples rather than treating all slow rhythms as sinus bradycardia.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=116\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-115.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 115 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-115.webp\" alt=\"Slide 115: Third Degree or Complete Heart Block\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>This additional complete-block example allows practice identifying atrial and ventricular independence and the escape rhythm&#x27;s appearance.</p><p>Compare it with the first- and second-degree examples rather than treating all slow rhythms as sinus bradycardia.</p>"},{"block":116,"title":"Slide 116 · Third Degree AV Block or Complete Heart ","text":"Slide 116 Third Degree AV Block or Complete Heart  Third Degree AV Block or Complete Heart \nBlock\nPossible causes:\n• Congenital\n• CV surgery\n• Medications: digitalis, \ntricyclic antidepressants\n• Acute coronary syndrome\n• Post MI\nSigns & Symptoms:\n• Asymptomatic to severe \nhemodynamic compromise\n• Dizziness\n• Syncope\n• Weakness\n• Chest pain The slide lists congenital disease, cardiac surgery, medication effects and ischemic events among possible settings for complete block. Presentation can range from few symptoms to marked hemodynamic compromise. Dizziness, syncope, weakness and chest pain require attention. In the later syncope section, complete block is explicitly treated as a high-risk finding.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:52:49-01:53:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=117\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-116.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 116 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-116.webp\" alt=\"Slide 116: Third Degree AV Block or Complete Heart \"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The slide lists congenital disease, cardiac surgery, medication effects and ischemic events among possible settings for complete block. Presentation can range from few symptoms to marked hemodynamic compromise.</p><p>Dizziness, syncope, weakness and chest pain require attention. In the later syncope section, complete block is explicitly treated as a high-risk finding.</p>"},{"block":117,"title":"Slide 117 · Syncope… Not For The Faint of Heart","text":"Slide 117 Syncope… Not For The Faint of Heart Syncope… Not For The Faint of Heart\n• clinical syndrome in which transient loss of \nconsciousness (TLOC) is caused by a period of \ninadequate cerebral blood flow and oxygenation, \nmost often the result of an abrupt drop of systemic \nblood pressure\n• the inadequate cerebral nutrient flow is of relatively brief \nduration, and, by definition, syncope is self-limited\n• term \"syncope\" is often misapplied to encompass \nother forms of abrupt collapse such as seizures or \naccidents, which may or may not be accompanied by \nTLOC\n• syncope is a transient, self-limited loss of \nconsciousness caused by transient, self-terminating, \ninadequate nutrient flow to the brain\n• syncopal episodes may or may not be preceded by \nprodromal symptoms\n• presyncope (or near-syncope) is a clinical manifestation that \nis suggestive of an impending syncope often occurring in \nconjunction with a similar set of prodromal symptoms \nreflecting the same conditions that might lead to syncope in \nthe same individual at other times Syncope is a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness caused by inadequate cerebral perfusion, often with a fall in systemic blood pressure. Consciousness returns when adequate perfusion is restored. Patients may use 'passed out' to describe a seizure, fall or other collapse, so establish whether there was an actual transient loss of consciousness and what happened around it. Presyncope refers to warning symptoms suggesting an impending faint, without necessarily losing consciousness. Syncope may occur with or without a prodrome; that distinction helps assess cause.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:53:17-01:55:45 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=118\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-117.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 117 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-117.webp\" alt=\"Slide 117: Syncope… Not For The Faint of Heart\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Syncope is a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness caused by inadequate cerebral perfusion, often with a fall in systemic blood pressure. Consciousness returns when adequate perfusion is restored.</p><p>Patients may use &#x27;passed out&#x27; to describe a seizure, fall or other collapse, so establish whether there was an actual transient loss of consciousness and what happened around it.</p><p>Presyncope refers to warning symptoms suggesting an impending faint, without necessarily losing consciousness. Syncope may occur with or without a prodrome; that distinction helps assess cause.</p>"},{"block":118,"title":"Slide 118 · Figure or report","text":"Slide 118 Figure or report  Use the figure to organize possible causes and the clinical features that point toward cardiac syncope. The lecturer's focus was recognizing cardiovascular causes within the much wider differential. The detailed history on the next slides matters because the patient can have an entirely normal examination after the event has resolved.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:55:45-01:56:32 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=119\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-118.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 118 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-118.webp\" alt=\"Slide 118: Figure or report\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Use the figure to organize possible causes and the clinical features that point toward cardiac syncope. The lecturer&#x27;s focus was recognizing cardiovascular causes within the much wider differential.</p><p>The detailed history on the next slides matters because the patient can have an entirely normal examination after the event has resolved.</p>"},{"block":119,"title":"Slide 119 · Syncope","text":"Slide 119 Syncope Syncope\nKey History Taking Considerations\n• history taking is paramount in presyncope/syncope because physical exam \nfindings are often benign\n• frequency and duration\n• number and frequency of episodes - benign causes (such as vasovagal faint) is \nvariable; single or very rare episodes, others will have multiple episodes over \nmany years\n• patient with multiple episodes occurring over a short period of time (i.e. several \ndays or weeks) is more likely to be suffering from a serious underlying disorder \n(likely cardiac) and requires more aggressive evaluation\n• span of time marked by episodes - the longer the period of time over which \nepisodes have occurred (i.e., many years versus very recent onset) and the \nyounger the patient’s age at onset (particularly < 35 years), the less likely that the \ncause of syncope is life-threatening\n• triggers\n• reflex syncope may be triggered by emotional or orthostatic stress, fear, or \nintense pain, or by a warm and/or crowded environment; may not be possible to \nidentify the trigger\n• situational syncope (a type of reflex syncope) occurs during or immediately after \ncertain apparent triggers such as urination, defecation, coughing, swallowing, or \nafter eating a meal Establish the number of episodes, their frequency, the time span over which they have occurred and age at first onset. New clustered events over days or weeks are more concerning than a long, stable pattern beginning when young. Ask about warning symptoms and triggers. Heat, prolonged standing, emotional distress, pain, urination, defecation or coughing can support reflex or situational syncope in context. Abrupt collapse without warning raises suspicion for arrhythmia, especially with an injury suggesting no attempt to protect against the fall. The lecturer contrasted a gradual hot, nauseated, dizzy faint with being well one moment and on the ground the next.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">01:56:32-02:01:44 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=120\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-119.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 119 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-119.webp\" alt=\"Slide 119: Syncope\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Establish the number of episodes, their frequency, the time span over which they have occurred and age at first onset. New clustered events over days or weeks are more concerning than a long, stable pattern beginning when young.</p><p>Ask about warning symptoms and triggers. Heat, prolonged standing, emotional distress, pain, urination, defecation or coughing can support reflex or situational syncope in context.</p><p>Abrupt collapse without warning raises suspicion for arrhythmia, especially with an injury suggesting no attempt to protect against the fall. The lecturer contrasted a gradual hot, nauseated, dizzy faint with being well one moment and on the ground the next.</p>"},{"block":120,"title":"Slide 120 · Syncope","text":"Slide 120 Syncope Syncope\n• patient position\n• assessing the patient's position (i.e., supine, sitting, or standing) at the time \nof syncope, along with any recent changes in position during the preceding \nfew minutes prior to syncope, provides clues to the etiology\n• while in an unprotected position (i.e., prolonged standing) - reflex syncope \nmost commonly occurs when the patient is upright (standing or seated) and \nalmost never when supine\n• syncope resulting from orthostatic hypotension is frequently associated with \na change from a supine to erect posture, although several minutes may pass \nbetween the patient arising and the subsequent collapse.\n• while supine - syncope that occurs when the patient is supine or recumbent \nsuggests a cardiac arrhythmia \n• even if the arrhythmia persists, the TLOC is often brief given the body's adaptive \nhemodynamic compensatory responses (i.e., vascular constriction).\n• relation to exercise – timing of syncope in relation to exercise is very \nimportant, as syncope during full-flight exercise may be indicative of a \nserious condition (i.e., exercise-triggered tachyarrhythmia or hypotension, \naortic stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy)\n• syncope immediately after exercise tends to be more innocent and reflex in origin Clarify posture and recent position changes: prolonged standing, rising from bed, sitting, or lying down can suggest different mechanisms. The lecturer was especially concerned by abrupt unexplained loss of consciousness while seated; supine events also raise concern for arrhythmia. Distinguish syncope during exercise from symptoms immediately after stopping. Collapse during exertion raises concern for a serious cardiac cause, including arrhythmia or structural obstruction. No single position establishes the diagnosis. Combine it with prodrome, trigger, associated symptoms and cardiac background.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:01:44-02:03:15 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=121\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-120.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 120 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-120.webp\" alt=\"Slide 120: Syncope\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Clarify posture and recent position changes: prolonged standing, rising from bed, sitting, or lying down can suggest different mechanisms. The lecturer was especially concerned by abrupt unexplained loss of consciousness while seated; supine events also raise concern for arrhythmia.</p><p>Distinguish syncope during exercise from symptoms immediately after stopping. Collapse during exertion raises concern for a serious cardiac cause, including arrhythmia or structural obstruction.</p><p>No single position establishes the diagnosis. Combine it with prodrome, trigger, associated symptoms and cardiac background.</p>"},{"block":121,"title":"Slide 121 · Syncope","text":"Slide 121 Syncope Syncope\n• onset/prodrome\n• symptoms preceding syncope can point toward a specific cause\n• warning premonition period of at least a few seconds or longer prior to losing consciousness\n• witnessed signs\n• manner in which the collapse occurred, appearance of the patient, duration of the loss of \nconsciousness, alterations in the patient's breathing pattern, any physical movements\n• associated symptoms\n• patients should be asked about experiencing any of the following during the event or shortly \nafter recovery: confusion, fatigue, injury, nausea, vomiting, feeling cold or clammy, \npalpitations, shortness of breath, chest pain, bladder or bowel incontinence\n• persistence of nausea, pallor, and diaphoresis in addition to prolonged fatigue (lasting \nminutes to hours) after a syncope episode suggest a reflex event (particularly a vasovagal \nepisode); these findings are helpful in distinguishing reflex syncope from syncope due to an \narrhythmia.\n• neurologic changes or confusion during the recovery period may suggest a stroke or seizure\n• past medical/preexisting conditions\n• drugs and alcohol\n• medications – classes/categories and why?\n• family history - sudden cardiac death, drowning Obtain a witness account when possible: the person who lost consciousness cannot describe the whole event. Ask about the collapse, duration, movements, breathing and recovery, as well as symptoms immediately before and after. Nausea, pallor, sweating and prolonged fatigue can support a reflex episode. Prolonged confusion or focal neurological changes suggest a different process and need assessment. The lecturer contrasted a prolonged washed-out feeling after a vasovagal faint with rapid recovery after an arrhythmia terminates. Recovery pattern is a clue, not a diagnosis by itself. Review medical conditions, medications, alcohol and other substances, and family sudden death or unexplained drowning.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:03:15-02:05:11 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=122\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-121.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 121 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-121.webp\" alt=\"Slide 121: Syncope\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Obtain a witness account when possible: the person who lost consciousness cannot describe the whole event. Ask about the collapse, duration, movements, breathing and recovery, as well as symptoms immediately before and after.</p><p>Nausea, pallor, sweating and prolonged fatigue can support a reflex episode. Prolonged confusion or focal neurological changes suggest a different process and need assessment.</p><p>The lecturer contrasted a prolonged washed-out feeling after a vasovagal faint with rapid recovery after an arrhythmia terminates. Recovery pattern is a clue, not a diagnosis by itself.</p><p>Review medical conditions, medications, alcohol and other substances, and family sudden death or unexplained drowning.</p>"},{"block":122,"title":"Slide 122 · Syncope","text":"Slide 122 Syncope Syncope\n• Physical Exam\n• Full cardiac and neurological\n• Orthostatic blood pressure\n• Often will have normal physical exam findings\n• Diagnostics\n• ECG\n• Echocardiogram\n• Holter monitor\n• CT – head\n• EEG\n• Focused Clinical Practice Update\n• Canadian Cardiovascular Society Clinical Practice Update on the Assessment \nand Management of Syncope (2020)\n• https://onlinecjc.ca/action/showPdf?pii=S0828-282X%2819%2931549-1 Perform cardiac and neurological examinations and orthostatic vital signs. A normal examination after recovery does not exclude an important cause. Choose investigations from the history and suspected mechanism. ECG and, when indicated, echo or ambulatory monitoring address possible cardiac causes. The lecturer pointed to the CCS syncope practice update as a risk-assessment reference. The slide lists CT and EEG among possible investigations; it should not be read as a routine panel for every faint. Their role depends on evidence of neurological disease, seizure or injury.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:05:11-02:05:59 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=123\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-122.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 122 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-122.webp\" alt=\"Slide 122: Syncope\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Perform cardiac and neurological examinations and orthostatic vital signs. A normal examination after recovery does not exclude an important cause.</p><p>Choose investigations from the history and suspected mechanism. ECG and, when indicated, echo or ambulatory monitoring address possible cardiac causes. The lecturer pointed to the CCS syncope practice update as a risk-assessment reference.</p><p>The slide lists CT and EEG among possible investigations; it should not be read as a routine panel for every faint. Their role depends on evidence of neurological disease, seizure or injury.</p>"},{"block":123,"title":"Slide 123 · Syncope","text":"Slide 123 Syncope Syncope The lecturer highlighted high-risk ECG findings in a patient with syncope: VT or runs of VT, significant rapid supraventricular rhythms, Mobitz II and complete heart block. These warrant urgent assessment rather than routine outpatient follow-up. For a patient presenting with a dangerous rhythm and concerning symptoms in clinic, she described calling an ambulance for emergency transfer. Combine the ECG with history and examination: known heart disease, exertional or supine syncope, absent prodrome, significant injury, abnormal vital signs and a concerning family history increase suspicion. A normal examination alone is not sufficient reassurance.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:05:59-02:07:31 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=124\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-123.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 123 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-123.webp\" alt=\"Slide 123: Syncope\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The lecturer highlighted high-risk ECG findings in a patient with syncope: VT or runs of VT, significant rapid supraventricular rhythms, Mobitz II and complete heart block. These warrant urgent assessment rather than routine outpatient follow-up.</p><p>For a patient presenting with a dangerous rhythm and concerning symptoms in clinic, she described calling an ambulance for emergency transfer.</p><p>Combine the ECG with history and examination: known heart disease, exertional or supine syncope, absent prodrome, significant injury, abnormal vital signs and a concerning family history increase suspicion. A normal examination alone is not sufficient reassurance.</p>"},{"block":124,"title":"Slide 124 · Figure or report","text":"Slide 124 Figure or report  Use the pathway to connect risk stratification with the next action. Low-risk features allow a more staged assessment for reflex or other benign causes; alarming features require a different urgency. Order tests to answer the suspected mechanism rather than working through every box. The lecturer emphasized that primary care can perform substantial initial assessment when the presentation is genuinely low risk.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:07:31-02:08:08 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=125\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-124.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 124 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-124.webp\" alt=\"Slide 124: Figure or report\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Use the pathway to connect risk stratification with the next action. Low-risk features allow a more staged assessment for reflex or other benign causes; alarming features require a different urgency.</p><p>Order tests to answer the suspected mechanism rather than working through every box. The lecturer emphasized that primary care can perform substantial initial assessment when the presentation is genuinely low risk.</p>"},{"block":125,"title":"Slide 125 · Ventricular Rhythms","text":"Slide 125 Ventricular Rhythms Ventricular Rhythms Ventricular tachycardia was identified as a rhythm students need to recognize. The following examples connect ventricular ectopy with potentially sustained ventricular arrhythmias. Separate rhythm recognition and emergency escalation from the specialist's longer-term arrhythmia treatment plan.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:08:08-02:08:44 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=126\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-125.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 125 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-125.webp\" alt=\"Slide 125: Ventricular Rhythms\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Ventricular tachycardia was identified as a rhythm students need to recognize. The following examples connect ventricular ectopy with potentially sustained ventricular arrhythmias.</p><p>Separate rhythm recognition and emergency escalation from the specialist&#x27;s longer-term arrhythmia treatment plan.</p>"},{"block":126,"title":"Slide 126 · Ventricular Tachycardia","text":"Slide 126 Ventricular Tachycardia Ventricular Tachycardia\n• three or more PVCs occur in a row and the ventricular rate exceeds 100 \nbeats/minute\n• extremely unstable rhythm when prolonged\n• can occur in short, paroxysmal bursts lasting fewer than 30 seconds and \ncausing few or no symptoms\n• can be sustained, requiring immediate treatment to prevent death, even in \npatients initially able to maintain adequate cardiac output\n• results from increased myocardial irritability\n• conditions that can cause ventricular tachycardia include:\n• myocardial ischemia, infarction, coronary artery disease\n• valvular heart disease\n• heart failure (poor LV function)\n• Cardiomyopathy\n• electrolyte imbalances\n• drug toxicity\n• pro-arrhythmic effects of some anti-arrhythymics The lecturer clarified the definition: three or more consecutive ventricular beats at a rate above 100/min are VT. This supersedes the earlier four-beat wording during the PVC discussion. Nonsustained runs may last less than 30 seconds and produce few symptoms, particularly when cardiac function is otherwise good. Symptoms and risk are greater with underlying structural disease or reduced LV function; absence of symptoms does not make VT harmless. Prolonged VT can become pulseless or deteriorate into VF. Consider ischemia, infarction, cardiomyopathy, valvular disease, impaired LV function, electrolyte disturbance, toxicity and proarrhythmic drugs as possible settings.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:08:44-02:10:39 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=127\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-126.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 126 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-126.webp\" alt=\"Slide 126: Ventricular Tachycardia\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The lecturer clarified the definition: three or more consecutive ventricular beats at a rate above 100/min are VT. This supersedes the earlier four-beat wording during the PVC discussion.</p><p>Nonsustained runs may last less than 30 seconds and produce few symptoms, particularly when cardiac function is otherwise good. Symptoms and risk are greater with underlying structural disease or reduced LV function; absence of symptoms does not make VT harmless.</p><p>Prolonged VT can become pulseless or deteriorate into VF. Consider ischemia, infarction, cardiomyopathy, valvular disease, impaired LV function, electrolyte disturbance, toxicity and proarrhythmic drugs as possible settings.</p>"},{"block":127,"title":"Slide 127 · Ventricular Tachycardia","text":"Slide 127 Ventricular Tachycardia Ventricular Tachycardia\n•\nRegular (can initially be \nirregular for a few beats)\n•\nP wave absent or buried\n•\nQRS complex is wide and \nbizarre \n•\nT wave opposite QRS\n•\nVentricular rate is 100 to \n250 Recognize the rapid, broad ventricular complexes. Monomorphic VT has a similar QRS appearance from beat to beat; polymorphic VT has changing morphology. For an approximately regular example, the lecturer estimated rate using 300 divided by large boxes between complexes: two boxes is about 150/min; shorter intervals imply a higher rate. Rate calculation supports the description but does not replace assessing the rhythm and the patient's circulation.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:10:39-02:11:52 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=128\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-127.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 127 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-127.webp\" alt=\"Slide 127: Ventricular Tachycardia\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Recognize the rapid, broad ventricular complexes. Monomorphic VT has a similar QRS appearance from beat to beat; polymorphic VT has changing morphology.</p><p>For an approximately regular example, the lecturer estimated rate using 300 divided by large boxes between complexes: two boxes is about 150/min; shorter intervals imply a higher rate.</p><p>Rate calculation supports the description but does not replace assessing the rhythm and the patient&#x27;s circulation.</p>"},{"block":128,"title":"Slide 128 · Ventricular Fibrillation","text":"Slide 128 Ventricular Fibrillation Ventricular Fibrillation\n• Chaotic pattern of electrical activity in the ventricles in which electrical \nimpulses arise from many different foci\n• Produces no effective muscular contraction and no cardiac output\n• Untreated ventricular fibrillation causes most cases of sudden cardiac death \nin people outside of a hospital \n• Causes of ventricular fibrillation include: \n• myocardial ischemia\n• MI\n• untreated ventricular tachycardia \n• underlying heart disease\n• acid-base imbalance\n• electric shock\n• severe hypothermia \n• electrolyte imbalances, such as hypokalemia, hyperkalemia, and hypercalcemia\n• drug toxicity, including digoxin\n• severe hypoxia. \n• Ventricles quiver instead of contract, so cardiac output falls to zero\n• if fibrillation continues, it leads to ventricular standstill and death VF is disorganized ventricular electrical activity without effective ventricular contraction or cardiac output. It is a cardiac-arrest rhythm. The lecturer linked it to the same serious substrates discussed for VT, including ischemia, damaged myocardium, electrolyte or metabolic problems and toxicity. Prolonged VT can deteriorate into VF. The key distinction from a perfusing rhythm is the absence of effective circulation, not simply a very fast rate.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:11:52-02:12:24 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=129\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-128.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 128 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-128.webp\" alt=\"Slide 128: Ventricular Fibrillation\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>VF is disorganized ventricular electrical activity without effective ventricular contraction or cardiac output. It is a cardiac-arrest rhythm.</p><p>The lecturer linked it to the same serious substrates discussed for VT, including ischemia, damaged myocardium, electrolyte or metabolic problems and toxicity. Prolonged VT can deteriorate into VF.</p><p>The key distinction from a perfusing rhythm is the absence of effective circulation, not simply a very fast rate.</p>"},{"block":129,"title":"Slide 129 · Ventricular Fibrillation","text":"Slide 129 Ventricular Fibrillation Ventricular Fibrillation\n•\nChaotic, no pattern\n•\nRate cannot be determined\n•\nP wave absent \n•\nPR interval unmeasurable \nand QT interval not \napplicable\n•\nQRS complex, T wave \nindiscernible\n•\nWaveform is a wavy line The waveform has no organized, identifiable P-QRS-T sequence and no meaningful ventricular rate to count. The lecturer used a discharge-summary example: a familiar primary-care patient survives an out-of-hospital VF arrest and returns with new diagnoses, specialists and a recovery plan. The NP may not manage the specialist arrhythmia treatment, but needs to understand what happened, its implications and their role in recovery and comorbidity management. Recognizing the terminology makes the discharge plan clinically meaningful.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:12:24-02:13:53 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=130\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-129.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 129 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-129.webp\" alt=\"Slide 129: Ventricular Fibrillation\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and practice context</h3><p>The waveform has no organized, identifiable P-QRS-T sequence and no meaningful ventricular rate to count.</p><p>The lecturer used a discharge-summary example: a familiar primary-care patient survives an out-of-hospital VF arrest and returns with new diagnoses, specialists and a recovery plan.</p><p>The NP may not manage the specialist arrhythmia treatment, but needs to understand what happened, its implications and their role in recovery and comorbidity management. Recognizing the terminology makes the discharge plan clinically meaningful.</p>"},{"block":130,"title":"Slide 130 · Case Study 1","text":"Slide 130 Case Study 1 Case Study 1\n• Kenneth (he/him) is a 59-year-old nurse, who has had several episodes of chest \npain for the past four months. When asked, he estimates having had five to six \nepisodes of moderate chest tightness with exertion (e.g., while playing football \nwith his sons). He states he usually rests when having an episode of chest pain \nand the pain goes away after about ten minutes. He denies any sharp or stabbing \nchest pain during exertion or chest pain aggravated by movement. He also \ndenies having increased shortness of breath, palpitations or nausea.\n• PMH: Asthma x 10 years; GERD x 5 years\n• Medications: Budesonide 2 puffs BID, Salbutamol MDI 2 puffs QID PRN, Ranitidine 150 mg po \nBID\n• FMH: MI @ age 55 for his father; mother diabetes mellitus type 2 @ 63 yrs\n• Social History: Married, father of four, shift worker\nLifestyle: Smoked ½ ppd x 20 years (quit when diagnosed with asthma), but recently \nrestarted smoking 3-4 cigarettes per day,  ETOH 3-4 beers/day\n• The physical examination reveals:\n• Tall, pleasant man with truncal obesity\n• Ht 180 cm (5’10ʺ); Wt 98 kg (215 lbs); BMI 30; waist circumference 118 cm\n• BP 142/85 (sitting), 148/86 (supine), HR 72 (reg), RR 18\n• CVS: S1 S2 normal, no S3 or S4, no murmurs, no peripheral edema, JVP 2 cm at 45°, no chest \npain reproduced by palpation\n• Resp: General decreased air entry, no wheezes, no crackles Kenneth's chest tightness occurs with substantial exertion, such as playing football, and resolves after rest. He is not describing sharp, movement-provoked pain or ongoing symptoms at the office visit. Assess his risk background: paternal MI at 55, smoking resumed after quitting, approximately 10 prior pack-years, obesity, raised blood pressure and regular alcohol intake. His otherwise unremarkable examination does not establish whether coronary disease is present. The lecturer used this history to introduce stable exertional symptoms, risk factors and the distinction from an active acute coronary presentation.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:13:53-02:15:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=131\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-130.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 130 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-130.webp\" alt=\"Slide 130: Case Study 1\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and case discussion</h3><p>Kenneth&#x27;s chest tightness occurs with substantial exertion, such as playing football, and resolves after rest. He is not describing sharp, movement-provoked pain or ongoing symptoms at the office visit.</p><p>Assess his risk background: paternal MI at 55, smoking resumed after quitting, approximately 10 prior pack-years, obesity, raised blood pressure and regular alcohol intake. His otherwise unremarkable examination does not establish whether coronary disease is present.</p><p>The lecturer used this history to introduce stable exertional symptoms, risk factors and the distinction from an active acute coronary presentation.</p>"},{"block":131,"title":"Slide 131 · Case Study 1","text":"Slide 131 Case Study 1 Case Study 1\n• List Kenneth’s modifiable and non-modifiable risk factors for \natherosclerosis.\n• Which symptoms significantly increase the probability of an acute \nMI?\n• What is the likelihood that Kenneth is having an MI based on his \nsymptoms? Provide rationale.\n• Would this be an appropriate case to consult? Explain. Classify Kenneth's risk factors into those he can modify and those he cannot. His history supports investigating coronary disease even though he currently has no chest pain. The lecturer considered the predictable exertional-and-rest pattern more consistent with stable angina than an active MI at that visit. She contrasted this with a patient experiencing ongoing chest pain in the office, which changes urgency. This is a case-based assessment, not a rule that being pain-free excludes ACS. Clarify recent changes, symptoms at rest, duration and associated features before deciding the pathway.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:15:23 and 02:25:13-02:25:46 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=132\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-131.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 131 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-131.webp\" alt=\"Slide 131: Case Study 1\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and case discussion</h3><p>Classify Kenneth&#x27;s risk factors into those he can modify and those he cannot. His history supports investigating coronary disease even though he currently has no chest pain.</p><p>The lecturer considered the predictable exertional-and-rest pattern more consistent with stable angina than an active MI at that visit. She contrasted this with a patient experiencing ongoing chest pain in the office, which changes urgency.</p><p>This is a case-based assessment, not a rule that being pain-free excludes ACS. Clarify recent changes, symptoms at rest, duration and associated features before deciding the pathway.</p>"},{"block":132,"title":"Slide 132 · Coronary Artery Disease","text":"Slide 132 Coronary Artery Disease Coronary Artery Disease\n• most common cause of cardiovascular morbidity \nand mortality\n• in recent years, heart disease (which includes \nCAD/ ischemic heart disease among other cardiac \nconditions) has been the 2nd leading cause of \ndeath in Canada \n• in 2023, there were 57,890 deaths due to \n“diseases of heart” in Canada; the percentage of \nall deaths due to heart disease was about 17.7%\n• atherosclerosis is a systemic disease characterized \nby lipid deposition and smooth muscle cell \nmigration in the intima of the larger arteries\n• starts in childhood with the development of fatty \nstreaks\n• risk factors\n• non-modifiable: age, male sex, family history, ethnicity \n(African, Hispanic, Latino, Southeast Asian)\n• modifiable: smoking, HTN, diabetes, dyslipidemia, \nobesity, sedentary lifestyle, diets high in saturated fats, \ndrug/alcohol use Atherosclerosis is systemic: lipid deposition and inflammation affect arterial walls, and risk develops over time. Coronary disease was presented as a major cause of cardiovascular illness and death. Nonmodifiable factors include age, family history and sex; the lecturer discussed ethnicity-related differences in risk. Premature coronary disease in a first-degree relative was described using male onset before 55 and female onset before 65. Modifiable factors include smoking, hypertension, diabetes, dyslipidemia, obesity, inactivity, diet and substance use. Assess them because they inform both diagnostic probability and subsequent prevention.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:15:23-02:17:23 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=133\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-132.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 132 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-132.webp\" alt=\"Slide 132: Coronary Artery Disease\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Atherosclerosis is systemic: lipid deposition and inflammation affect arterial walls, and risk develops over time. Coronary disease was presented as a major cause of cardiovascular illness and death.</p><p>Nonmodifiable factors include age, family history and sex; the lecturer discussed ethnicity-related differences in risk. Premature coronary disease in a first-degree relative was described using male onset before 55 and female onset before 65.</p><p>Modifiable factors include smoking, hypertension, diabetes, dyslipidemia, obesity, inactivity, diet and substance use. Assess them because they inform both diagnostic probability and subsequent prevention.</p>"},{"block":133,"title":"Slide 133 · Chronic Stable Angina","text":"Slide 133 Chronic Stable Angina Chronic Stable Angina\n• typical\n1.\nretrosternal chest pain, tightness or discomfort radiating to left (± \nright) shoulder/arm/neck/jaw, associated with diaphoresis, \nnausea, anxiety\n2.\npredictably precipitated by the “3 Es”: exertion, emotion, eating\n3.\nbrief duration, lasting <10-15 min and typically relieved by rest and \nnitrates\n• atypical/probable angina (meets 2 of the above)\n• non-cardiac chest pain (meets none or 1 of the above)\n• anginal equivalents: dyspnea, acute LV failure, flash \npulmonary edema Typical stable angina has a reproducible pattern: retrosternal tightness or discomfort, provoked by exertion, emotion or eating, and relieved with rest or prescribed nitrate. Episodes are generally brief, resolving within about 10-15 minutes in the teaching description. Radiation can involve the arm, shoulder, neck or jaw; sweating, nausea or anxiety may accompany it. The lecturer emphasized predictability and the level of exertion needed to provoke symptoms. Some patients have anginal equivalents rather than classic chest pain. Reproducible exertional dyspnea that resolves with rest may be the presenting complaint. Ask directly about associated tightness rather than assuming the problem is solely respiratory.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:17:23-02:20:08 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=134\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-133.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 133 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-133.webp\" alt=\"Slide 133: Chronic Stable Angina\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Typical stable angina has a reproducible pattern: retrosternal tightness or discomfort, provoked by exertion, emotion or eating, and relieved with rest or prescribed nitrate. Episodes are generally brief, resolving within about 10-15 minutes in the teaching description.</p><p>Radiation can involve the arm, shoulder, neck or jaw; sweating, nausea or anxiety may accompany it. The lecturer emphasized predictability and the level of exertion needed to provoke symptoms.</p><p>Some patients have anginal equivalents rather than classic chest pain. Reproducible exertional dyspnea that resolves with rest may be the presenting complaint. Ask directly about associated tightness rather than assuming the problem is solely respiratory.</p>"},{"block":134,"title":"Slide 134 · Angina/Chest Pain","text":"Slide 134 Angina/Chest Pain Angina/Chest Pain\nDifferential Diagnoses\n• Chest pain is a localized sensation of distress or discomfort that may or may \nnot be associated with actual tissue damage\n• Cardiac - Ischemia, atherosclerosis, MI, angina, myocarditis, pericarditis, \ndissecting aortic aneurysm\n• Musculoskeletal - Muscle strain and inflammation caused by costochondritis, \nchest wall syndrome, cervicodorsal arthritis or intercostal myositis\n• Neurologic – Nerve inflammation 2’ herpes zoster and nerve root \ncompression\n• Pulmonary - PE, pneumothorax/hemothorax, tension pneumothorax, \npneumonia, empyema, pulmonary neoplasm, bronchiectasis, pleuritis, \npulmonary hypertension, and TB\n• Gastrointestinal - GERD, esophageal rupture, spasm, esophagitis, ulceration, \nachalasia, neoplasm, PUD, gastritis, pancreatitis, and biliary colic\n• Mediastinal - Lymphoma, thymoma\n• Vascular - Dissecting aortic aneurysm and aortic rupture\n• Psychogenic – Stress caused by anxiety, depression, or panic disorder Chest discomfort has a wide differential: cardiac, musculoskeletal, neurological, pulmonary, gastrointestinal, vascular and psychological causes. The lecturer emphasized the history as the main tool for narrowing these possibilities. Establish location, quality, duration, triggers, relief, associated symptoms and risk factors before anchoring on a familiar diagnosis such as reflux or anxiety. The slide includes serious alternatives, including aortic disease, pulmonary embolism and pneumothorax; the clinical presentation determines which require urgent exclusion.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:20:08-02:21:16 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=135\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-134.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 134 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-134.webp\" alt=\"Slide 134: Angina/Chest Pain\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Chest discomfort has a wide differential: cardiac, musculoskeletal, neurological, pulmonary, gastrointestinal, vascular and psychological causes.</p><p>The lecturer emphasized the history as the main tool for narrowing these possibilities. Establish location, quality, duration, triggers, relief, associated symptoms and risk factors before anchoring on a familiar diagnosis such as reflux or anxiety.</p><p>The slide includes serious alternatives, including aortic disease, pulmonary embolism and pneumothorax; the clinical presentation determines which require urgent exclusion.</p>"},{"block":135,"title":"Slide 135 · Canadian Cardiovascular Society (CCS)","text":"Slide 135 Canadian Cardiovascular Society (CCS) Canadian Cardiovascular Society (CCS)\nFunctional Classification of Angina Use the CCS angina class to communicate functional limitation. Class I means symptoms only with strenuous, rapid or prolonged exertion; II is slight limitation of ordinary activity; III is marked limitation; IV is inability to do physical activity without discomfort, with possible symptoms at rest. In a consult letter, 'CCS class III' immediately conveys substantially more limitation than class I. The lecturer compared this shared language with the NYHA functional classification used for heart-failure symptoms. Describe the actual activity threshold as well as the class, especially if it is changing.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:21:16-02:23:04 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=136\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-135.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 135 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-135.webp\" alt=\"Slide 135: Canadian Cardiovascular Society (CCS)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Use the CCS angina class to communicate functional limitation. Class I means symptoms only with strenuous, rapid or prolonged exertion; II is slight limitation of ordinary activity; III is marked limitation; IV is inability to do physical activity without discomfort, with possible symptoms at rest.</p><p>In a consult letter, &#x27;CCS class III&#x27; immediately conveys substantially more limitation than class I. The lecturer compared this shared language with the NYHA functional classification used for heart-failure symptoms.</p><p>Describe the actual activity threshold as well as the class, especially if it is changing.</p>"},{"block":136,"title":"Slide 136 · Clinical Assessment & Diagnostics","text":"Slide 136 Clinical Assessment & Diagnostics Clinical Assessment & Diagnostics\n• history including directed risk factor assessment and physical exam\n• physical exam will often be non-contributory\n• labs: Hb, HbA1C/fasting glucose, fasting lipid profile\n• ECG (at rest and during episode of chest pain if possible)\n• CXR (suspected heart failure, valvular disease, pericardial disease, \naortic dissection/aneurysm, or signs or symptoms of pulmonary \ndisease)\n• stress testing, nuclear medicine imaging (MIBI scan) or coronary \nangiography\n• echocardiogram to assess for wall motion abnormalities, valvular \ndisease, LV function in patients with Hx of prior MI, pathological Q \nwaves, signs or symptoms of CHF Take a detailed history and assess vascular risk factors; the physical examination may be normal. ECG is important, with additional tests selected for the question being asked. The lecturer discussed bloodwork for risk factors, CXR when dyspnea or examination findings suggest heart failure or pulmonary disease, and echo for cardiac structure, function and regional movement. Coronary investigations may include exercise testing, perfusion imaging or angiography, depending on the presentation. She stated that her NP ordering pathway required a physician for nuclear perfusion imaging; treat that as the local lecture context rather than a universal current ordering rule.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:23:04-02:25:13 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=137\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-136.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 136 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-136.webp\" alt=\"Slide 136: Clinical Assessment &amp; Diagnostics\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Take a detailed history and assess vascular risk factors; the physical examination may be normal. ECG is important, with additional tests selected for the question being asked.</p><p>The lecturer discussed bloodwork for risk factors, CXR when dyspnea or examination findings suggest heart failure or pulmonary disease, and echo for cardiac structure, function and regional movement.</p><p>Coronary investigations may include exercise testing, perfusion imaging or angiography, depending on the presentation. She stated that her NP ordering pathway required a physician for nuclear perfusion imaging; treat that as the local lecture context rather than a universal current ordering rule.</p>"},{"block":137,"title":"Slide 137 · Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS)","text":"Slide 137 Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS) Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS)\n• Used to refer to the pathophysiologic continuum that begins with \nplaque rupture in a coronary artery and ultimately results in \npermanent cell damage (infarction) if the process is not arrested\n• ACS continuum\n• Unstable angina (UA)\n• Non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI)\n• ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI)\n• ECG changes consistent with ischemia\n• T wave flattening, inversion or peaked\n• can also be non-specific\n• ST depression\n• ST elevation ACS comprises unstable angina, NSTEMI and STEMI. The lecturer described plaque rupture and thrombus formation leading to ischemia and, when injury is sufficient, myocardial necrosis. Review ST segments and T waves across the relevant leads. The symptoms, ECG pattern, biomarkers and time course together determine the diagnosis and pathway. Do not think of NSTEMI as automatically a minor event. A delayed presentation after days of symptoms can involve substantial injury. Prompt STEMI recognition and reperfusion may limit damage; both forms need timely attention. The practical message was 'time is muscle': the eventual injury depends partly on how long tissue is deprived of adequate flow.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:25:46-02:29:58 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=138\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-137.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 137 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-137.webp\" alt=\"Slide 137: Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS)\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>ACS comprises unstable angina, NSTEMI and STEMI. The lecturer described plaque rupture and thrombus formation leading to ischemia and, when injury is sufficient, myocardial necrosis.</p><p>Review ST segments and T waves across the relevant leads. The symptoms, ECG pattern, biomarkers and time course together determine the diagnosis and pathway.</p><p>Do not think of NSTEMI as automatically a minor event. A delayed presentation after days of symptoms can involve substantial injury. Prompt STEMI recognition and reperfusion may limit damage; both forms need timely attention.</p><p>The practical message was &#x27;time is muscle&#x27;: the eventual injury depends partly on how long tissue is deprived of adequate flow.</p>"},{"block":138,"title":"Slide 138 · • Unstable angina","text":"Slide 138 • Unstable angina • Unstable angina\n• Change in pattern of angina symptoms, including prolonged or worsening \nangina and new onset of severe angina symptoms\n• non occlusive thrombus\n• non-specific on 12-Lead ECG, normal cardiac enzymes\n• Myocardial infarction: occurs where a portion of heart muscle is \ndeprived of oxygen due to blockage of a coronary artery\n• STEMI: Complete thrombus occlusion, ST elevation on 12-Lead ECG or new \nLBBB, elevated cardiac enzymes, high risk for cardiac arrest\n• NSTEMI: Non-occlusive thrombus sufficient to cause tissue damage and \nmyocardial necrosis, ST depression +/- T wave, inversion on 12-Lead ECG, \nelevated cardiac enzymes Stable angina is predictable with a consistent exertional threshold. Increasing duration, a lower activity threshold, slower relief or symptoms at rest suggest a changing pattern and possible instability. The lecture distinguishes unstable angina, ischemia without infarction, from MI with myocardial injury. STEMI and NSTEMI describe important clinical ECG/biomarker presentations; prolonged occlusion can cause more extensive wall injury. !The slide's 'new LBBB' wording should not be used as an automatic STEMI criterion. New LBBB alone is not a STEMI equivalent; assess for ischemia using the full presentation and appropriate ECG criteria. [5] !Complete versus partial blockage is a simplified teaching model, not an infallible distinction between STEMI and NSTEMI. [5,6]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:29:58-02:32:33 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=139\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-138.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 138 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-138.webp\" alt=\"Slide 138: • Unstable angina\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Stable angina is predictable with a consistent exertional threshold. Increasing duration, a lower activity threshold, slower relief or symptoms at rest suggest a changing pattern and possible instability.</p><p>The lecture distinguishes unstable angina, ischemia without infarction, from MI with myocardial injury. STEMI and NSTEMI describe important clinical ECG/biomarker presentations; prolonged occlusion can cause more extensive wall injury.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The slide&#x27;s &#x27;new LBBB&#x27; wording should not be used as an automatic STEMI criterion. New LBBB alone is not a STEMI equivalent; assess for ischemia using the full presentation and appropriate ECG criteria. [5]</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> Complete versus partial blockage is a simplified teaching model, not an infallible distinction between STEMI and NSTEMI. [5,6]</p>"},{"block":139,"title":"Slide 139 · 12 Lead ECG and Coronary Artery Territories","text":"Slide 139 12 Lead ECG and Coronary Artery Territories 12 Lead ECG and Coronary Artery Territories Use groups of leads to localize the affected myocardial territory. The lecturer highlighted inferior leads II, III and aVF; lateral I and aVL; lateral chest leads V5-V6; and anterior/septal V1-V4. Changes in V1-V4 raise concern for the LAD territory in the appropriate presentation. Inferior changes commonly relate to the RCA, although coronary anatomy varies, as the next diagram indicates. Relate the lead pattern to symptoms and the whole ECG. The territory map supports interpretation; it is not a definitive angiogram of the patient's coronary anatomy.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:32:33-02:33:37 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=140\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-139.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 139 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-139.webp\" alt=\"Slide 139: 12 Lead ECG and Coronary Artery Territories\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Use groups of leads to localize the affected myocardial territory. The lecturer highlighted inferior leads II, III and aVF; lateral I and aVL; lateral chest leads V5-V6; and anterior/septal V1-V4.</p><p>Changes in V1-V4 raise concern for the LAD territory in the appropriate presentation. Inferior changes commonly relate to the RCA, although coronary anatomy varies, as the next diagram indicates.</p><p>Relate the lead pattern to symptoms and the whole ECG. The territory map supports interpretation; it is not a definitive angiogram of the patient&#x27;s coronary anatomy.</p>"},{"block":140,"title":"Slide 140 · v T wave flattening, inversion or peaked","text":"Slide 140 v T wave flattening, inversion or peaked v T wave flattening, inversion or peaked\nv ST depression\nv ST elevation Look for ST elevation, ST depression and T-wave abnormalities within meaningful lead groups. The annotated map connects those groups to likely coronary supply. The lecturer's example was ST elevation across V1-V4 suggesting an LAD-related anterior event. Use the figure to revise contiguous lead groups rather than memorizing an isolated lead change as a diagnosis.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:33:37-02:34:15 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=141\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-140.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 140 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-140.webp\" alt=\"Slide 140: v T wave flattening, inversion or peaked\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>Look for ST elevation, ST depression and T-wave abnormalities within meaningful lead groups. The annotated map connects those groups to likely coronary supply.</p><p>The lecturer&#x27;s example was ST elevation across V1-V4 suggesting an LAD-related anterior event. Use the figure to revise contiguous lead groups rather than memorizing an isolated lead change as a diagnosis.</p>"},{"block":141,"title":"Slide 141 · STEMI – ST elevation myocardial infarction","text":"Slide 141 STEMI – ST elevation myocardial infarction STEMI – ST elevation myocardial infarction\nNSTEMI – non-ST elevation myocardial infarction The illustration contrasts common ST-segment patterns used in the STEMI/NSTEMI discussion. It was not given a separate detailed walkthrough. The lecturer emphasized that the amount of damage is affected by the duration and extent of impaired flow and how quickly treatment occurs. The absence of ST elevation does not imply that myocardial injury is small.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:26:13-02:31:22 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=142\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-141.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 141 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-141.webp\" alt=\"Slide 141: STEMI – ST elevation myocardial infarction\"></a><h3>Related lecture explanation</h3><p>The illustration contrasts common ST-segment patterns used in the STEMI/NSTEMI discussion. It was not given a separate detailed walkthrough.</p><p>The lecturer emphasized that the amount of damage is affected by the duration and extent of impaired flow and how quickly treatment occurs. The absence of ST elevation does not imply that myocardial injury is small.</p>"},{"block":142,"title":"Slide 142 · Q waves in ACS","text":"Slide 142 Q waves in ACS Q waves in ACS\n•Pathologic Q waves are a sign of previous \nmyocardial infarction\n•They are the result of absence of electrical activity\n•A myocardial infarction can be thought of as an \nelectrical 'hole' as scar tissue is electrically dead \nand therefore results in pathologic Q waves The lecturer described pathological Q waves as a possible clue to current or previous myocardial damage, including an infarction the patient may not recall. If the ECG suggests a prior event, revisit the history and cardiovascular risk and consider further cardiac assessment rather than accepting an unfamiliar finding without explanation. !Not every Q wave is abnormal, and even pathological Q waves are not exclusive to infarction. Small septal Q waves can be normal; other conditions can produce infarct-like patterns. Interpret morphology, lead distribution and the clinical context. [6]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:34:15-02:35:17 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=143\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-142.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 142 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-142.webp\" alt=\"Slide 142: Q waves in ACS\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The lecturer described pathological Q waves as a possible clue to current or previous myocardial damage, including an infarction the patient may not recall.</p><p>If the ECG suggests a prior event, revisit the history and cardiovascular risk and consider further cardiac assessment rather than accepting an unfamiliar finding without explanation.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> Not every Q wave is abnormal, and even pathological Q waves are not exclusive to infarction. Small septal Q waves can be normal; other conditions can produce infarct-like patterns. Interpret morphology, lead distribution and the clinical context. [6]</p>"},{"block":143,"title":"Slide 143 · • Q waves are considered pathological if:","text":"Slide 143 • Q waves are considered pathological if: • Q waves are considered pathological if:\n• > 40 ms (1 mm) wide\n• > 2 mm deep\n• > 25% of depth of QRS complex\n• Seen in leads V1-3\n• Pathological Q waves usually indicate current or prior \nmyocardial infarction The slide lists width, depth and distribution features that raise concern for pathological Q waves. The lecturer's practical point was to recognize an unexpected potential marker of myocardial damage and investigate its context. !The listed measurements are a simplified screen. Q-wave interpretation is lead-dependent and cannot be reduced to 'any Q wave means MI.' Use the qualification on slide 142 when reviewing this table. [6]","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:34:47-02:35:58 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=144\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-143.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 143 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-143.webp\" alt=\"Slide 143: • Q waves are considered pathological if:\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The slide lists width, depth and distribution features that raise concern for pathological Q waves. The lecturer&#x27;s practical point was to recognize an unexpected potential marker of myocardial damage and investigate its context.</p><p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The listed measurements are a simplified screen. Q-wave interpretation is lead-dependent and cannot be reduced to &#x27;any Q wave means MI.&#x27; Use the qualification on slide 142 when reviewing this table. [6]</p>"},{"block":144,"title":"Slide 144 · Where is the ischemia?","text":"Slide 144 Where is the ischemia? Where is the ischemia? This ECG was left as a localization exercise rather than answered in detail in the recording. Identify the leads with ST-T abnormalities, group them anatomically and compare with slides 139-140. An instructor-confirmed answer for this specific tracing is not available in the supplied audio.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:35:58 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=145\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-144.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 144 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-144.webp\" alt=\"Slide 144: Where is the ischemia?\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>This ECG was left as a localization exercise rather than answered in detail in the recording. Identify the leads with ST-T abnormalities, group them anatomically and compare with slides 139-140.</p><p>An instructor-confirmed answer for this specific tracing is not available in the supplied audio.</p>"},{"block":145,"title":"Slide 145 · Anterior STEMI","text":"Slide 145 Anterior STEMI Anterior STEMI\nPCI to the LAD The lecturer returned to angiographic images while discussing Mr. Hart. Compare coronary flow before intervention, the wire and stent placement, and flow after the stent is deployed. She pointed out a large territory that was not filling before intervention and became visible afterward. The slide is labelled anterior STEMI with PCI to the LAD; it illustrates the anatomical target of reperfusion, rather than proving that Mr. Hart's own presentation was a STEMI.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:45:24-02:46:24 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=146\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-145.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 145 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-145.webp\" alt=\"Slide 145: Anterior STEMI\"></a><h3>Related lecture explanation</h3><p>The lecturer returned to angiographic images while discussing Mr. Hart. Compare coronary flow before intervention, the wire and stent placement, and flow after the stent is deployed.</p><p>She pointed out a large territory that was not filling before intervention and became visible afterward. The slide is labelled anterior STEMI with PCI to the LAD; it illustrates the anatomical target of reperfusion, rather than proving that Mr. Hart&#x27;s own presentation was a STEMI.</p>"},{"block":146,"title":"Slide 146 · Where is the ischemia?","text":"Slide 146 Where is the ischemia? Where is the ischemia? Independent ECG localization exercise. Assess the whole tracing, identify the main abnormal lead groups and describe the associated territory using the map on slides 139-140. No specific verbal answer was given for this image in the recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:35:58 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=147\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-146.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 146 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-146.webp\" alt=\"Slide 146: Where is the ischemia?\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Independent ECG localization exercise. Assess the whole tracing, identify the main abnormal lead groups and describe the associated territory using the map on slides 139-140.</p><p>No specific verbal answer was given for this image in the recording.</p>"},{"block":147,"title":"Slide 147 · Where is the ischemia?","text":"Slide 147 Where is the ischemia? Where is the ischemia? Use the same approach for this additional ECG: rhythm and rate first, then QRS, ST segments and T waves in contiguous leads. This was supplied for review. The recording does not provide a separate worked answer for this tracing.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:35:58 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=148\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-147.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 147 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-147.webp\" alt=\"Slide 147: Where is the ischemia?\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Use the same approach for this additional ECG: rhythm and rate first, then QRS, ST segments and T waves in contiguous leads.</p><p>This was supplied for review. The recording does not provide a separate worked answer for this tracing.</p>"},{"block":148,"title":"Slide 148 · Where is the ischemia?","text":"Slide 148 Where is the ischemia? Where is the ischemia? Another localization practice tracing. Describe the findings before naming a likely territory, and compare with the lead map rather than relying on one conspicuous waveform. The lecturer moved to Mr. Hart's case without a recorded answer-by-answer analysis of this example.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:35:58 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=149\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-148.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 148 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-148.webp\" alt=\"Slide 148: Where is the ischemia?\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Another localization practice tracing. Describe the findings before naming a likely territory, and compare with the lead map rather than relying on one conspicuous waveform.</p><p>The lecturer moved to Mr. Hart&#x27;s case without a recorded answer-by-answer analysis of this example.</p>"},{"block":149,"title":"Slide 149 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1","text":"Slide 149 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1\n• Mr. Hart (he/him) is a 58-year-old male who presents to his NP-PHC \nwith complaints of new shortness of breath on exertion\n• He exercises regularly walking 45-60 min 3-4 times per week\n• He has a PMHx of hypertension which is well controlled on Micardis \nPlus (80 mg-25 mg); HbA1C in early 2022 was 5.4% and LDL was \n3.6mmol/L\n• On further history taking, he endorses a ‘chest tightness’ that is \nalways present with the shortness of breath and he thought it was \nrelated to his breathing\n• No relevant family history\n• The NP-PHC ordered an ECG Mr. Hart is 58 and reports new exertional dyspnea despite regular walking. Directed questioning reveals accompanying chest tightness that he had attributed to his breathing. His hypertension is treated, and the case provides previous HbA1c and LDL results. No relevant family history is reported. The NP orders an ECG. The teaching point is to unpack dyspnea: the patient may not volunteer exertional chest discomfort unless asked, and a respiratory description may conceal a cardiac symptom pattern.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:35:58-02:36:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=150\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-149.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 149 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-149.webp\" alt=\"Slide 149: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and case discussion</h3><p>Mr. Hart is 58 and reports new exertional dyspnea despite regular walking. Directed questioning reveals accompanying chest tightness that he had attributed to his breathing.</p><p>His hypertension is treated, and the case provides previous HbA1c and LDL results. No relevant family history is reported. The NP orders an ECG.</p><p>The teaching point is to unpack dyspnea: the patient may not volunteer exertional chest discomfort unless asked, and a respiratory description may conceal a cardiac symptom pattern.</p>"},{"block":150,"title":"Slide 150 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1","text":"Slide 150 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1 The ECG is reported as sinus rhythm with occasional PVCs. The lecturer checked the lower rhythm strip and agreed with that description, then reviewed the remaining leads. Do not stop after reading the machine label. Confirm the rhythm yourself and assess whether the tracing explains the symptoms. The subsequent case demonstrates that a relatively unremarkable resting ECG does not settle the cause of progressive exertional limitation.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:36:56-02:37:33 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=151\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-150.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 150 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-150.webp\" alt=\"Slide 150: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The ECG is reported as sinus rhythm with occasional PVCs. The lecturer checked the lower rhythm strip and agreed with that description, then reviewed the remaining leads.</p><p>Do not stop after reading the machine label. Confirm the rhythm yourself and assess whether the tracing explains the symptoms.</p><p>The subsequent case demonstrates that a relatively unremarkable resting ECG does not settle the cause of progressive exertional limitation.</p>"},{"block":151,"title":"Slide 151 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1","text":"Slide 151 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1\n• Mr. Hart returns for a follow-up appointment after his ECG\n• He has cut back on his walking; he has slowed down his pace, now \nwalking only 30 min; his shortness of breath is mild when walking; he \ncan still perform his ADLs with no symptoms\n• Based on the ECG, what next steps would the NP-PHC take?\n• Echocardiogram\n• PFTs\n• CXR At follow-up, Mr. Hart has slowed his pace and reduced walks from 45-60 minutes to about 30 minutes. He remains comfortable with ordinary activities at home. The change from his own baseline is clinically important. Students suggested CBC and electrolytes; the lecturer also discussed BNP or NT-proBNP for dyspnea, CXR and echo. PFTs were not considered unreasonable if respiratory disease remained in the differential. Select tests according to what the history and examination suggest. A test that identifies a different cause may help, but progressive exertional dyspnea with tightness still requires cardiac consideration.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:37:33-02:39:59 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=152\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-151.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 151 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-151.webp\" alt=\"Slide 151: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1\"></a><h3>Lecture notes and discussion</h3><p>At follow-up, Mr. Hart has slowed his pace and reduced walks from 45-60 minutes to about 30 minutes. He remains comfortable with ordinary activities at home. The change from his own baseline is clinically important.</p><p>Students suggested CBC and electrolytes; the lecturer also discussed BNP or NT-proBNP for dyspnea, CXR and echo. PFTs were not considered unreasonable if respiratory disease remained in the differential.</p><p>Select tests according to what the history and examination suggest. A test that identifies a different cause may help, but progressive exertional dyspnea with tightness still requires cardiac consideration.</p>"},{"block":152,"title":"Slide 152 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1","text":"Slide 152 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1\nEcho Report The echo summary reports normal LV size but reduced LVEF of 35-40%, with akinesis involving the anteroseptum, inferoseptum, inferior wall and apex. No LV thrombus is reported; RV size and systolic function are normal. The lecturer emphasized that chamber size and pumping function are different findings: normal size does not mean normal LV function. Read beyond the first sentence. Connect the regional movement abnormalities and reduced EF with the patient's exertional symptoms, then review the valves and the rest of the report.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:39:59-02:41:29 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=153\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-152.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 152 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-152.webp\" alt=\"Slide 152: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 1\"></a><h3>Lecture notes</h3><p>The echo summary reports normal LV size but reduced LVEF of 35-40%, with akinesis involving the anteroseptum, inferoseptum, inferior wall and apex. No LV thrombus is reported; RV size and systolic function are normal.</p><p>The lecturer emphasized that chamber size and pumping function are different findings: normal size does not mean normal LV function.</p><p>Read beyond the first sentence. Connect the regional movement abnormalities and reduced EF with the patient&#x27;s exertional symptoms, then review the valves and the rest of the report.</p>"},{"block":153,"title":"Slide 153 · Julie’s Cardiology Case ","text":"Slide 153 Julie’s Cardiology Case  Julie’s Cardiology Case \nStudy 1\nLet’s review the details\nof this report\n•\nWhat findings are most concerning to the NP-\nPHC on this report?\n•\nWhat is the most likely cause of this reduced \nLV function? Why?\n•\nWhat would be the appropriate next steps for \nthe NP-PHC in this case?\n•\nWhat investigation could the NP-PHC expect \nwould be next for this patient?\n•\nAny guesses what that investigation would \ndemonstrate?\n•\nHint… what walls are not moving well?\n•\nWhat supplies those walls? The most concerning findings are reduced EF and regional wall-motion abnormalities. Akinesis means absent motion; hypokinesis means reduced motion. The lecturer linked the regional pattern to possible impaired coronary blood supply and suspected ischemic heart disease. These findings can explain dyspnea, reduced exercise tolerance and exertional discomfort even without a dramatic acute presentation. They require timely cardiology assessment rather than attributing symptoms to occasional PVCs. The expected next specialist investigation discussed was coronary angiography to define anatomy and obstruction. Review lipids, diabetes status and other vascular risks so that primary care can address its part of risk reduction. The case supports suspected coronary disease; echo alone does not establish the precise coronary lesion.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:41:29-02:45:24 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=154\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-153.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 153 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-153.webp\" alt=\"Slide 153: Julie’s Cardiology Case \"></a><h3>Lecture notes and case reasoning</h3><p>The most concerning findings are reduced EF and regional wall-motion abnormalities. Akinesis means absent motion; hypokinesis means reduced motion. The lecturer linked the regional pattern to possible impaired coronary blood supply and suspected ischemic heart disease.</p><p>These findings can explain dyspnea, reduced exercise tolerance and exertional discomfort even without a dramatic acute presentation. They require timely cardiology assessment rather than attributing symptoms to occasional PVCs.</p><p>The expected next specialist investigation discussed was coronary angiography to define anatomy and obstruction. Review lipids, diabetes status and other vascular risks so that primary care can address its part of risk reduction.</p><p>The case supports suspected coronary disease; echo alone does not establish the precise coronary lesion.</p>"},{"block":154,"title":"Slide 154 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2","text":"Slide 154 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2\n• Mr. Aorta (he/him) is a 51-year-old male who had an inferior STEMI \nwhile visiting his daughter out of province\n• He presented to hospital within 60 minutes of the onset of his chest \npain and underwent successful rescue PCI to the RCA\n• His hospitalization was uneventful, and he was started on goal \ndirectedmedical therapy as per guidelines post STEMI\n• The NP-PHC received a discharge summary with documentation of \nthe event, an echocardiogram report showing an EF of 45% with \ninferior wallmotion abnormalities and a post STEMI ECG that was \nNSR with no ST or T wave changes\n• The discharge summary requests primary care follow-up with a \nrepeat echocardiogram to reassess LV function This case was left for independent review. Mr. Aorta had an inferior STEMI treated with RCA PCI; the discharge echo showed EF 45% and inferior regional wall-motion abnormalities. Follow-up reassessment of LV function was requested. Use the case to distinguish the acute discharge findings from later recovery and to identify the follow-up responsibilities communicated to primary care.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:24-02:46:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=155\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-154.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 154 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-154.webp\" alt=\"Slide 154: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>This case was left for independent review. Mr. Aorta had an inferior STEMI treated with RCA PCI; the discharge echo showed EF 45% and inferior regional wall-motion abnormalities. Follow-up reassessment of LV function was requested.</p><p>Use the case to distinguish the acute discharge findings from later recovery and to identify the follow-up responsibilities communicated to primary care.</p>"},{"block":155,"title":"Slide 155 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2","text":"Slide 155 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2\n• What time frame post STEMI would be appropriate to reassess the LV \nfunction?\n• Why? Self-study question: when should LV function be reassessed after STEMI, and why? The supplied recording does not contain the lecturer's specific timing recommendation or a worked answer. Retain this as a question to resolve against the course material and the patient's discharge or cardiology plan rather than inserting an unspoken interval into the lecture notes.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:24-02:46:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=156\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-155.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 155 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-155.webp\" alt=\"Slide 155: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Self-study question: when should LV function be reassessed after STEMI, and why? The supplied recording does not contain the lecturer&#x27;s specific timing recommendation or a worked answer.</p><p>Retain this as a question to resolve against the course material and the patient&#x27;s discharge or cardiology plan rather than inserting an unspoken interval into the lecture notes.</p>"},{"block":156,"title":"Slide 156 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2","text":"Slide 156 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2\n• How does the NP-PHC interpret \nthis echocardiogram report?\n• What other referral would be \nappropriate at this time?\n• What other diagnostic testing will \nbe appropriate for Mr. Aorta? Now \nor within the next 12 months? The displayed follow-up report describes normal LV size and wall thickness, normal systolic function, EF 65-70% and no regional wall-motion abnormality. RV function is normal, and no major valve abnormality is described. Compare these findings with the initial EF 45% and inferior movement abnormality in slide 154: the supplied example shows recovery of function. The lecturer did not give recorded answers to the additional referral and future-testing questions on this slide.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:24-02:46:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-156.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 156 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-156.webp\" alt=\"Slide 156: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 2\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The displayed follow-up report describes normal LV size and wall thickness, normal systolic function, EF 65-70% and no regional wall-motion abnormality. RV function is normal, and no major valve abnormality is described.</p><p>Compare these findings with the initial EF 45% and inferior movement abnormality in slide 154: the supplied example shows recovery of function.</p><p>The lecturer did not give recorded answers to the additional referral and future-testing questions on this slide.</p>"},{"block":157,"title":"Slide 157 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3","text":"Slide 157 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3\n• Mrs. Ventricle (she/her) is a 91-year-old woman who presents to the \nNP-PHC with complaints of exertional chest discomfort and shortness \nof breath\n• PMHx: HTN well controlled on Perindopril and Amlodipine, \nosteoarthritis, Type II DM on Metformin and Sitagliptin (last HbA1C \n7.3%), CKD Stage II\n• The physical exam is unremarkable except for a new murmur not \npreviously heard on auscultation\n• How are murmurs described or classified? Mrs. Ventricle is 91 with exertional chest discomfort, dyspnea and a newly recognized murmur. The case includes hypertension, diabetes and CKD among her comorbidities. This case was left for independent review. Describe the murmur carefully and relate it to the symptoms before reviewing the echo findings on subsequent slides.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=158\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-157.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 157 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-157.webp\" alt=\"Slide 157: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Mrs. Ventricle is 91 with exertional chest discomfort, dyspnea and a newly recognized murmur. The case includes hypertension, diabetes and CKD among her comorbidities.</p><p>This case was left for independent review. Describe the murmur carefully and relate it to the symptoms before reviewing the echo findings on subsequent slides.</p>"},{"block":158,"title":"Slide 158 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3","text":"Slide 158 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3\n• character of a murmur is described by intensity (grade), pitch (frequency), \nconfiguration, timing, quality, location, and radiation\n• intensity - determined by the quantity and velocity of blood flow at the site of its \norigin, the transmission characteristic of the tissues between the blood flow and \nstethoscope, the site of auscultation or recording, and the distance of \ntransmission\n• pitch - frequency of the murmur determines the pitch, which may be high or \nlow; can be described as harsh, rumbling, scratchy, grunting, blowing, squeaky, \nvibratory, and musical\n• location and radiation - location on the patient's chest where the murmur is \nloudest is typically described as apical, and parasternal or over the base of the \nheart\n• parasternal murmurs are further characterized by the intercostal space and right \nor left side of the sternum\n• assess the area over which the murmur is audible (radiation)\n• classification by timing - duration of a murmur is assessed by determining the \nlength of systole or diastole that the murmur occupies; murmur can be long (i.e. \nit occupies most of systole or diastole), or it can be short Use a consistent murmur description: timing, site of maximal intensity, radiation, grade, pitch, quality and configuration. Distinguish how loud the murmur is from its other characteristics. A clear description allows another clinician to understand what was heard and why a particular valve lesion is being considered. The lecturer did not separately teach each descriptor during this recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=159\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-158.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 158 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-158.webp\" alt=\"Slide 158: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Use a consistent murmur description: timing, site of maximal intensity, radiation, grade, pitch, quality and configuration.</p><p>Distinguish how loud the murmur is from its other characteristics. A clear description allows another clinician to understand what was heard and why a particular valve lesion is being considered.</p><p>The lecturer did not separately teach each descriptor during this recording.</p>"},{"block":159,"title":"Slide 159 · Figure or report","text":"Slide 159 Figure or report  The table reviews grades 1-6. Grades 1-3 describe increasing audibility without a thrill; a palpable thrill is part of the higher-grade descriptions. Use the original table for the precise grade definitions. This grading exercise was supplied for review, without an additional spoken walkthrough.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=160\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-159.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 159 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-159.webp\" alt=\"Slide 159: Figure or report\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The table reviews grades 1-6. Grades 1-3 describe increasing audibility without a thrill; a palpable thrill is part of the higher-grade descriptions.</p><p>Use the original table for the precise grade definitions. This grading exercise was supplied for review, without an additional spoken walkthrough.</p>"},{"block":160,"title":"Slide 160 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3","text":"Slide 160 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3\n• Mrs. Ventricle’s murmur is characterized as the following:\n• Systolic ejection murmur, crescendo decrescendo\n• Grade 3\n• Radiates across the precordium and to the carotid arteries\n• What differential diagnosis would the NP-PHC make at this point?\n• Aortic stenosis, aortic sclerosis, heart murmur NYD\n• What would be appropriate next step for Mrs. Ventricle?\n• Echocardiogram\n• Referral to Cardiology or depending on severity Cardiac Surgery The murmur is described as grade 3, systolic ejection, crescendo-decrescendo, radiating across the precordium and to the carotids. The slide's differential includes aortic stenosis and aortic sclerosis, with echo as the next investigation and referral based on severity. This is the course's supplied case progression, not an independently discussed live answer.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=161\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-160.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 160 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-160.webp\" alt=\"Slide 160: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The murmur is described as grade 3, systolic ejection, crescendo-decrescendo, radiating across the precordium and to the carotids.</p><p>The slide&#x27;s differential includes aortic stenosis and aortic sclerosis, with echo as the next investigation and referral based on severity. This is the course&#x27;s supplied case progression, not an independently discussed live answer.</p>"},{"block":161,"title":"Slide 161 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3","text":"Slide 161 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3 The echo summary identifies severe aortic stenosis, mean systolic gradient 47 mmHg and valve area 0.4 cm2. LV size is normal, with low-normal function and concentric hypertrophy; mild aortic regurgitation and mild-to-moderate mitral regurgitation are also described. Use these actual report findings to connect the murmur and exertional symptoms with a structural diagnosis. The original report is reproduced above for review of image limitations and other details.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=162\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-161.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 161 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-161.webp\" alt=\"Slide 161: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The echo summary identifies severe aortic stenosis, mean systolic gradient 47 mmHg and valve area 0.4 cm2. LV size is normal, with low-normal function and concentric hypertrophy; mild aortic regurgitation and mild-to-moderate mitral regurgitation are also described.</p><p>Use these actual report findings to connect the murmur and exertional symptoms with a structural diagnosis. The original report is reproduced above for review of image limitations and other details.</p>"},{"block":162,"title":"Slide 162 · Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3","text":"Slide 162 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3 Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3\n• What symptoms are classic of aortic \nstenosis?\n• What is the natural course of severe aortic \nstenosis?\n• Prevalence of AS increases with age; \nprospective population-based studies \nprevalence of AS varied from 0.2% 50-59 \nyears, 1.3% at ages 60-69, 3.9% 70-79 years, \nand 9.8% 80 to 89 years The slide asks about classic aortic-stenosis symptoms and the natural history of severe disease and gives age-related prevalence figures. These were self-study questions in the supplied recording. Review them with the symptomatic case and the severe echo findings rather than assuming the lecturer provided a recorded answer.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=163\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-162.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 162 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-162.webp\" alt=\"Slide 162: Julie’s Cardiology Case Study 3\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The slide asks about classic aortic-stenosis symptoms and the natural history of severe disease and gives age-related prevalence figures.</p><p>These were self-study questions in the supplied recording. Review them with the symptomatic case and the severe echo findings rather than assuming the lecturer provided a recorded answer.</p>"},{"block":163,"title":"Slide 163 · Atria","text":"Slide 163 Atria Atria This is the detailed report accompanying the aortic-stenosis case. It describes a heavily calcified valve, severe stenosis, an estimated EF around 50-55%, concentric LV hypertrophy and limited image quality. Read the full report alongside the summary on slide 161, including the ventricular findings, valves and limitations. No separate oral interpretation was recorded.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=164\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-163.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 163 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-163.webp\" alt=\"Slide 163: Atria\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>This is the detailed report accompanying the aortic-stenosis case. It describes a heavily calcified valve, severe stenosis, an estimated EF around 50-55%, concentric LV hypertrophy and limited image quality.</p><p>Read the full report alongside the summary on slide 161, including the ventricular findings, valves and limitations. No separate oral interpretation was recorded.</p>"},{"block":164,"title":"Slide 164 · Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology","text":"Slide 164 Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\nTransthoracic Echocardiography (TTE)\n• ultrasound beams are directed across the chest wall to obtain images of the \nheart\n• evaluation of chamber size, LVEF, wall motion abnormalities, myocardial \nischemia and complications of MI, evaluation of wall thickness, valve \nmorphology, proximal great vessel morphology, pericardial effusion\n• evaluation of unexplained hypotension, murmurs, syncope, and congenital heart \ndisease or other cardiac symptoms and unexplained signs on physical exam (i.e. \nleg edema)\n• bubble study improves visualization and provides real-time assessment of \nintracardiac blood flow using conventional agent is agitated saline\n• visualization of right heart and intracardiac shunts, most commonly patent \nforamen ovale (PFO) and intrapulmonary shunt\n• contrast (no renal effects) study helps visualize blood flow and apex of the heart The remaining investigation slides were provided as a reference: what a test shows, when it is useful, and how it helps answer a diagnostic question. TTE assesses chamber size, ventricular function, regional wall motion, valves and pericardial findings. The slide distinguishes agitated-saline studies for shunt assessment from image-enhancing contrast used to improve endocardial visualization. The lecturer did not separately expand every listed indication in this closing section.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=165\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-164.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 164 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-164.webp\" alt=\"Slide 164: Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The remaining investigation slides were provided as a reference: what a test shows, when it is useful, and how it helps answer a diagnostic question.</p><p>TTE assesses chamber size, ventricular function, regional wall motion, valves and pericardial findings. The slide distinguishes agitated-saline studies for shunt assessment from image-enhancing contrast used to improve endocardial visualization.</p><p>The lecturer did not separately expand every listed indication in this closing section.</p>"},{"block":165,"title":"Slide 165 · Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology","text":"Slide 165 Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\nTransthoracic Echocardiography (TTE) The image illustrates a transthoracic echo view. Use it with the previous slide to distinguish an imaging study of cardiac structure and movement from an ECG recording of electrical activity. No individual interpretation of this echo image was given in the audio.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=166\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-165.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 165 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-165.webp\" alt=\"Slide 165: Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The image illustrates a transthoracic echo view. Use it with the previous slide to distinguish an imaging study of cardiac structure and movement from an ECG recording of electrical activity.</p><p>No individual interpretation of this echo image was given in the audio.</p>"},{"block":166,"title":"Slide 166 · Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology","text":"Slide 166 Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\nTransesophageal Echocardiography (TEE)\n• ultrasound probe inserted into the esophagus to allow for better resolution \nof the heart and structures\n• better visualization of posterior structures, including left atrium, mitral, and \naortic valves, interatrial septum\n• indications\n• TTE is used to evaluate valvular morphology and function (particularly the aortic \nand mitral valves)\n• intracardiac thrombi, tumors, valvular vegetations (infective endocarditis), aortic \ndissection, aortic atheromas, prosthetic valve function, shunt, technically \ninadequate transthoracic study\n• valuation for left atrial thrombus/left atrial appendage thrombus in a patient with \natrial fibrillation/atrial flutter to facilitate clinical decision making regarding \nelectrical cardioversion or ablation\n• risks\n• serious complications are extremely rare (<1 in 5000)\n• esophageal perforation\n• gastrointestinal bleeding\n• pharyngeal hematoma TEE places an ultrasound probe in the esophagus to obtain closer views of selected structures. The slide lists valve assessment, intracardiac thrombus, endocarditis and other structural questions among its uses. An AF-related use is assessment for left atrial or appendage thrombus when planning cardioversion. This is a different test from a routine chest-wall TTE and has procedural risks. The bullet beginning 'TTE is used' appears within the TEE section; read the test name in context rather than treating it as a separate lecture instruction.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=167\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-166.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 166 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-166.webp\" alt=\"Slide 166: Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>TEE places an ultrasound probe in the esophagus to obtain closer views of selected structures. The slide lists valve assessment, intracardiac thrombus, endocarditis and other structural questions among its uses.</p><p>An AF-related use is assessment for left atrial or appendage thrombus when planning cardioversion. This is a different test from a routine chest-wall TTE and has procedural risks.</p><p>The bullet beginning &#x27;TTE is used&#x27; appears within the TEE section; read the test name in context rather than treating it as a separate lecture instruction.</p>"},{"block":167,"title":"Slide 167 · Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology","text":"Slide 167 Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\nExercise Stress Testing\n• treadmill or bicycle exercise with electrocardiographic and blood \npressure monitoring\n• indications\n• patients with intermediate (10-90%) pretest probability of CAD based on age, \ngender, and symptoms\n• ST depression <1 mm at rest, no left bundle branch block, no digoxin \n• exercise test results stratify patients into 3 risk groups\n• 1. low risk patients can be treated medically without invasive testing\n• 2. intermediate risk patients may need additional testing in the form of \nexercise imaging studies or cardiac catheterization\n• 3. high risk patients should be referred for cardiac catheterization Exercise testing combines exertion with ECG and blood-pressure monitoring. The slide describes using the result to inform risk assessment and whether additional imaging or invasive testing is needed. Suitability depends on the clinical question, the patient's ability to exercise and whether the baseline ECG permits useful interpretation. No detailed live selection algorithm was taught in the closing comments.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=168\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-167.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 167 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-167.webp\" alt=\"Slide 167: Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Exercise testing combines exertion with ECG and blood-pressure monitoring. The slide describes using the result to inform risk assessment and whether additional imaging or invasive testing is needed.</p><p>Suitability depends on the clinical question, the patient&#x27;s ability to exercise and whether the baseline ECG permits useful interpretation. No detailed live selection algorithm was taught in the closing comments.</p>"},{"block":168,"title":"Slide 168 · Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology","text":"Slide 168 Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\nNuclear Stress Testing - MIBI\n• myocardial perfusion imaging (MPI) with ECG-gated single photon \nemission computed tomography (SPECT), using radiolabelled tracer\n• evaluates myocardial viability, detects ischemia, and assesses \nperfusion and LV function simultaneously\n• predicts the likelihood of further cardiac event rates independent of \nthe patient’s history, examination, resting ECG, and stress ECG\n• often denoted as MIBI scan with reference to radiolabelled tracer \n(sestamibi)\n• stress with either treadmill or IV vasodilator stress (dipyridamole, \nadenosine)\n• images of the heart obtained during stress and at rest 3-4 h later Nuclear myocardial perfusion imaging compares tracer distribution under stress and at rest to evaluate perfusion. The deck describes exercise or pharmacological stress with ECG-gated SPECT and the tracer sestamibi, often called a MIBI scan. Use this as a reference for the kind of information the test provides; the lecturer did not walk through the acquisition process or individual results in the recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=169\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-168.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 168 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-168.webp\" alt=\"Slide 168: Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Nuclear myocardial perfusion imaging compares tracer distribution under stress and at rest to evaluate perfusion. The deck describes exercise or pharmacological stress with ECG-gated SPECT and the tracer sestamibi, often called a MIBI scan.</p><p>Use this as a reference for the kind of information the test provides; the lecturer did not walk through the acquisition process or individual results in the recording.</p>"},{"block":169,"title":"Slide 169 · Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology","text":"Slide 169 Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\nNuclear Stress Testing - MIBI\n• to diagnose CAD in possible ACS patients with non-diagnostic ECG \nand negative serum biomarker\n• exercise\n• when ECG cannot be interpreted appropriately due to LBBB or abnormal \nbaseline ECG\n• intermediate pre-test probability with normal/equivocal exercise ECG\n• in patients with previous imaging whose symptoms have changed\n• to diagnose ischemia\n• dipyridamole/adenosine MPI\n• exercise testing is always preferred\n• pharmacological stress imaging test for patients who cannot exercise or do \nnot want to hold cardiac meds The slide lists circumstances in which stress imaging may add information beyond a standard exercise ECG, including baseline ECG limitations, changed symptoms or inability to exercise adequately. Match the modality to the diagnostic question and patient factors. The lecturer's local ordering-pathway comment is recorded under slide 136; it should not be generalized across jurisdictions or dates.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=170\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-169.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 169 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-169.webp\" alt=\"Slide 169: Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The slide lists circumstances in which stress imaging may add information beyond a standard exercise ECG, including baseline ECG limitations, changed symptoms or inability to exercise adequately.</p><p>Match the modality to the diagnostic question and patient factors. The lecturer&#x27;s local ordering-pathway comment is recorded under slide 136; it should not be generalized across jurisdictions or dates.</p>"},{"block":170,"title":"Slide 170 · Figure or report","text":"Slide 170 Figure or report  This image places stress and rest perfusion views side by side in several cardiac planes. Use it to understand the comparison underlying the test. No case-specific interpretation or verbal answer for this image was provided in the supplied audio.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=171\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-170.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 170 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-170.webp\" alt=\"Slide 170: Figure or report\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>This image places stress and rest perfusion views side by side in several cardiac planes. Use it to understand the comparison underlying the test.</p><p>No case-specific interpretation or verbal answer for this image was provided in the supplied audio.</p>"},{"block":171,"title":"Slide 171 · Nuclear Stress Testing ","text":"Slide 171 Nuclear Stress Testing  Nuclear Stress Testing \n– MIBI\nRed Flags\n• Ischemia involving > 10% \nof the LV mass\n• Resting ischemia\n• Always be concerned \n‘balanced ischemia’… \nwhere all 3 main vessel are \ndiseased so no obvious \nischemia appears\n• If history and symptoms fit \nthe bill... then move on to \nnext level of investigation The slide highlights substantial ischemic burden and warns about balanced ischemia: relative perfusion imaging can appear less striking when multiple territories are similarly affected. Its main teaching point is to revisit the clinical story when symptoms and risk remain concerning despite a reassuring-looking investigation. This was supplied as reference content, without further live explanation.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=172\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-171.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 171 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-171.webp\" alt=\"Slide 171: Nuclear Stress Testing \"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The slide highlights substantial ischemic burden and warns about balanced ischemia: relative perfusion imaging can appear less striking when multiple territories are similarly affected.</p><p>Its main teaching point is to revisit the clinical story when symptoms and risk remain concerning despite a reassuring-looking investigation. This was supplied as reference content, without further live explanation.</p>"},{"block":172,"title":"Slide 172 · Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology","text":"Slide 172 Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\nLeft Heart Catheterization- Coronary Angiography\n• accomplished by introducing a catheter into the brachial or femoral artery and \nadvancing it through the aorta, across the aortic valve, and into the left ventricle\n• evaluates mitral and aortic valvular defects and myocardial disease\n• systolic and end-diastolic pressure tracings recorded\n• LV size, wall motion and ejection fraction can be assessed by injecting contrast \ninto the LV (left ventriculography) via femoral/radial artery catheterization\n• cardiac output and index (measured by the Fick oxygen method or the indicator \ndilution method)\n• indications\n• identification of the extent and severity of CAD and evaluation of left ventricular function\n• assessment of the severity of valvular or myocardial disorders (e.g. aortic stenosis or \ninsufficiency, mitral stenosis or insufficiency, and various cardiomyopathies) to determine \nthe need for surgical correction\n• collection of data to confirm and complement noninvasive studies\n• investigating CAD in patients with confusing clinical features or chest pain of uncertain origin The deck introduces invasive catheter-based assessment of coronary anatomy, LV function and hemodynamics. Define which question the proposed procedure is intended to answer. Read this overview alongside the next slide's more specific discussion of coronary angiography. The lecturer's live example focused on coronary flow and stenting rather than teaching every listed measurement or access route.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=173\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-172.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 172 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-172.webp\" alt=\"Slide 172: Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The deck introduces invasive catheter-based assessment of coronary anatomy, LV function and hemodynamics. Define which question the proposed procedure is intended to answer.</p><p>Read this overview alongside the next slide&#x27;s more specific discussion of coronary angiography. The lecturer&#x27;s live example focused on coronary flow and stenting rather than teaching every listed measurement or access route.</p>"},{"block":173,"title":"Slide 173 · Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology","text":"Slide 173 Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\nLeft Heart Catheterization- Coronary Angiography\n• radiographic visualization of the coronary vessels after injection of \nradiopaque contrast media\n• coronary vasculature accessed via the coronary ostia\n• indications\n• to define the coronary anatomy and the degree of luminal obstruction of the \ncoronary arteries\n• to determine the presence and extent of obstructive CAD\n• to assess the feasibility and appropriateness of various forms of therapy, such as \nrevascularization by percutaneous or surgical interventions (PCI)\n• can be used when the diagnosis of CAD is uncertain and cannot be excluded by \nnon-invasive techniques\n• contraindications\n• severe renal failure due to contrast agent toxicity (must check patient's renal \nstatus due to contrast agent toxicity)\n• risks\n• major complications for diagnostic catheterization (death, MI, stroke) <3 in 1000\n• minor complications e.g. vascular assess issue, kidney damage <1/100 Coronary angiography visualizes contrast within coronary vessels to define anatomy and obstruction and help plan revascularization when appropriate. The slide emphasizes contrast-related renal considerations and procedural risks. Integrate renal status, clinical urgency and the specialist plan rather than using a single listed contraindication without context. The live angiographic explanation is captured under slide 145.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-173.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 173 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-173.webp\" alt=\"Slide 173: Commonly Ordered Tests in Cardiology\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>Coronary angiography visualizes contrast within coronary vessels to define anatomy and obstruction and help plan revascularization when appropriate.</p><p>The slide emphasizes contrast-related renal considerations and procedural risks. Integrate renal status, clinical urgency and the specialist plan rather than using a single listed contraindication without context.</p><p>The live angiographic explanation is captured under slide 145.</p>"},{"block":174,"title":"Slide 174 · Cardiac Investigations… Clinical Pearls","text":"Slide 174 Cardiac Investigations… Clinical Pearls Cardiac Investigations… Clinical Pearls\n•Remember…\n•They are not the “silver bullets” of diagnostic \ncardiology\n•It gives you a “picture” at a moment in time \n•It’s “all in the history”\n• Detailed history and physical can give you many of the \nanswers you need\n• Think about what question you are asking before \nordering a specific diagnostic test The closing reference reinforces a theme repeated throughout the lecture: decide what clinical question a test is meant to answer, and interpret it together with the history and examination. A test samples particular information at a particular time. A normal brief recording or otherwise reassuring result may not explain intermittent or progressive symptoms.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">02:46:56-02:47:28 · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=175\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-174.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 174 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-174.webp\" alt=\"Slide 174: Cardiac Investigations… Clinical Pearls\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The closing reference reinforces a theme repeated throughout the lecture: decide what clinical question a test is meant to answer, and interpret it together with the history and examination.</p><p>A test samples particular information at a particular time. A normal brief recording or otherwise reassuring result may not explain intermittent or progressive symptoms.</p>"},{"block":175,"title":"Slide 175 · Figure or report","text":"Slide 175 Figure or report  This slide shows clinical reference apps rather than additional case teaching. No separate spoken recommendations about the individual apps were captured. The substantive AF and syncope references discussed in the lecture appear in the relevant sections above.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">No matching recorded segment · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=176\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-175.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 175 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-175.webp\" alt=\"Slide 175: Figure or report\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>This slide shows clinical reference apps rather than additional case teaching. No separate spoken recommendations about the individual apps were captured.</p><p>The substantive AF and syncope references discussed in the lecture appear in the relevant sections above.</p>"},{"block":176,"title":"Slide 176 · Julie's WTW (What The Wow!!!)","text":"Slide 176 Julie's WTW (What The Wow!!!) Julie's WTW (What The Wow!!!)\nCase Study\nDiagnosis??? An additional ECG challenge is supplied at the end of the deck. It was not discussed in the recording. The following slides identify the case as an anterior STEMI treated with LAD PCI. Compare the before-and-after tracings as a self-study exercise; do not interpret this as a separate spoken case presentation.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">No matching recorded segment · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=177\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-176.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 176 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-176.webp\" alt=\"Slide 176: Julie&#x27;s WTW (What The Wow!!!)\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>An additional ECG challenge is supplied at the end of the deck. It was not discussed in the recording.</p><p>The following slides identify the case as an anterior STEMI treated with LAD PCI. Compare the before-and-after tracings as a self-study exercise; do not interpret this as a separate spoken case presentation.</p>"},{"block":177,"title":"Slide 177 · Code STEMI","text":"Slide 177 Code STEMI Code STEMI\nPost Percutaneous Coronary Intervention The deck labels this tracing as post-PCI following a code STEMI. Compare it with the preceding tracing to examine the evolution of the ECG after intervention. No detailed verbal interpretation or timing of this follow-up tracing is available in the recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">No matching recorded segment · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=178\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-177.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 177 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-177.webp\" alt=\"Slide 177: Code STEMI\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The deck labels this tracing as post-PCI following a code STEMI. Compare it with the preceding tracing to examine the evolution of the ECG after intervention.</p><p>No detailed verbal interpretation or timing of this follow-up tracing is available in the recording.</p>"},{"block":178,"title":"Slide 178 · Anterior STEMI – large infarct","text":"Slide 178 Anterior STEMI – large infarct Anterior STEMI – large infarct\nPCI to LAD\n• Heart function post MI?\n• Guesses? The final supplied case summary identifies a large anterior infarct treated with LAD PCI. The echo shows severely reduced LV systolic function, EF 20-25%, with regional abnormalities including apical, anterior and anteroseptal akinesis. This answers the slide's question about post-MI function using the printed report itself. The lecturer did not separately discuss the case in the supplied recording.","html":"<p class=\"tiny\">No matching recorded segment · <a href=\"downloads/Cardiology-II-Oct22-2026-Slides-and-Notes.pdf#page=179\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open this slide in the PDF</a></p><a href=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-178.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open slide 178 at full size\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1426\" height=\"1102\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/cardiology-oct22/slide-178.webp\" alt=\"Slide 178: Anterior STEMI – large infarct\"></a><h3>Slide reference</h3><p>The final supplied case summary identifies a large anterior infarct treated with LAD PCI. The echo shows severely reduced LV systolic function, EF 20-25%, with regional abnormalities including apical, anterior and anteroseptal akinesis.</p><p>This answers the slide&#x27;s question about post-MI function using the printed report itself. The lecturer did not separately discuss the case in the supplied recording.</p>"},{"block":179,"title":"References for clarifications","text":"References for editorial clarifications ACC AHA HRS Merck Canadian Cardiovascular Society CCS ESC atrial fibrillation myocardial infarction","html":"<p>These references support the separately labelled clarifications. Lecture notes and slide-reference summaries remain distinguished above.</p><ul><li>[1] <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000000628\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ACC AHA HRS 2018 Bradycardia and Conduction Delay Guideline</a></li><li>[2] <a href=\"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/cardiovascular-tests-and-procedures/electrocardiography\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Merck Manual Professional Electrocardiography</a></li><li>[3] <a href=\"https://ccs.ca/guideline/2020-atrial-fibrillation/chapter-8-stroke-prevention/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Cardiovascular Society 2020 AF Guideline Chapter 8 Stroke Prevention</a></li><li>[4] <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/eurheartj/article/45/36/3314/7738779\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ESC 2024 Atrial Fibrillation Guideline</a></li><li>[5] <a href=\"https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacc.2022.08.750\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ACC 2022 Expert Consensus Decision Pathway on Acute Chest Pain</a></li><li>[6] <a href=\"https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacc.2018.08.1038\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fourth Universal Definition of Myocardial Infarction 2018</a></li><li>[7] <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/health/practitioner-professional-resources/bc-guidelines/atrial-fibrillation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">British Columbia Atrial Fibrillation Diagnosis and Management</a></li></ul>"}]});
